window.NOTES = [{"slug": "README", "label": "About these notes & syllabus", "tag": "", "group": "start", "title": "International Logistics — End-Sem Study Notes", "md": "# International Logistics — End-Sem Study Notes\nIn-depth notes for the **International Logistics** end-semester exam (MBA Operations Management, Semester III), built from the course material zip: the faculty PPTs (Incoterms, Containerization and container imbalance, Maritime and logistics costing, Air Transportation), the *Air security and cost* PDF, the *KPIs book cases* and pre-read tables, and the three problem files (*IL problems for practice final part 1*, *Insurance and contract problems*, *KPIs, outsourcing and contract problems*). 📘 marks content from your course material; 📗 marks standard textbook knowledge added to complete an answer.\n\n## Syllabus for the end-sem (as announced) ⭐⭐⭐\n| # | Topic | Source in the zip | Theory page | Problems page |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Outsourcing and Contract Problems** | KPIs, outsourcing and contract problems (Que 1–13) | [Outsourcing & Contracts](ch01-outsourcing-contracts.md) | [Outsourcing & Contract Problems](p1-outsourcing-contract-problems.md) |\n| 2 | **Incoterms PPT + Problems** | Incoterms.pdf; IL problems for practice (Cases 1–10, Problems 11–12) | [Incoterms® 2020](ch02-incoterms.md) | [Incoterms Problems](p2-incoterms-problems.md) |\n| 3 | **Containerization and Container Balance PPT + Problems** | Containerization and container imbalance.pdf; IL problems for practice (Q1–Q5) | [Containerization & Imbalance](ch03-containerization-container-imbalance.md) | [Container Imbalance Problems](p3-container-imbalance-problems.md) |\n| 4 | **Maritime Costing PPT** | Maritime and logistics costing.pdf | [Maritime & Logistics Costing](ch04-maritime-logistics-costing.md) | [Freight Costing Numericals](p5-freight-costing-numericals.md) |\n| 5 | **Air Security Cost PDF** | Air security and cost.pdf | [Air Freight Pricing & Security](ch05-air-freight-pricing-security.md) | [Freight Costing Numericals](p5-freight-costing-numericals.md) (Q13–Q20) |\n| 6 | **Insurance and Contract Problems** | Insurance and contract problems.docx (Que 1–7, 9) | [Marine Insurance & GA](ch06-marine-insurance-general-average.md) | [Insurance & GA Problems](p4-insurance-general-average-problems.md) |\n| 7 | **Air Transportation PPT** | Air Transportation.pdf | [Air Transportation](ch07-air-transportation.md) | — |\n| 8 | **KPIs Book Case** | KPIs book cases.pdf + Pre-read material for KPI case.pdf | [Transportation KPIs & Scorecards](ch08-transportation-kpis-scorecards.md) | [KPI Book Cases](p6-kpi-book-cases.md) |\n\n**Not in the exam** (per the announcement): the **HBR cases** (Maersk, DHL, JSW Steel, Adani, KTZ, Bolloré, Flying Cargo A/B, Cold chain at Indo-Euro Pharma) and the other zip items (Intro to IL, Port shipping, Import–Export documents, cold-chain research papers, the warehouse-layout textbook scan). The only file from the \"HBR cases and articles\" folder that *is* in scope is *Air security and cost* and the *KPIs book cases* with their pre-read. The Omega research article on empty container repositioning is used only where the Containerization PPT refers to it (\"refer the omega paper\").\n\n## About the numericals ⭐⭐⭐\nEvery numerical in the problem pages is the **exact problem** from the zip — same data, same sub-questions, same numbering — followed by a full worked answer. The course files give questions but **no answers**, so the answers are worked out here step by step. Where a question cannot be solved without an assumption (risk probabilities, vendor ratings for a new weight, carrying-cost rate), the assumption is written in **bold** before the working, and alternative readings are shown. Do the same in the exam: **state the assumption, show the formula, show the substitution, box the answer, interpret it in one line.**\n\n## How to use this site\n- **Start** with [Rapid Revision](rapid-revision.md) for all formulas, lists and numbers on one page.\n- For each topic read the **theory page**, then attempt the **problems page** with **Self-test** on (press `T`) — answers stay hidden until you reveal them.\n- Use the **[Exam Q&A Bank](exam-questions-qna.md)** for long-answer practice, the **[MCQ Bank](mcq-bank.md)** for quick checks, and the **[Glossary](glossary.md)**, Flashcards and Quiz for terms.\n- ⭐⭐⭐ = high-yield, ⭐⭐ = important, ⭐ = good to know.\n\n## Suggested 3-day plan\n| Day | Morning | Afternoon | Evening |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Incoterms theory + 12 cases | Container imbalance theory + 5 questions | MCQs on both |\n| 2 | Insurance & GA theory + all GA numericals (do them by hand) | Outsourcing theory + Que 1–13 | KPI chapter + Vibrant Video & Bob's BBQs |\n| 3 | Maritime costing + Air security cost + freight numericals | Air Transportation PPT | Rapid revision, Q&A bank, redo every numerical once |\n\n## Exam-day answer tips ⭐⭐\n- **Incoterms case**: rule + named place + \"Incoterms® 2020\" → clue words → seller/buyer table → exact risk point → insurance → why not the nearest alternative.\n- **GA numerical**: always present the **settlement table** (made good − contribution = net) and check that nets sum to zero.\n- **Outsourcing/contract case**: identify clause → apply facts → evidence → recommendation with a condition.\n- **KPI**: formula → value → compare with target → corrective action → reward or penalty.\n- Draw: the E-F-C-D Incoterms ladder, the container imbalance loop, the fishbone, the risk heat-map, the 15-step air freight flow.\n"}, {"slug": "rapid-revision", "label": "Rapid Revision", "tag": "★", "group": "start", "title": "Rapid Revision — Everything on One Page", "md": "# Rapid Revision — Everything on One Page\nThe night-before sheet: every list, number and formula from the eight topics. Click through to the full pages when something is unclear.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Outsourcing & contracts ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Outsourcing** = who (third party). **Offshoring** = where (another country). Captive offshoring = own unit abroad. **Nearshoring** = nearby country (US→Mexico, EU→Poland).\n- **Why outsource**: cost, core focus, capability/technology, flexibility, speed, risk sharing, capital release. **Risks**: control loss, hidden costs, quality, IP, lock-in, compliance, currency/political, ethics.\n- **1PL** shipper · **2PL** carrier · **3PL** contract logistics · **4PL** integrator managing 3PLs · **5PL** network aggregator.\n- **Weighted score** = Σ(weight × rating) ÷ 100 → rank → **sensitivity** (re-scale weights to 100).\n- **Total cost** = contract value + **hidden costs** (audit, travel, ERP integration, QC, contract management, transition, exit).\n- **Risk score** = Probability × Impact (1–5 each): 15–25 high, 8–14 medium, 1–7 low. 4 Ts: Treat, Transfer, Tolerate, Terminate.\n- **SLA element**: KPI + target + measurement + reporting + penalty + incentive + exclusions + escalation.\n- **Clauses**: SOW, price/indexation, SLA, **liquidated damages** (pre-estimate, s.74), **force majeure** (beyond control, notice, mitigation, suspension), **termination** (cause + cure period; convenience), warranties/indemnity, limitation of liability, NDA/IP, **audit rights**, code of conduct/ESG, BCP, change control, dispute resolution (arbitration), exit, step-in.\n- **BATNA** = best alternative to a negotiated agreement; ZOPA = overlap of reservation prices; interests not positions.\n- **Single vs multi-sourcing**: cost & partnership vs resilience & flexibility.\n- **Payback** = one-off cost ÷ annual saving. Que 12: Year-1 cost $12.0 m vs $14.3 m → save $2.3 m; later $2.9 m/yr (+$0.55 m productivity); payback ≈ 2.5 months.\n- **Que 1 answer**: B 8.20, C 8.10, A 7.75, D 7.50 → **B**. Sustainability 25% (proportional): B 8.33, C 8.08, D 7.58, A 7.46.\n- **Que 4**: hidden $720k → total $8.72 m → **8.26%**.\n- **Que 13**: order accuracy **98.37%**, inventory **98.50%**, damage **0.73%**, complaints **0.18%** → only complaints meets target → **penalty**.\n- **Que 9**: landed cost India $152.25, Mexico $172.80, Poland $176.80 → India cheapest; weighted matrix → Poland.\n\n## 2. Incoterms® 2020 ⭐⭐⭐\n- ICC, **1936**; revised 1953, 67, 76, 80, 90, 2000, 2010, **2020**. Answer: tasks, costs, **risk transfer**. Not: title, payment, price, breach remedies, FM, IP, tariffs, sanctions, disputes.\n- **Any mode (7)**: EXW, FCA, CPT, CIP, DAP, DPU, DDP. **Sea only (4)**: FAS, FOB, CFR, CIF.\n- **E** departure · **F** main carriage unpaid · **C** main carriage paid (risk at origin — two critical points) · **D** arrival.\n- Risk points: EXW seller's premises · FCA to buyer's carrier · FAS alongside ship · FOB on board · CFR/CIF on board at origin · CPT/CIP first carrier · DAP at destination ready for unloading · **DPU unloaded** · DDP destination, duty paid.\n- Insurance: **CIF = ICC (C)**, **CIP = ICC (A)**, both **110%**. Others: no obligation.\n- Export clearance: buyer only in **EXW**. Import clearance & duty: seller only in **DDP**.\n- 2020 changes: DAT→**DPU**; CIP to ICC (A); FCA on-board B/L; own transport; security costs; costs listed in A9/B9.\n- Best: containers → **FCA**; high value → **CIP**; bulk → **FOB**; max buyer convenience → **DDP**; max buyer control → **EXW**. Never FOB/CIF for air or containers.\n- Format: *FCA Warehouse 3, Shanghai, Incoterms® 2020*.\n- Cases: vaccines **DAP** · machinery **FCA** · coffee **FOB** · engine parts **CIF** · garments **CPT** · laptops **CIP** · agri machinery **DPU** · solar **DDP** · grapes **CFR** · chemicals **EXW** · Problem 11: **CIF / DAP / DDP** · Problem 12: FOB wrong → **FCA/CIP**.\n\n## 3. Containerization & imbalance ⭐⭐⭐\n- ISO-standard box; intermodal = one load unit, many modes. McLean, *Ideal X*, 1956.\n- **20'**: 33.2 m³, 28,180 kg, 5.9 × 2.35 × 2.4 m. **40'**: 67.7 m³, 28,750 kg, 12.03 × 2.35 × 2.4 m. TEU/FEU.\n- **Objectives (7)**: weather protection, standard box, easy loading, temporary storage, anti-theft, crane hooks, less handling.\n- **Types**: general, thermal (reefer/insulated/ventilated), dry, flat rack, bulk, garment, tank, gas, open top, car carrier, special, open side, double door, tunnel, half-height.\n- **Imbalance**: cargo but no boxes here, idle empties there. Stockpiling: late orders, cash flow, storage space.\n- **Causes**: high rates, space, one forwarder, cargo weight, container shortage (+ trade imbalance, storage cost, time pressure, Excel planning, no cooperation).\n- **Effects**: late orders/cash flow, higher costs, delays, environment, unreliable commerce, inventory shortages, contract penalties.\n- **CAx**: 0.5 balanced; >0.5 surplus (more full in); <0.5 shortage (more full out).\n- **ECR facts**: 56% of life idle/empty; 170 m shipped, 50 m empty; 10% assets empty; 20.5% port handlings; $15–20 bn/yr; 5 free days then ~$100/day; 75% empty US/EU→Asia in 2020–21.\n- **CIC**: surcharge for repositioning; consignee pays if it receives more than it ships; shipper if it ships more.\n- **Solutions**: digitalisation, forecasts, CAx, ECR, virtual pools, **SOC**, street turns, sharing, substitution, connectainers, foldable (4-in-5, 6-in-1, −75% space).\n- Demurrage (inside terminal) vs detention (outside).\n\n## 4. Maritime & logistics costing ⭐⭐⭐\n- **7 steps**: export transport, export clearance, origin handling, freight, import clearance, destination handling, import transport.\n- LCL < 15 m³ (priced per m³); FCL > 15 m³ (supply–demand volatile). Sea minimum 1 m³; air may win if < 0.5 m³ and < 100 kg.\n- Road: LTL 1–2 pallets; **FTL 33 pallets ≈ 90 m³**. Rail: cheaper than air, faster than sea, greener than road.\n- Rate types: **spot, validity period, contract, all-in**. Actors: carriers, NVOs, **NVOCC** (own B/L), forwarders.\n- Surcharges: **BAF** fuel, **CAF** currency, **THC** terminal, **PSS** peak (Aug–Oct), **EBS** emergency bunker, **GRI**, green/CII fees, ISPS, documentation.\n- Methods: per mile, weight, DIM (÷139 domestic / ÷166 intl, inches→lb), flat, zone, freight class (density; NMFC 50–500).\n- Ocean: chargeable = max(actual, volume × factor); **1 m³ = 1,000 kg** (W/M). Total = base + surcharges + additional.\n- Examples: 2×500 = $1,000 · 0.5×2,000 = $1,000 · 24×18×12/139 = 37.2 lb · 500/64 = 7.81 lb/ft³ · 30×200 = 6,000 kg · 125×50 = $6,250 · Canada–US $20,000 · Shanghai–LA **$2,966** (CAF 5% of 2,280 = $114).\n- UNCTAD 2024: freight +15–25% (2020–23). Events: trade war 2018, COVID, Russia–Ukraine 2022, **Red Sea 2024** (Cape of Good Hope). 2M = Maersk + MSC (2015).\n\n## 5. Air freight pricing & security ⭐⭐⭐\n- Measure to extreme points; odd shapes = enclosing box; protrusions charged in full.\n- **6 m³ = 1 t; 6,000 cm³ = 1 kg; 1 m³ = 166.67 kg**; some use 5,000. Chargeable = higher, **rounded up to next 0.5 kg**.\n- Example: 150 kg, 120×80×50 → 80 kg vol → **150 kg** chargeable.\n- Minimum charge; rate per kg falls with weight; fuel & war-risk surcharges; origin and destination extras.\n- **Montreal 1999**: **22 SDR/kg** (1 SDR = $1.43493 → **$31.57/kg**) → insure door-to-door.\n- Risks: bombs, undeclared DG, contraband, theft, hijack/sabotage. Controls: screening, facility security, **known shipper**, training, access control, tamper seals, blast-resistant ULDs, biometrics.\n- **EU Reg 831/06 (2007)** client info + audits · UK known vs unknown cargo · **2010 Yemen bomb** · **TSA 2012** 100% screening on passenger aircraft · EU screening at origin 2012 · **ACC3 2014** · US–EU mutual recognition 2012.\n\n## 6. Insurance & General Average ⭐⭐⭐\n- Interests: ship (H&M), cargo (ICC), freight; liabilities (P&I).\n- Principles: utmost good faith, insurable interest, indemnity, subrogation, contribution, proximate cause.\n- **ICC A** all risks · **B** + earthquake, washing overboard, sea water entry · **C** fire, sinking, stranding, collision, jettison, GA. Exclusions: packing, inherent vice, delay, wilful misconduct, war/strikes unless added.\n- Losses: total (actual, constructive) and partial (**PA** accidental, own loss; **GA** intentional, common safety, shared).\n- **GA conditions**: real imminent common peril · extraordinary · intentional · reasonable · common safety · success · direct consequence.\n- **York-Antwerp Rules** (1864 … **2016**) via B/L and policies. Adjuster: GA bond + guarantee/deposit, values, rate, statement.\n- **GA rate = GA loss ÷ CV**; contribution = value × rate; net = made good − contribution (sum = 0).\n- Answers: **Q1** 5% (ship 450k, elec 125k, text 75k, furn 50k; elec net +175k) · **Q2** 4.5833% (wheat net +167,500) · **Q3** 3.75% (ship 375k, mach 112.5k, chem 75k, steel 37.5k) · **Q4** 7.931% (crude pays net 46,551.72) · **Q5** 7% (cars net +80k) · **Q6** 1.3333% → cargo ₹9,33,333 · **Q7** 1.0909% → cargo ₹19,63,636, ship ₹13,09,091 · **Q9** claim ₹28,22,400, uninsured ₹12,09,600.\n\n## 7. Air transportation ⭐⭐⭐\n- Services: express, standard, deferred. Direct routing = flexibility/speed; indirect = cheapest, remote reach.\n- AAI (Department of Cargo); **IATA** — non-political commercial regulator; 290 airlines, 400 partners, 100,000 agents.\n- **ULD** = containers of the skies. **AWB** (airline contract, limits liability, non-negotiable) · **HAWB** (forwarder/IATA agent) · **MAWB** (forwarder ↔ airline).\n- Hubs & spokes (high unit cost). **CASS**: one payment a month.\n- Advantages: speed, reliability, accuracy, control, scheduling. Disadvantages: risk, high cost, limited capacity, weather, unfit products.\n- Air = **4–5× road, 12–16× sea**.\n- Industry: IATA, airlines, lessors, cargo agents, airport authorities.\n- Price factors: **density, quantity, distance, shape, type**.\n- Services (6): air mail, express, scheduled, charter, leased (dry/damp/wet/**ACMI**), forwarder.\n- Aircraft: passenger (belly), **quick-change**, **combi**, freighter.\n- 15 steps: supplier → Incoterms → forwarder → documents → volumetric ratio → pack (security) → route → documentation → AWB → transit → pre-arrival clearance → arrival → CTO stores under bond → collection → delivery.\n- 2019: FedEx no. 1 airline (17,503 m FTK); Hong Kong no. 1 airport (4.8 m t). IATA benefits: errors −87%, fines 81%, customs delays 79%, aircraft damage 79%, speed 70%.\n- **IEC**: DGFT, 10-digit = PAN, update yearly.\n\n## 8. KPIs book case ⭐⭐⭐\n- **FOB Origin** → buyer owns & claims; **FOB Destination** → seller owns & claims. **Collect** → buyer pays carrier; **Prepaid** → seller pays. **Charged back** → buyer bears; **Allowed** → seller bears.\n- Table 11.6 (1 best): truck access 1; air transit 1; pipeline reliability/security/cost 1; air cost 5.\n- Metrics: on-time > 95%, transit low variation, damage < 1%, shortage < 1%, billing > 99%, **PDI = on-time × damage-free × billing > 95%**.\n- Table 11.8 example: 140 + 120 + 45 + 25 + 75 = **405/500**.\n- **Vibrant Video**: speakers LTL $12.43 → landed $187.43; **TL $7.37 → $182.37 ✔**; receivers **ground $10.85 → $235.85 ✔**; air $24.11 → $249.11.\n- **Bob's BBQs**: **Allied 460**, Certaint 400, **Bestway 360 → eliminate**.\n"}, {"slug": "p1-outsourcing-contract-problems", "label": "Outsourcing & Contract Problems", "tag": "P1", "group": "focus", "title": "Outsourcing, KPI & Contract Problems — Full Solutions", "md": "# Outsourcing, KPI & Contract Problems — Full Solutions\nThese are the **exact problems** from the course file *\"KPIs, outsourcing and contract problems word copy.docx\"* 📘 (Que 1 to Que 13, with the file's own numbering; note that the file uses \"Que 4\" twice). The file gives the questions only, so every answer below is a worked model answer. Where a question needs data that is not given (probabilities, ratings, weights), the assumption is stated in **bold** first: in the exam, state your assumptions the same way and you get full credit. Turn on **Self-test** (press `T`) to hide the answers.\n\nTheory for all of these is on [Outsourcing, Contracts & Partnerships](ch01-outsourcing-contracts.md).\n\n---\n\n## Que 1. Vendor Selection — ABC Electronics (PCB assembly) ⭐⭐⭐\nABC Electronics wants to outsource PCB assembly. Four vendors have been shortlisted. Ratings are out of 10.\n\n| Criteria | Weight | Vendor A | Vendor B | Vendor C | Vendor D |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cost | 30 | 8 | 9 | 7 | 6 |\n| Quality | 25 | 7 | 8 | 9 | 9 |\n| Delivery | 20 | 9 | 7 | 8 | 8 |\n| Technology | 15 | 8 | 8 | 9 | 7 |\n| Sustainability | 10 | 6 | 9 | 8 | 8 |\n\n**Q1.1 Calculate the weighted score of each vendor.**\nWeighted score = Σ (weight × rating) ÷ 100.\n\n| Criteria (weight) | A | B | C | D |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cost (30) | 30×8 = 240 | 30×9 = 270 | 30×7 = 210 | 30×6 = 180 |\n| Quality (25) | 25×7 = 175 | 25×8 = 200 | 25×9 = 225 | 25×9 = 225 |\n| Delivery (20) | 20×9 = 180 | 20×7 = 140 | 20×8 = 160 | 20×8 = 160 |\n| Technology (15) | 15×8 = 120 | 15×8 = 120 | 15×9 = 135 | 15×7 = 105 |\n| Sustainability (10) | 10×6 = 60 | 10×9 = 90 | 10×8 = 80 | 10×8 = 80 |\n| **Total (out of 1000)** | **775** | **820** | **810** | **750** |\n| **Weighted score (out of 10)** | **7.75** | **8.20** | **8.10** | **7.50** |\n\n**Q1.2 Rank the vendors.**\n1st **Vendor B (8.20)**, 2nd Vendor C (8.10), 3rd Vendor A (7.75), 4th Vendor D (7.50).\n\n**Q1.3 Which vendor should be selected?**\n**Vendor B.** It has the best cost rating (9) on the heaviest criterion, strong quality (8) and the best sustainability (9). The margin over C is small (0.10), so before signing, verify B's quality claims with a pilot lot and a site audit, and keep C as the backup/second source.\n\n**Q1.4 If the sustainability weight increases to 25%, does the ranking change?**\nSustainability rises from 10 to 25 (+15 points), so the other weights must fall by 15 points in total to keep 100. Two common ways:\n\n*Method 1 — reduce the other four proportionally* (each × 75/90): Cost 25, Quality 20.83, Delivery 16.67, Technology 12.5, Sustainability 25.\n\n| Vendor | Calculation | Score |\n|---|---|---|\n| A | 25×8 + 20.83×7 + 16.67×9 + 12.5×8 + 25×6 = 200 + 145.8 + 150 + 100 + 150 | **7.46** |\n| B | 25×9 + 20.83×8 + 16.67×7 + 12.5×8 + 25×9 = 225 + 166.7 + 116.7 + 100 + 225 | **8.33** |\n| C | 25×7 + 20.83×9 + 16.67×8 + 12.5×9 + 25×8 = 175 + 187.5 + 133.3 + 112.5 + 200 | **8.08** |\n| D | 25×6 + 20.83×9 + 16.67×8 + 12.5×7 + 25×8 = 150 + 187.5 + 133.3 + 87.5 + 200 | **7.58** |\n\nNew ranking: **B (8.33) > C (8.08) > D (7.58) > A (7.46)**. B stays first; **A and D swap** places because A has the weakest sustainability rating (6).\n\n*Method 2 — take the 15 points from Cost* (Cost 15, Quality 25, Delivery 20, Technology 15, Sustainability 25): A = 7.45, B = 8.20, **C = 8.25**, D = 7.80. Now **C overtakes B** and D again beats A.\n\n**Conclusion:** Yes, the ranking changes. The top choice is sensitive to how the extra weight is funded; B wins if all other criteria are scaled down, C wins if the weight comes out of cost. A, the least sustainable vendor, falls to last either way. This is why a sensitivity analysis belongs in every vendor-selection answer.\n\n---\n\n## Que 2. Outsourcing Risk Analysis — pharmaceutical packaging abroad ⭐⭐⭐\nRisks: IP leakage, quality failure, delayed shipment, regulatory non-compliance, currency fluctuation, labour strike.\n\n**Q2.1 Prepare a Risk Matrix using Probability × Impact.**\n**Assumption: 1–5 scales (1 = very low, 5 = very high), rated for pharma packaging in a lower-cost country.**\n\n| Risk | Probability (1–5) | Impact (1–5) | Score P×I | Rating |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Quality failure (wrong label, mix-up, contamination, seal failure) | 3 | 5 | **15** | High |\n| Regulatory non-compliance (GMP, US FDA/EU GMP, serialisation) | 3 | 5 | **15** | High |\n| IP leakage (formulation, artwork, packaging design) | 3 | 4 | **12** | Medium-high |\n| Delayed shipment | 4 | 3 | **12** | Medium-high |\n| Currency fluctuation | 4 | 2 | 8 | Medium |\n| Labour strike | 2 | 3 | 6 | Low |\n\nHeat-map view (Impact across, Probability down):\n\n| P \\ I | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **5** | | | | | |\n| **4** | | Currency (8) | Delay (12) | | |\n| **3** | | | | IP leakage (12) | Quality (15), Regulatory (15) |\n| **2** | | | Strike (6) | | |\n| **1** | | | | | |\n\n**Q2.2 Identify the top three risks.**\n1. **Quality failure** (15) — a packaging mix-up in pharma causes recalls and patient harm.\n2. **Regulatory non-compliance** (15) — a GMP warning letter or import alert can stop all supply to a market.\n3. **IP leakage / delayed shipment** (12 each) — joint third; for a patented drug rank IP leakage third, for a generic rank delay third.\n\n**Q2.3 Suggest mitigation strategies.**\n| Risk | Mitigation |\n|---|---|\n| Quality failure | Supplier qualification and GMP audit before award; quality agreement; AQL sampling and batch release by your QA; line clearance and vision systems; CAPA process; pilot lots |\n| Regulatory non-compliance | Choose a site already approved by US FDA / EU / WHO-GMP / CDSCO; regulatory warranty; audit rights; serialisation and track-and-trace compliance (DSCSA, EU FMD) |\n| IP leakage | NDA, IP ownership and indemnity clauses; share only what is needed (split artwork/data); restricted access; legal protection in that country; audits |\n| Delayed shipment | Safety stock; dual sourcing; delivery SLA with LDs; visibility (ASN, tracking); buffer lead time; freight contract with priority |\n| Currency fluctuation | Price in INR/USD; currency adjustment band; forward contracts/natural hedging |\n| Labour strike | Business continuity plan; second site; force-majeure definitions that exclude supplier's own labour disputes |\n\n**Q2.4 Which risks should be transferred through contractual clauses?**\n- **Quality failure** → quality agreement, warranty, rejection/replacement at supplier's cost, recall-cost indemnity, liquidated damages.\n- **Regulatory non-compliance** → compliance warranty and indemnity for fines/recalls, right to terminate for cause.\n- **IP leakage** → NDA, IP clause, indemnity, injunctive relief, audit rights.\n- **Delayed shipment** → delivery SLA with **liquidated damages** and service credits.\n- **Currency** → currency clause (fixed currency or sharing band), or transfer financially through hedging.\n- **Labour strike** → cannot be fully transferred: if it is the supplier's own labour dispute, exclude it from force majeure so the supplier remains liable; otherwise mitigate operationally (dual sourcing).\n\n---\n\n## Que 3. SLA Design — outsourced e-commerce warehouse ⭐⭐⭐\n**Q3.1 Design an SLA covering order accuracy, order fulfilment time, inventory accuracy, customer complaints, warehouse safety and damage rate; for each give target, measurement method, penalty and incentive.**\n**Assumptions: monthly measurement, fee-based contract; penalties and incentives as service credits on the monthly management fee, capped at 10% of the fee.**\n\n| KPI | Definition / formula | Target | Measurement method | Penalty (service credit) | Incentive |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Order accuracy** | Orders shipped with correct SKU, quantity and address ÷ total orders × 100 | ≥ 99.5% | WMS scan-verified pick/pack data + customer-reported mis-ships; monthly | 1% of monthly fee for each 0.1% below 99.5%; supplier bears re-shipping cost | 2% bonus if ≥ 99.8% for 3 consecutive months |\n| **Order fulfilment time** | Hours from order release to carrier hand-over (dispatch) | 95% of orders dispatched within 24 h; same-day for orders before 2 pm | WMS time-stamps (order release → manifest scan) | 1% of fee for each 1% below 95% | 1% bonus if ≥ 98% within 24 h |\n| **Inventory accuracy** | Locations/SKUs with system qty = physical qty ÷ counted × 100 | ≥ 99.5% | Weekly cycle counts (ABC), quarterly wall-to-wall audit by buyer or third party | Supplier pays for shrinkage above 0.2% of inventory value + 1% fee credit per 0.1% shortfall | 1% bonus if ≥ 99.9% |\n| **Customer complaints** | Complaints attributable to warehouse ÷ orders × 100 | ≤ 0.1% | CRM/ticketing tagged by root cause; monthly | 1% fee credit per 0.05% above target | Recognition + 1% bonus if ≤ 0.05% |\n| **Warehouse safety** | Lost-time injuries (LTIFR) and safety audit score | Zero lost-time injuries; audit score ≥ 90% | Incident register; monthly safety audit; quarterly external audit | 2% credit per reportable incident due to non-compliance; corrective plan within 7 days | 1% bonus for 6 months incident-free |\n| **Damage rate** | Orders damaged in warehouse handling ÷ orders shipped × 100 | ≤ 0.2% | Returns inspection + carrier claims + photo evidence at packing | Supplier reimburses product value of damaged goods + 1% credit per 0.1% over | 1% bonus if ≤ 0.1% |\n\nAdd: data source owned by buyer (WMS access), monthly KPI review, quarterly business review, root-cause and CAPA within 10 days for any miss, exclusions (force majeure, buyer-caused system outages, volume above forecast +20%), escalation ladder, and termination right after three consecutive months of failure on two or more KPIs.\n\n---\n\n## Que 4 (first). Hidden Cost of Outsourcing — textile production ⭐⭐⭐\nAnnual contract value = **$8 million**.\n\n| Item | Cost ($) |\n|---|---|\n| Supplier audit | 120,000 |\n| Travel | 80,000 |\n| ERP integration | 150,000 |\n| Quality inspection | 200,000 |\n| Contract management | 170,000 |\n\n**Q4.1 Calculate the total outsourcing cost.**\nHidden costs = 120,000 + 80,000 + 150,000 + 200,000 + 170,000 = **$720,000**.\nTotal outsourcing cost = 8,000,000 + 720,000 = **$8,720,000**.\n\n**Q4.2 What percentage of total cost is hidden cost?**\n720,000 ÷ 8,720,000 × 100 = **8.26%** of total cost. (As a share of the contract value it is 720,000 ÷ 8,000,000 = 9%.)\n\n**Q4.3 Why are hidden costs often ignored?**\nThey sit in other departments' budgets (IT, quality, travel), not procurement's; many arise after the decision (transition, integration); vendor proposals show only the price; procurement is measured on price savings; they are hard to estimate in advance; and decision makers are optimistic about a smooth transition.\n\n**Q4.4 Suggest methods to reduce these costs.**\nUse TCO in the business case; choose certified suppliers to cut audit and inspection (rely on ISO 9001/OEKO-TEX certificates, risk-based audits); shift to supplier self-inspection with third-party random checks (AQL); use virtual audits and fewer, combined trips; integrate through EDI/cloud portals or the supplier's existing connectors instead of custom ERP work; build quality clauses so the supplier pays for defects; standard contract templates and vendor-management tools; long-term contracts that spread one-time costs.\n\n---\n\n## Que 4 (second). Contract Breach — missed Monday deliveries ⭐⭐⭐\nA supplier agreed to deliver components every Monday and missed deliveries for four consecutive weeks. The manufacturer suffered production stoppage, emergency procurement and customer penalties. The contract includes liquidated damages, force majeure and a termination clause.\n\n**Q4.5 Which contract clauses become applicable?**\n- **Liquidated damages** — applies straight away: each missed delivery is a breach and the pre-agreed LD amount becomes payable without proving the exact loss.\n- **Termination clause** — four consecutive misses are a persistent/material breach, which normally triggers the right to terminate for cause (after any notice/cure period).\n- **Force majeure** — applies only if the supplier proves an event beyond its control that prevented delivery and gave timely notice. If it does, LDs are suspended for that period. If not, FM is not a defence.\n\n**Q4.6 Can the buyer terminate immediately?**\nOnly if the contract allows immediate termination for this breach. Most termination clauses require **written notice of breach and a cure period** (e.g. 15–30 days); if the supplier fails to cure, the buyer terminates. If the contract makes \"time of the essence\" for Monday deliveries, or lists repeated failure (e.g. 3 misses in a quarter) as grounds for immediate termination, the buyer can terminate at once. Practically, the buyer should first secure supply (a second source), then serve notice, so termination does not worsen the stoppage.\n\n**Q4.7 What evidence is needed before claiming damages?**\nThe contract and delivery schedule; purchase orders and the supplier's acknowledgements; proof of non-delivery (GRN records, gate logs, ASN vs receipt); written notices sent to the supplier and its replies; production stoppage records (downtime logs, idle labour/machine cost); emergency procurement invoices and the price difference; customer penalty invoices and correspondence linking them to the shortage; evidence of mitigation; and proof that no force-majeure notice was received (or that the claimed event does not qualify).\n\n**Q4.8 What preventive clauses could have avoided the issue?**\nEarly-warning/notice obligation (supplier must alert within 24 h of any risk to delivery); safety-stock or **vendor-managed inventory/consignment** clause; **dual sourcing** right and step-in rights; escalating LDs per week of delay; KPI/OTIF SLA with reviews; cure period with a recovery plan; capacity reservation; business continuity plan obligation; clear force-majeure definition with mitigation duty; right to buy elsewhere at the supplier's cost (\"cover\" clause).\n\n---\n\n## Que 5. Outsourcing vs Offshoring — software company ⭐⭐\nOption A: hire another Indian IT company. Option B: open its own development centre in Vietnam.\n\n**Q5.1 Which option is outsourcing?**\n**Option A** — a third party does the work (domestic/onshore outsourcing).\n\n**Q5.2 Which option is offshoring?**\n**Option B** — the work moves to another country but stays in-house (captive offshoring).\n\n**Q5.3 Compare cost, control, flexibility and IP protection.**\n| Factor | Option A — outsource to Indian IT firm | Option B — own centre in Vietnam |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Cost** | Low set-up cost; variable fees with vendor margin; quick savings | High set-up cost (entity, office, hiring, legal); lower long-run cost per developer once at scale |\n| **Control** | Lower: managed through contract and SLA | High: own people, processes and culture |\n| **Flexibility** | High: scale up/down by changing the contract | Lower: fixed costs, local labour law, exit is slow |\n| **IP protection** | Weaker: code and know-how with a third party (mitigate with NDA, IP clauses) | Stronger: IP stays in the company, though Vietnam's IP enforcement must be checked |\n| Other | Same time zone and language, fast start | Time to build (6–12 months), new legal and tax regime, talent risk |\n\n**Q5.4 Recommend one option with justification.**\nFor a company whose aim is **cost reduction** now: **Option A** — fastest savings, no capital, flexible, and IP can be protected contractually; keep architecture and core IP in-house. Choose Option B only if the work is core/IP-sensitive and the company expects a large, long-term team (e.g. > 100 developers for 5+ years), where captive economics and control beat vendor margins.\n\n---\n\n## Que 6. Contract Negotiation — transportation contract ⭐⭐⭐\nSupplier offers: $900 per container, 4-day delivery, minimum annual volume 5,000 containers. Buyer wants: price below $850, delivery within 3 days, no minimum commitment.\n\n**Q6.1 Identify the negotiation issues.**\nThree issues, all in conflict: **price** (gap of $50/container = $250,000 a year at 5,000 containers: 5,000 × $900 = $4.5 million vs 5,000 × $850 = $4.25 million), **transit time** (4 vs 3 days), and **volume commitment** (5,000 vs none). Underlying interests: the carrier needs **asset utilisation and predictable volume** to plan trucks/space; the buyer needs **low cost, speed and flexibility** because its demand is uncertain.\n\n**Q6.2 Suggest possible trade-offs.**\n- **Volume for price**: buyer commits to a realistic volume (e.g. 3,500–4,000) in exchange for a price near $850–870.\n- **Tiered pricing**: $880 up to 3,000 containers, $850 beyond 3,000 (volume discount).\n- **Speed for price**: standard 4-day service at the lower price plus a **3-day express option** at a premium (e.g. +$30) for urgent loads only.\n- **Contract length for price**: 2–3 year contract in exchange for a lower rate.\n- **Payment terms**: faster payment (15 days) for a discount.\n- **Fuel indexation (BAF)** so the carrier is protected against fuel risk and can quote lower base rates.\n- **Shortfall fee** instead of a hard minimum: buyer pays a small fee per container below the commitment.\n- **Bonus/penalty**: on-time bonus for 3-day delivery achievement.\n\n**Q6.3 Define BATNA for buyer and supplier.**\n- **Buyer's BATNA**: use other carriers/the spot market, or another contract carrier's quote (e.g. $870 with 3.5 days); or rail/multimodal. If the spot rate averages below $900 with acceptable speed, the buyer has a strong BATNA.\n- **Supplier's BATNA**: sell the capacity to other shippers or on the spot market; if the market is tight (high demand), its BATNA is strong and it can hold price; if there is overcapacity, it needs this volume.\n\n**Q6.4 Recommend a win-win agreement.**\nA **two-year contract** with a **committed volume of 3,500 containers/year**, **tiered price** of $870 for the first 3,000 and $840 above 3,000 (blended ≈ $866 at 3,500), **3.5-day standard transit** with a **3-day express lane at +$30** for up to 20% of loads, **BAF fuel indexation**, **95% on-time SLA** with a 1% rebate for misses and a 1% bonus above 98%, and a **shortfall fee of $25/container** below 3,500 instead of a hard minimum. The buyer saves vs $900 and gains speed; the carrier gets predictable volume and fuel protection.\n\n---\n\n## Que 7. Multi-Sourcing vs Single Sourcing — smartphone batteries ⭐⭐⭐\nOption A: single supplier, lowest cost, excellent quality, located in one country. Option B: three suppliers, higher cost, lower disruption risk, flexible capacity.\n\n**Q7.1 Compare advantages and disadvantages.**\n| | Option A — single supplier | Option B — three suppliers |\n|---|---|---|\n| Advantages | Lowest unit cost (volume leverage), excellent and consistent quality, simple management, deep collaboration on design (battery is a critical, safety-relevant part) | Lower disruption risk, flexible capacity for demand spikes, competition keeps prices and service honest, bargaining power |\n| Disadvantages | Single point of failure (earthquake, fire, export ban, sanctions); concentrated in one country; supplier gains bargaining power; capacity limits | Higher unit cost and more management (three qualifications, audits, tooling); quality variation between batteries; smaller volume per supplier |\n\n**Q7.2 Which strategy supports supply chain resilience?**\n**Option B (multi-sourcing)** — geographic and supplier diversity means one disruption does not stop production; spare capacity elsewhere absorbs shocks.\n\n**Q7.3 Recommend a sourcing strategy after a major earthquake.**\nMove immediately to **multi-sourcing (Option B)**: shift orders to the unaffected suppliers, expedite (air freight), build safety stock, and qualify suppliers in **different countries/seismic zones**. A practical steady design is **dual/triple sourcing with a 60/25/15 split** so the main supplier keeps scale but others are \"warm\" and qualified.\n\n**Q7.4 How would your answer change during stable economic conditions?**\nIn stable conditions cost and quality dominate, so **Option A (single sourcing)** or a **primary supplier with a small qualified backup (e.g. 80/20)** is attractive. Even then, keep a qualified second source and some safety stock for a critical component, because disruptions are unpredictable.\n\n---\n\n## Que 8. Outsourcing Failure — cold-chain transportation for a food company ⭐⭐⭐\nProblems: temperature excursions, delayed deliveries, product spoilage, customer complaints.\n\n**Q8.1 Identify root causes using a Fishbone (Ishikawa) diagram.**\nHead of the fish (effect): **\"Temperature excursions → spoilage, delays and complaints.\"**\n\n```\n MAN (People)            MACHINE (Equipment)        METHOD (Process)\n - Untrained drivers      - Old/poorly maintained     - No pre-cooling of vehicle/product\n - Doors opened often       reefer units              - Poor loading (blocked airflow)\n - Fatigue, no incentive  - Reefer switched off to    - No SOP for breakdowns\n   to protect temp          save fuel                 - Route planning ignores traffic\n          \\                        |                        /\n           \\_______________________|_______________________/____ EFFECT: Temperature\n           /                       |                        \\     excursions, spoilage,\n          /                        |                         \\    delays, complaints\n MATERIAL                  MEASUREMENT                ENVIRONMENT / MANAGEMENT\n - Poor insulated packaging - No real-time IoT loggers  - High ambient heat, traffic jams\n - Wrong gel packs/dry ice  - Uncalibrated sensors      - Contract awarded on lowest price\n - Mixed loads with          - No alarm/escalation      - No temperature/OTIF SLA or penalty\n   incompatible temps                                    - Weak supplier audits\n```\n\n**Q8.2 Recommend corrective actions.**\nImmediate: quarantine and inspect affected stock; root-cause each excursion from logger data; replace defective reefers. Corrective/preventive (CAPA): mandatory **real-time IoT temperature monitoring** with alerts to both parties; pre-cooling and loading SOPs; driver training and certification (e.g. GDP-style); preventive maintenance schedule for reefer units; better insulated packaging; route optimisation and time-window planning; backup vehicles; **temperature and OTIF clauses with penalties** in the contract; monthly joint reviews.\n\n**Q8.3 Should the company terminate the contract or improve supplier performance?**\n**First improve, with a deadline.** Issue a formal notice and a 60–90 day **performance improvement plan** with measurable targets (excursions, OTIF). Termination is costly and disruptive (finding and qualifying a new cold-chain carrier takes time) and the causes are mostly fixable. **Terminate** if the supplier refuses to invest, misses the improvement targets, or there is a food-safety incident. Meanwhile qualify a second carrier as a backup.\n\n**Q8.4 Design five KPIs to monitor future performance.**\n| KPI | Formula | Target |\n|---|---|---|\n| Temperature compliance | Shipments kept within range for 100% of transit ÷ total shipments | ≥ 99.5% |\n| Temperature excursion incidents | Number of excursions (> X minutes outside range) per 100 shipments | ≤ 0.5 |\n| On-time delivery (OTIF) | Deliveries on time and in full ÷ total deliveries | ≥ 97% |\n| Spoilage / damage rate | Value (or units) spoiled ÷ value shipped | ≤ 0.2% |\n| Customer complaints | Cold-chain-related complaints per 1,000 deliveries | ≤ 1 |\n(Also: reefer uptime, data-logger availability, claim settlement time.)\n\n---\n\n## Que 9. Global Outsourcing Decision — automotive supplier in India, Mexico or Poland ⭐⭐⭐\n| Factor | India | Mexico | Poland |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Unit cost ($) | 145 | 160 | 170 |\n| Lead time (days) | 30 | 18 | 15 |\n| Quality (%) | 96 | 98 | 99 |\n| Tariff (%) | 5 | 8 | 4 |\n| Political risk | Medium | Medium | Low |\n| Currency risk | High | Medium | Low |\n\n**Q9.1 Recommend a supplier using a weighted decision matrix.**\n**Assumptions: weights — landed cost 30, lead time 20, quality 20, political risk 15, currency risk 15; ratings 1–10 (10 = best).**\n\n| Criterion (weight) | India | Mexico | Poland |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Landed cost (30) | 10 → 300 | 7 → 210 | 6 → 180 |\n| Lead time (20) | 4 → 80 | 8 → 160 | 9 → 180 |\n| Quality (20) | 6 → 120 | 8 → 160 | 10 → 200 |\n| Political risk (15) | 6 → 90 | 6 → 90 | 9 → 135 |\n| Currency risk (15) | 4 → 60 | 6 → 90 | 9 → 135 |\n| **Total ÷ 100** | **6.50** | **7.10** | **8.30** |\n\n**Recommendation: Poland (8.30)** — best quality, shortest lead time and lowest political/currency risk; it loses only on price. Even if cost weight is raised to 50% (others 15/15/10/10), Poland (7.65) still edges India (7.50) and Mexico (7.10).\n\n**Q9.2 Which country minimises total landed cost?**\nLanded cost = unit cost × (1 + tariff):\n- India = 145 × 1.05 = **$152.25**\n- Mexico = 160 × 1.08 = **$172.80**\n- Poland = 170 × 1.04 = **$176.80**\n\nAdjusting for quality (cost per good unit = landed cost ÷ quality yield): India 152.25 ÷ 0.96 = $158.59; Mexico 172.80 ÷ 0.98 = $176.33; Poland 176.80 ÷ 0.99 = $178.59. Adding in-transit inventory carrying cost (**assume 20% a year**: value × 20% × days ÷ 365): India +$2.50, Mexico +$1.70, Poland +$1.45 → India ≈ $161.1, Mexico ≈ $178.0, Poland ≈ $180.0.\n**India has the lowest total landed cost** (about $19 a unit cheaper than Poland), even after quality and pipeline inventory. Freight is not given; adding it would widen the gap slightly against India for a European or US plant.\n\n**Q9.3 How should geopolitical risk influence supplier selection?**\nIt should be an explicit weighted criterion and a **cost** in the landed-cost model (risk premium, buffer stock, insurance). Consider political stability, trade wars and tariffs (USMCA for Mexico, EU single market for Poland, India's FTAs), sanctions, export controls, conflict near shipping routes (Red Sea for India–Europe), currency volatility, and the exposure of a long, single route. High-risk sources need safety stock, dual sourcing, contracts in hard currency and hedging.\n\n**Q9.4 Should the company adopt nearshoring or offshoring?**\nFor an automotive (often JIT/JIS) supply chain, **nearshoring** — Mexico for a North American plant, Poland for a European plant — because short lead times, low inventory, quality and low risk outweigh India's ~$15–20 unit cost advantage. **Offshoring to India** makes sense for standard, high-volume, non-time-critical parts where cost dominates, possibly as a second source (a \"China+1/India+nearshore\" dual strategy).\n\n---\n\n## Que 10. Outsourcing Ethics — underage workers at a garment supplier ⭐⭐⭐\nThe contract contains a Code of Conduct, an ESG compliance clause and audit rights.\n\n**Q10.1 What ethical responsibilities does the buyer have?**\nDuty of care for human rights in its supply chain (UN Guiding Principles, ILO conventions C138 and C182); protect the children's welfare first (no harm from the remedy); investigate and verify; remediate; be transparent with stakeholders; prevent recurrence; ensure its own buying practices (low prices, short lead times) are not driving the abuse; comply with law (India's Child and Adolescent Labour Act; foreign due-diligence laws for export markets).\n\n**Q10.2 Which contract clauses should be enforced?**\n- **Code of Conduct** — breach of the no-child-labour requirement.\n- **ESG compliance clause** — material breach; demand a corrective action plan and possibly penalties.\n- **Audit rights** — unannounced and third-party audits (SA8000, SMETA, WRAP) of all lines and sub-contractors.\n- Also: remediation obligations, reporting, and the termination-for-cause clause if the supplier fails to remediate.\n\n**Q10.3 Should the contract be terminated immediately?**\n**Not automatically.** Immediate termination (\"cut and run\") can push children into more dangerous work and workers lose jobs. Recommended: suspend new orders, require an immediate **remediation plan** — remove the children safely from work, continue paying their wages or a stipend while they attend school until legal working age, offer the job to an adult family member, age-verification for all workers. **Terminate** if the supplier refuses, conceals, repeats the violation, or uses forced labour.\n\n**Q10.4 Recommend a supplier development plan.**\n1. Root-cause analysis (why children were hired: cost pressure, sub-contracting, poor HR checks).\n2. Corrective action plan with deadlines; age-verification and HR systems.\n3. Training for management and HR on labour law and the Code.\n4. Fair purchasing: realistic prices and lead times so compliance is affordable; longer-term orders.\n5. Worker grievance mechanism / helpline.\n6. Regular third-party audits and transparent reporting; traceability to sub-contractors.\n7. Recognition: preferred-supplier status and more orders for sustained compliance.\n\n---\n\n## Que 11. Force Majeure During a Pandemic — nationwide lockdown ⭐⭐⭐\nA supplier could not deliver because the government imposed a nationwide lockdown. The buyer wants compensation.\n\n**Q11.1 Does force majeure apply?**\nMost likely **yes**, if the clause covers epidemics/pandemics, government action, lockdowns or \"acts of government\" (India's Ministry of Finance in Feb 2020 declared COVID-19 a natural calamity for FM in government procurement). A government lockdown is beyond the supplier's control and makes performance impossible or illegal. It applies only for the **duration** of the lockdown, only if the lockdown actually **caused** the non-delivery, and only if the supplier gave **timely notice** and tried to mitigate. If the contract has no FM clause, the supplier can rely on **frustration** (Indian Contract Act s.56). FM normally means **no damages** for the period, so the buyer's compensation claim fails.\n\n**Q11.2 What evidence must the supplier provide?**\nThe government order/notification and its dates; proof that the facility and logistics were covered (not an essential service); written FM notice sent to the buyer promptly as the clause requires; evidence of the causal link (production halted, transport stopped); evidence of mitigation attempts (alternative sites, partial supply, essential-service permits); and resumption plans once restrictions lifted.\n\n**Q11.3 What alternatives can the buyer pursue?**\nSource from alternative suppliers (in unaffected regions/countries); use safety stock; reallocate available inventory; negotiate partial or phased deliveries; extend the delivery schedule; renegotiate terms/prices; terminate if FM continues beyond the period allowed in the clause; claim on **business interruption or contingent business interruption insurance** if covered.\n\n**Q11.4 How should future contracts be revised after the pandemic?**\nName **pandemics, epidemics, lockdowns and government orders** explicitly; require notice within a fixed time (e.g. 7 days) with evidence; a **duty to mitigate** and partial performance; allocation of available capacity; a time limit after which either party may terminate (e.g. 90 days); price/currency adjustment and **hardship** clauses; business continuity and multi-site obligations; data/visibility sharing; and dual sourcing rights for the buyer.\n\n---\n\n## Que 12. Outsourcing Financial Evaluation ⭐⭐⭐\nCurrent spend: production $12 million, logistics $1.5 million, administration $0.8 million. Supplier proposal: outsourcing price $11 million, transition cost $600,000, annual contract management $400,000, expected productivity improvement 5%.\n\n**Assumption: the $11 million price covers all three activities (production, logistics and administration), and the 5% productivity improvement reduces the outsourced cost by 5% of $11 million = $0.55 million a year from Year 2 (Year 1 is taken up by transition).**\n\n**Q12.1 Calculate the first-year outsourcing cost.**\nFirst-year cost = outsourcing price + transition cost + contract management\n= 11,000,000 + 600,000 + 400,000 = **$12,000,000**.\nCurrent in-house cost = 12 + 1.5 + 0.8 = **$14.3 million**, so Year-1 saving = 14.3 − 12.0 = **$2.3 million**.\n\n**Q12.2 Calculate annual savings after Year 1.**\nFrom Year 2 there is no transition cost:\nAnnual outsourcing cost = 11.0 + 0.4 = $11.4 million → saving = 14.3 − 11.4 = **$2.9 million a year**.\nWith the 5% productivity improvement (5% × 11 = $0.55 million): cost = 11.4 − 0.55 = $10.85 million → saving = **$3.45 million a year**.\n\n**Q12.3 Estimate the payback period.**\nPayback = one-time investment ÷ annual saving = 0.6 ÷ 2.9 = **0.21 years ≈ 2.5 months** (0.6 ÷ 3.45 = 0.17 years ≈ 2.1 months with the productivity gain). Even counting Year 1, the $2.3 million saving recovers the $0.6 million transition cost within the first quarter.\n\n**Q12.4 Should outsourcing be approved?**\n**Yes, financially** — savings of $2.3 million in Year 1 (16% of current cost) and $2.9–3.45 million a year after, with a payback of under three months. Approve subject to: an SLA with KPIs and penalties for quality and delivery; IP and confidentiality protection; a risk review of the supplier's financial health; a phased transition with a parallel run; and an exit clause.\n\n> **Alternative reading** (if the $11 million replaces only production, and logistics and administration stay in-house): Year-1 cost of the outsourced part = $12.0 million vs $12 million today → **no saving in Year 1**; from Year 2 the saving is 12.0 − 11.4 = $0.6 million (or $1.15 million with productivity), and payback = 0.6 ÷ 0.6 = **1 year** (≈ 0.5 years with productivity). Still approvable, but the case is much weaker, and qualitative factors (control, quality, risk) decide. Say which reading you use.\n\n---\n\n## Que 13. Contract Performance Dashboard — outsourced warehouse (KPIs) ⭐⭐⭐\nData for the month: orders shipped 24,500; correct orders 24,100; average fulfilment time 19 hours; inventory records 10,000; correct inventory records 9,850; damaged orders 180; customer complaints 45.\n\n**Q13.1 Calculate order accuracy, inventory accuracy, damage rate and complaint rate.**\n- **Order accuracy** = 24,100 ÷ 24,500 × 100 = **98.37%**\n- **Inventory accuracy** = 9,850 ÷ 10,000 × 100 = **98.50%**\n- **Damage rate** = 180 ÷ 24,500 × 100 = **0.73%**\n- **Complaint rate** = 45 ÷ 24,500 × 100 = **0.18%**\n- (Average fulfilment time = 19 hours — no target given; good if the SLA is 24 h.)\n\n**Q13.2 Compare the results against the targets.**\n| KPI | Actual | Target | Gap | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Order accuracy | 98.37% | ≥ 99% | −0.63 pts (≈155 more errors than allowed: 400 wrong vs 245 allowed) | ❌ Below |\n| Inventory accuracy | 98.50% | ≥ 99.5% | −1.0 pt (150 wrong records vs 50 allowed) | ❌ Below |\n| Damage rate | 0.73% | ≤ 0.5% | +0.23 pts (180 vs 122 allowed) | ❌ Above limit |\n| Complaint rate | 0.18% | ≤ 0.2% | −0.02 pts (45 vs 49 allowed) | ✅ Meets |\n\n**Q13.3 Recommend corrective actions for any KPI below target.**\n- **Order accuracy**: barcode/RF scan verification at pick and pack, pick-to-light or voice picking, weight check at packing, slotting look-alike SKUs apart, picker training and error-based coaching, root-cause log of mis-picks.\n- **Inventory accuracy**: ABC cycle counting (A items weekly), immediate put-away scanning, location labelling, control of adjustments, investigate variances, restrict system overrides, WMS discipline.\n- **Damage rate**: better packaging standards and dunnage, handling training, equipment maintenance (forklifts, conveyors), proper stacking/load limits, damage root-cause by SKU and shift, carrier hand-over inspection with photos.\n\n**Q13.4 Should the supplier receive a performance incentive or a penalty?**\n**A penalty (service credit).** Three of four KPIs miss target, including the two most important (order and inventory accuracy). Apply the contractual service credits, require a **corrective action plan** within an agreed time (e.g. 15 days) and track monthly. No incentive is due; the complaint rate meeting its target does not offset the other failures. If the misses repeat for three months, escalate under the termination/step-in provisions.\n"}, {"slug": "p2-incoterms-problems", "label": "Incoterms Problems", "tag": "P2", "group": "focus", "title": "Incoterms Problems — Full Solutions", "md": "# Incoterms Problems — Full Solutions\nThe **exact 12 cases** from *IL problems for practice final part 1* 📘 (Cases 1–10 are 5–10 marks; Problems 11 and 12 are 10 marks). The *Incoterms* PPT repeats three of them as \"Problem 1, 2, 3\" — Problem 1 = Case 5 (textiles, CPT), Problem 2 = Case 1 (vaccines, DAP), Problem 3 = Case 4 (engine parts, CIF). The PPT also ends with two questions, answered at the bottom. Theory: [Incoterms® 2020](ch02-incoterms.md).\n\n**Answer pattern for every Incoterms case (scores full marks):** ① name the rule with place and version (e.g. *DAP Berlin DC, Incoterms® 2020*); ② quote the clue words from the case that point to it; ③ seller's vs buyer's tasks, costs and customs; ④ the exact risk-transfer point; ⑤ insurance; ⑥ why not the nearest alternative.\n\n---\n\n## Case 1: Pharmaceuticals Export (DAP) ⭐⭐⭐\nAn Indian pharmaceutical manufacturer exports temperature-sensitive vaccines to a hospital network in Germany. The shipment moves by road–air–road. The exporter agrees to transport the vaccines up to the hospital's distribution center in Berlin, but the buyer will handle customs clearance and import duties.\n\n**Q1.1 Identify the most appropriate Incoterm.**\n**DAP — Delivered at Place (Hospital DC, Berlin), Incoterms® 2020.** Clues: seller carries the goods **to the buyer's premises** (a D term), the buyer **clears import and pays duties** (so not DDP), nothing is said about the seller unloading (so not DPU), and the route is multimodal road–air–road (DAP works for any mode).\n\n**Q1.2 Justify why this Incoterm is suitable for temperature-sensitive pharmaceutical products.**\n- The **seller controls the whole cold chain** door-to-door — one party chooses the GDP-certified forwarder, the active/passive temperature-controlled packaging, the airline (CEIV Pharma), monitoring loggers and the transfer at each hand-over (road → air → road). Splitting the chain between two parties (as under FCA/CPT) creates gaps at hand-overs, where most excursions happen.\n- **Risk stays with the seller until arrival in Berlin**, so the seller has every incentive to protect the temperature, and the hospital receives a product it has not had to bear transit risk on.\n- The **buyer**, a German hospital network, is better placed to handle **EU import clearance, import VAT and any regulatory release (GDP/EMA requirements)** — so DAP is better than DDP for an Indian exporter.\n\n**Q1.3 Explain the responsibilities of the seller and buyer.**\n| Seller (Indian manufacturer) | Buyer (German hospital network) |\n|---|---|\n| Provide goods, commercial invoice, packing list, certificate of origin, GMP/batch certificates | Pay the price |\n| Temperature-controlled export packaging and labelling | Unload at the Berlin DC |\n| Pre-carriage by road to the Indian airport; export customs clearance (shipping bill, IEC, drug export licence) | Import customs clearance in Germany/EU; pay import duty and import VAT |\n| Contract and pay main air carriage + on-carriage by road to Berlin DC | Obtain any import licences/regulatory clearances |\n| Bear all risk of loss/damage until arrival (insurance is not compulsory, but the seller insures in its own interest) | Bear risk from the moment goods are placed at its disposal at the DC |\n| Notify buyer of arrival; provide delivery document/POD | Take delivery; pay demurrage/storage if import clearance is delayed |\n\n**Q1.4 At what point does risk transfer from seller to buyer?**\nWhen the goods are **placed at the buyer's disposal on the arriving vehicle at the Berlin distribution centre, ready for unloading**. Unloading, and anything after, is at the buyer's risk.\n\n---\n\n## Case 2: Steel Machinery Export (FCA) ⭐⭐⭐\nA machinery manufacturer in Pune sells industrial equipment to a buyer in South Korea. The buyer appoints an international freight forwarder to collect the cargo from the seller's warehouse and arrange the entire international shipment.\n\n**Q2.1 Which Incoterm best fits this transaction?**\n**FCA — Free Carrier (Seller's warehouse, Pune), Incoterms® 2020.** Clue: the **buyer's forwarder collects** at the seller's warehouse and arranges the whole international move — an F term with the delivery point at the seller's premises.\n\n**Q2.2 Why is this term preferred over EXW for international containerized shipments?**\n- **Export clearance**: under EXW the foreign buyer must clear Indian export — it usually cannot (no IEC, no Indian presence) — while under FCA the **seller clears export**, which it can easily do.\n- **Loading**: under FCA at the seller's premises, the **seller loads** the container/truck with its own forklifts/cranes (safer, insured, it knows the cargo); under EXW the buyer's trucker must load at someone else's site.\n- **Proof of export**: the seller gets the shipping bill/export evidence it needs for GST refund (zero-rated export) and duty drawback/RoDTEP; under EXW it may not.\n- **Container practice**: FCA fits containers and multimodal transport; the 2020 version even allows an **on-board B/L** to be issued to the seller for L/C payment.\n\n**Q2.3 Explain the responsibilities of both parties.**\n| Seller (Pune manufacturer) | Buyer (South Korean importer) |\n|---|---|\n| Goods, invoice, packing list; export packing and marking | Nominate and pay the forwarder/carrier; tell seller the pick-up date |\n| **Load** the goods onto the buyer's collecting vehicle at its warehouse | Pay all main carriage (road to port, ocean/air to Korea, on-carriage) |\n| **Export customs clearance** and export duties/formalities | Insurance (optional, buyer's interest) |\n| Bear risk until loaded on the buyer's vehicle | Bear risk from loading onward; Korean import clearance, duty, VAT |\n| Provide proof of delivery; assist with documents | Take delivery at destination |\n\n**Q2.4 Who bears the export clearance responsibility?**\nThe **seller** — under FCA the seller clears the goods for export (in India: shipping bill filed via ICEGATE under the seller's IEC).\n\n---\n\n## Case 3: Coffee Beans Export (FOB) ⭐⭐⭐\nA coffee exporter in India sells 15 containers of coffee beans to a buyer in Italy. The buyer nominates the shipping line. The seller is responsible for transporting the containers to Mumbai Port and loading them onto the buyer's vessel.\n\n**Q3.1 Identify the correct Incoterm.**\n**FOB — Free On Board (Mumbai / Nhava Sheva port), Incoterms® 2020.** Clues: **buyer nominates the shipping line** (F term, buyer pays main freight), seller takes the goods to the port **and loads them onto the vessel**.\n(*Exam note:* strictly, for containers ICC recommends **FCA Mumbai container terminal**, because the containers are handed over at the CY before loading. The case explicitly makes the seller responsible up to loading on board, so FOB is the expected answer; mention FCA as the better practice for a bonus mark.)\n\n**Q3.2 Explain when the risk transfers.**\nWhen the goods are **placed on board the vessel** nominated by the buyer at Mumbai port (or when the seller procures goods already so delivered). Damage before loading (e.g. a container dropped on the quay) is the seller's; damage at sea is the buyer's.\n\n**Q3.3 Why is this Incoterm suitable for sea transport?**\nFOB is a maritime-only rule built around the ship's rail/on-board point; the seller handles what it knows (inland haulage in India, export clearance, port handling and loading) and the buyer controls the ocean freight, vessel choice and insurance — useful for buyers who have contract rates with shipping lines. It is the traditional term for **commodity trade** (coffee is a classic FOB commodity) and gives the buyer control over the main carriage.\n\n**Q3.4 Compare it with CIF in terms of insurance responsibility.**\n| | FOB | CIF |\n|---|---|---|\n| Who arranges and pays marine insurance? | **Buyer** (optional; in its own interest, since risk is the buyer's at sea) | **Seller must** buy insurance for the buyer's benefit |\n| Level of cover | Whatever the buyer chooses | Minimum **ICC (C)** for **110%** of the contract value |\n| Who pays ocean freight? | Buyer | Seller |\n| Risk transfer | On board at Mumbai | **Also** on board at Mumbai — the same point |\nSo the risk point is identical; the difference is who **buys** the insurance and freight. Under CIF the buyer gets a policy without arranging it but only basic cover; under FOB the buyer can buy better cover (ICC A).\n\n---\n\n## Case 4: Automobile Parts Export (CIF) ⭐⭐⭐\nAn automobile component manufacturer exports engine parts to Brazil. The seller agrees to arrange sea freight and marine insurance until Santos Port. After arrival, the buyer will clear customs and arrange inland transportation.\n\n**Q4.1 Which Incoterm should be used?**\n**CIF — Cost, Insurance and Freight (Santos Port), Incoterms® 2020.** Clues: sea transport, seller pays **freight and marine insurance** to the destination port, buyer clears import and arranges inland transport. (If the parts are containerised, **CIP Santos container terminal** is the modern equivalent.)\n\n**Q4.2 Explain why insurance is important under this term.**\nUnder CIF the **risk passes to the buyer when goods are on board in India**, but the seller controls the voyage. The buyer bears the risk for the whole sea journey without having arranged the carriage — so the contract requires the **seller to insure for the buyer's benefit**: minimum cover ICC (C) for 110% of the CIF value, with the policy/certificate endorsed to the buyer so it can claim in Brazil. It also protects the bank in L/C transactions. Because ICC (C) covers only major perils (fire, sinking, stranding, collision, jettison, general average), the buyer should ask for ICC (A) or buy top-up cover. Brazil also assesses import duty on CIF value.\n\n**Q4.3 What responsibilities remain with the buyer?**\nRisk of loss/damage from loading on board in India; unloading charges at Santos (unless included in the freight contract); **Brazilian import clearance** (Siscomex registration, import licence/LI, duties: II, IPI, ICMS, PIS/COFINS); paying duties and taxes; inland transport from Santos to its plant; any additional insurance; taking delivery and paying the price.\n\n**Q4.4 At which point does risk transfer?**\n**On board the vessel at the port of shipment in India** — not at Santos. (Two critical points: risk at the Indian port, costs paid by seller to Santos.)\n\n---\n\n## Case 5: Textile Export (CPT) ⭐⭐⭐\nA textile company exports garments from India to Spain using multimodal transportation. The seller pays the freight up to Madrid Inland Terminal, but the buyer agrees to arrange insurance.\n\n**Q5.1 Identify the most suitable Incoterm.**\n**CPT — Carriage Paid To (Madrid Inland Terminal), Incoterms® 2020.** Clues: **multimodal**, seller **pays freight** to a named inland destination, **buyer arranges insurance** (so not CIP).\n\n**Q5.2 Differentiate this term from CIP.**\n| | CPT | CIP |\n|---|---|---|\n| Carriage to named destination | Seller pays | Seller pays |\n| Insurance | **No obligation** on either party (buyer insures itself) | **Seller must insure**: ICC (A) all risks, 110% of value |\n| Risk transfer | At hand-over to the **first carrier** in India | Same — first carrier |\n| Price quoted | Lower (no premium) | Higher (includes premium) |\n| Suits | Buyers with their own (open/annual) cargo policy | High-value or fragile goods; buyers who want the seller to insure |\n\n**Q5.3 Explain who bears transportation costs and who bears risk.**\n- **Costs**: the **seller** pays export packing, export clearance and the **entire freight** (road/rail to Indian port or ICD, ocean, and rail/road to Madrid Inland Terminal). The buyer pays unloading at Madrid (unless included in the carriage contract), import clearance, duties/VAT and onward delivery.\n- **Risk**: passes to the **buyer** when the seller **hands the goods to the first carrier** in India (e.g. at the ICD/CFS). So the buyer bears transit risk for nearly the whole journey even though the seller pays the freight — which is why the buyer arranges insurance.\n\n**Q5.4 Why is this Incoterm useful in multimodal logistics?**\nCPT is designed for **any mode or combination**: one through-freight contract (often a multimodal B/L or FIATA FBL) covers road, sea and rail legs. Risk passes at the first carrier, a clear point that does not depend on a ship's rail; the seller controls routing and gets better rates, while an experienced buyer saves by using its own cargo insurance. It also fits containerised cargo, unlike CFR.\n\n---\n\n## Case 6: Electronics Export (CIP) ⭐⭐⭐\nA consumer electronics company exports laptops from Bengaluru to Singapore through road–air transportation. Since the products are expensive and fragile, the seller agrees to arrange comprehensive insurance during transit.\n\n**Q6.1 Which Incoterm is most appropriate?**\n**CIP — Carriage and Insurance Paid To (Singapore Changi Airfreight Centre / buyer's named place), Incoterms® 2020.** Clues: road–air (multimodal, not sea), seller pays carriage and **arranges comprehensive insurance**.\n\n**Q6.2 Explain why this term is preferred over CPT.**\nLaptops are **high value and fragile** and theft-prone; under CPT nobody is obliged to insure, and the risk passes to the buyer at Bengaluru. CIP obliges the seller to buy **all-risks (ICC A / Institute Cargo Clauses Air)** cover for 110%, giving the buyer comprehensive protection without arranging it — the course's recommended term for **high-value goods**. It also reassures banks (L/C) and makes the seller's offer more attractive.\n\n**Q6.3 Discuss the seller's insurance obligation under Incoterms 2020.**\n- Insure at the seller's cost, with reputable underwriters, **from the point of delivery (first carrier) to at least the named destination**.\n- **Minimum cover: Institute Cargo Clauses (A)** — \"all risks\" subject to exclusions (or ICC Air for air cargo). This is the 2020 change; under 2010 CIP needed only ICC (C).\n- **Minimum amount: 110% of the contract price** (CIP value + 10%), in the contract currency.\n- Provide the buyer with the **policy or insurance certificate** so the buyer (who bears the risk) can claim directly.\n- At the buyer's request and cost, add war/strikes cover (Institute War and Strikes Clauses).\n- Parties may agree a lower cover, but must say so expressly.\n\n**Q6.4 Explain the point of risk transfer.**\nRisk passes when the seller **hands the laptops to the first carrier** — e.g. the trucking company collecting at Bengaluru (or the airline/forwarder at Kempegowda airport if the seller delivers there). The insurance then protects the buyer for the remaining road and air transit.\n\n---\n\n## Case 7: Agricultural Equipment (DPU) ⭐⭐⭐\nAn Indian company exports heavy agricultural machinery to Kenya. The seller agrees not only to transport the machinery to the buyer's logistics center but also to unload it using cranes arranged by the seller.\n\n**Q7.1 Identify the correct Incoterm.**\n**DPU — Delivered at Place Unloaded (Buyer's logistics centre, Kenya), Incoterms® 2020.** Clue: the seller **unloads** at destination — the only rule with this obligation.\n\n**Q7.2 What makes this Incoterm different from DAP?**\n| | DAP | DPU |\n|---|---|---|\n| Delivery | On the arriving vehicle, **ready for unloading** | **Unloaded** from the arriving vehicle |\n| Who unloads | Buyer | **Seller** |\n| Risk transfer | Before unloading | **After unloading** |\n| 2010 equivalent | DAP | DAT (Delivered at Terminal) |\nBoth are D terms: seller carries to destination and clears export; buyer clears import and pays duty.\n\n**Q7.3 Explain the unloading responsibility.**\nThe **seller** must organise and pay for unloading at the Kenyan logistics centre — here, hiring and operating the cranes, riggers and lifting gear — and **bears the risk of damage during unloading**. It must make sure the place can physically accept the cargo (ground strength, access for cranes) before agreeing to DPU. The buyer must provide access to the site.\n\n**Q7.4 Why is this Incoterm appropriate for oversized cargo?**\nHeavy machinery needs **specialised lifting equipment and expertise**; the manufacturer knows the machine's lifting points, weight distribution and handling instructions. Making the seller responsible for unloading avoids disputes over damage caused during the riskiest operation (the lift), puts the job with the party that has the know-how and equipment contracts, and gives the buyer a complete delivered-and-landed service.\n\n---\n\n## Case 8: Solar Panels Export (DDP) ⭐⭐⭐\nA solar equipment manufacturer exports solar panels to a customer in Canada. The exporter agrees to arrange transportation, insurance, customs clearance, payment of import duties, and delivery to the buyer's warehouse.\n\n**Q8.1 Which Incoterm is applicable?**\n**DDP — Delivered Duty Paid (Buyer's warehouse, Canada), Incoterms® 2020.** Clue: seller pays **import duties** and clears **import customs** — only DDP.\n\n**Q8.2 Explain why this term places the maximum obligation on the seller.**\nThe seller does **everything**: export packing, export clearance, all carriage to the buyer's door, (voluntary) insurance, **import clearance in Canada, import duties and taxes (customs duty, GST/HST, any anti-dumping duties on solar products)**, and bears **all risk** until the goods are ready for unloading at the buyer's warehouse. It is the mirror image of EXW.\n\n**Q8.3 What responsibilities remain with the buyer?**\nUnloading at its warehouse; taking delivery; paying the price; providing information and assistance the seller needs for Canadian import clearance (e.g. business number, end-use details); bearing risk after delivery. (Insurance in this case is arranged by the seller though DDP does not oblige it.)\n\n**Q8.4 Discuss one advantage and one disadvantage of this Incoterm for exporters.**\n- **Advantage**: most **customer-friendly** offer — the buyer gets a landed, duty-paid price, which wins sales against competitors (common in e-commerce and B2C); the exporter controls the whole delivery experience.\n- **Disadvantage**: **high cost and risk uncertainty** — the exporter must know Canadian customs (CBSA), may need a non-resident importer registration and GST/HST account, bears duty changes (e.g. new tariffs or anti-dumping/countervailing duties on solar panels) and clearance delays/storage costs, and may be unable to recover import GST as a foreign company.\n\n---\n\n## Case 9: Fresh Fruits Export (CFR) ⭐⭐⭐\nA fruit exporter ships refrigerated containers of fresh grapes to Dubai by sea. The exporter agrees to pay ocean freight to Jebel Ali Port but does not arrange cargo insurance.\n\n**Q9.1 Identify the appropriate Incoterm.**\n**CFR — Cost and Freight (Jebel Ali Port), Incoterms® 2020.** Clues: sea, seller pays **freight to the destination port**, **no insurance** by seller (so not CIF). (For reefer containers, **CPT Jebel Ali** is the multimodal alternative.)\n\n**Q9.2 Differentiate this term from CIF.**\nSame as CIF in every respect — seller pays freight to Jebel Ali, risk passes on board at the Indian port, buyer clears import — **except insurance**: under **CFR** the seller has no duty to insure; under **CIF** the seller must buy ICC (C) cover for 110% of value for the buyer.\n\n**Q9.3 When does the transfer of risk occur?**\nWhen the grapes are **loaded on board the vessel at the Indian port of shipment** (e.g. Nhava Sheva/Mundra). Spoilage at sea from a reefer failure is the **buyer's** risk (the buyer then claims from its insurer or the carrier).\n\n**Q9.4 Why might buyers choose to arrange their own insurance?**\n- They hold an **annual/open cover** policy that is cheaper per shipment.\n- They want **better cover** than CIF's minimum ICC (C) — perishables need ICC (A) with **refrigeration machinery breakdown** cover (ICC Frozen Food clauses), which ICC (C) does not include.\n- Claims are easier with a **local insurer** in Dubai (local currency, local surveyors).\n- Control over the insurer's rating and claim handling; can bundle with storage/inland cover.\n- It lowers the price paid to the seller (no premium plus seller margin).\n\n---\n\n## Case 10: Industrial Chemicals Export (EXW) ⭐⭐⭐\nA chemical manufacturer sells industrial chemicals to a multinational company. The buyer collects the goods directly from the seller's factory and manages loading, export documentation, transportation, customs clearance, insurance, and delivery.\n\n**Q10.1 Which Incoterm best fits this transaction?**\n**EXW — Ex Works (Seller's factory, named address), Incoterms® 2020.** Clue: the buyer collects **at the factory** and does **everything, including loading and export documentation**.\n\n**Q10.2 Explain why this Incoterm gives minimum responsibility to the seller.**\nThe seller's only obligations are to **make the goods available at its premises**, suitably packed, at the agreed time, with the invoice, and to assist (at the buyer's risk and cost) with export information. It does **not load** the goods, does not clear export, pays no freight or insurance, and its risk ends when the goods are placed at the buyer's disposal — before loading.\n\n**Q10.3 Discuss the challenges of using this Incoterm in international trade.**\n- **Export clearance**: the foreign buyer generally **cannot be the exporter of record** in India (needs IEC, local registration); the seller ends up doing it anyway, outside the Incoterm.\n- **Loading risk**: the buyer's truck crew loads at the seller's site — safety issues (hazardous chemicals!), insurance gaps, and the seller usually loads anyway \"as a courtesy\" but at the buyer's risk, which creates disputes.\n- **No proof of export** for the seller → GST refund/zero-rating and drawback problems; possible tax liability.\n- **Dangerous goods compliance**: chemicals need correct classification, DG packaging, labelling and MSDS; the buyer may not know Indian DG rules.\n- **Security & screening, VGM** (verified gross mass for containers) and documentation responsibilities are unclear.\n- The buyer carries the most cost and risk and must have strong logistics capability.\n\n**Q10.4 Why do many exporters prefer FCA instead of EXW?**\nUnder FCA (seller's premises) the **seller loads** (with its own equipment, safely) and **clears export** (it is the natural exporter of record and gets export proof for tax refunds), while the buyer still controls and pays the main carriage. FCA keeps the low-responsibility spirit of EXW but removes its legal and practical problems; ICC itself recommends FCA over EXW for international sales.\n\n---\n\n## Problem 11: Choosing the Best Incoterm (10 Marks) ⭐⭐⭐\nA food processing company in India exports ready-to-eat meals to three international buyers. **Buyer A (Japan)**: wants the seller to arrange freight and insurance only until Tokyo Port. **Buyer B (Germany)**: wants delivery to its warehouse but will pay import duties. **Buyer C (Australia)**: wants the seller to bear every responsibility, including import duties and customs clearance.\n\n**Q11.1 Recommend the most appropriate Incoterm for each buyer.**\n| Buyer | Incoterm |\n|---|---|\n| A — Japan | **CIF Tokyo Port**, Incoterms® 2020 (or **CIP Tokyo port terminal** if containerised) |\n| B — Germany | **DAP Buyer's warehouse, Germany**, Incoterms® 2020 |\n| C — Australia | **DDP Buyer's warehouse, Australia**, Incoterms® 2020 |\n\n**Q11.2 Justify your recommendations.**\n- **A — CIF**: seller pays **freight and insurance to the destination port** and nothing beyond; the buyer handles Japanese import (food safety checks under the Food Sanitation Act), duties and inland transport. Risk passes on board in India. Because ready-to-eat meals travel in containers, CIP is the technically better term; both satisfy \"freight and insurance until Tokyo Port\".\n- **B — DAP**: seller delivers **to the warehouse** (a D term, door delivery) but **buyer pays import duties** and clears import, so not DDP; buyer unloads, so not DPU.\n- **C — DDP**: \"every responsibility including import duties and customs clearance\" is the definition of DDP.\n\n**Q11.3 Compare the seller's risk and cost under all three Incoterms.**\n| | CIF (A) | DAP (B) | DDP (C) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Seller pays | Export clearance, origin charges, ocean freight to Tokyo, minimum insurance | Everything to buyer's warehouse door: main + on-carriage, destination terminal handling | Everything in DAP **plus** import clearance, Australian duties and GST, biosecurity inspection fees |\n| Seller's risk ends | **On board vessel in India** (earliest) | On arrival at buyer's warehouse, ready for unloading | Same as DAP, but also bears risk of **customs/biosecurity delay, rejection and duty changes** |\n| Seller's cost level | Lowest of the three | Medium–high | Highest |\n| Seller's risk level | Lowest | High | **Highest** |\n| Customs knowledge needed | Indian export only | Indian export + transit to Germany | Indian export **and** Australian import (DAFF biosecurity, ICS) |\n\n**Q11.4 Which transaction exposes the exporter to the highest operational risk? Explain.**\n**Buyer C (DDP Australia).** The seller bears transit risk all the way to the door **and** the import process: Australia has some of the world's strictest **biosecurity and food import rules** (Department of Agriculture, Fisheries and Forestry — Imported Food Inspection Scheme, ingredient/meat restrictions, labelling under FSANZ). Any non-compliance can mean holding, testing, treatment, re-export or destruction at the seller's cost, plus demurrage and storage. The seller also pays duties and GST it may not recover, and needs an import agent/customs broker in Australia. Shelf-life of ready-to-eat meals makes delay especially costly.\n\n---\n\n## Problem 12: Wrong Incoterm Selection (10 Marks) ⭐⭐⭐\nAn exporter of precision medical equipment signs a contract using FOB, even though the shipment is transported by road–air. During air transportation, the cargo is damaged. The buyer argues that the seller should bear the loss, while the seller claims the responsibility ended after handing over the goods to the carrier.\n\n**Q12.1 Was FOB the appropriate Incoterm? Justify your answer.**\n**No.** FOB is a **sea and inland waterway only** rule; its delivery point is \"on board the **vessel** at the named **port** of shipment\". In a road–air move there is no vessel and no port of shipment, so the FOB delivery point **does not exist**. ICC explicitly says FOB is inappropriate for goods handed to a carrier before loading (containers, air). The contract therefore has an unclear risk-transfer point.\n\n**Q12.2 Which Incoterm should have been used?**\n- **FCA (named place, e.g. seller's factory or the airline's cargo terminal), Incoterms® 2020** — if the buyer arranges and pays the air freight. Risk passes on hand-over to the buyer's carrier, which is exactly what the seller believes happened.\n- **CPT / CIP (named destination airport)** — if the seller pays the freight; **CIP** is best for **precision medical equipment** (high value, fragile) because the seller must insure all-risks (ICC A / ICC Air) for 110% for the buyer.\n- **DAP** — if the buyer wants the seller to bear transit risk to its premises.\nUnder FCA, CPT or CIP, damage during the air leg would clearly be the **buyer's** risk (and under CIP the buyer would simply claim on the seller-provided policy).\n\n**Q12.3 Explain how the incorrect Incoterm created confusion regarding risk transfer.**\n- **Seller's view**: it handed the goods to the carrier (truck/airline), which is the \"FCA logic\", so risk passed then.\n- **Buyer's view**: FOB means risk passes only when goods are \"on board the vessel\"; since there was no vessel, it never passed — or passes only when loaded on the aircraft at the named place, or even later — so the seller still bore the risk when the damage happened.\n- The named place in FOB is a **port**, so the contract may name an airport or city that does not match the rule; each side reads the rule to suit itself. Insurance may also be missing or wrongly assigned, because each party assumed the other was at risk. The damage could have happened in the trucking leg, at the airport warehouse or in flight — with FOB, none of these maps to a defined delivery point. The result: a dispute that must go to negotiation or arbitration, delayed payment, and possibly the insurer refusing a claim from a party without insurable interest.\n\n**Q12.4 Suggest measures that companies should adopt to avoid such contractual disputes.**\n1. **Choose the Incoterm by transport mode** — never FAS/FOB/CFR/CIF for air, road, rail or containers; use FCA/CPT/CIP/DAP/DPU/DDP.\n2. Write the rule fully: **rule + precise named place + \"Incoterms® 2020\"**.\n3. **Insurance clause**: specify who insures, the cover (ICC A/Air), the amount (110%) and the period (warehouse-to-warehouse).\n4. Align the Incoterm with the **payment terms and documents** (L/C, AWB, packing, inspection).\n5. Train sales, logistics and finance staff on Incoterms; use a contract checklist/review by logistics before signing.\n6. For high-value/fragile goods, agree **packaging standards, handling instructions and pre-shipment inspection** with photos at hand-over.\n7. Add a **dispute-resolution clause** (arbitration, governing law, CISG applicability) and allocation of other risks Incoterms do not cover.\n8. Obtain a clean receipt (AWB/forwarder's cargo receipt) at the hand-over point to prove condition at delivery.\n\n---\n\n## Questions at the end of the Incoterms PPT\n**Q13. What INCOTERMS should you use?**\nChoose by: (1) **mode** — sea-only terms (FAS, FOB, CFR, CIF) only for goods loaded directly on a ship (bulk, break-bulk); all-mode terms (EXW, FCA, CPT, CIP, DAP, DPU, DDP) for containers, air, road, rail and multimodal; (2) **who controls the main carriage** — buyer → E/F, seller → C/D; (3) **where risk should pass** — origin (C, F) or destination (D); (4) **insurance** — seller insures under CIF (ICC C) or CIP (ICC A); (5) **customs ability** — the party that can best clear export/import does so (avoid EXW for exports, DDP if the seller cannot clear import). Defaults: **FCA** for containerised exports, **CIP** for high-value goods, **FOB** for bulk commodities, **DAP** when the seller delivers and the buyer clears import, **DDP** for maximum buyer convenience.\n\n**Q14. Classify INCOTERMS based on their mode of transport.**\n**Any mode or modes (7)**: EXW (Ex Works), FCA (Free Carrier), CPT (Carriage Paid To), CIP (Carriage and Insurance Paid To), DAP (Delivered at Place), DPU (Delivered at Place Unloaded), DDP (Delivered Duty Paid). **Sea and inland waterway (4)**: FAS (Free Alongside Ship), FOB (Free on Board), CFR (Cost and Freight), CIF (Cost, Insurance and Freight).\n"}, {"slug": "p3-container-imbalance-problems", "label": "Container Imbalance Problems", "tag": "P3", "group": "focus", "title": "Container Imbalance Problems — Full Solutions", "md": "# Container Imbalance Problems — Full Solutions\nThe **exact five questions** (5 marks each) from *IL problems for practice final part 1* 📘, slides 2–6. Theory: [Containerization & Container Imbalance](ch03-containerization-container-imbalance.md). For 5 marks, write about 8–10 crisp points, using the PPT's terms (empty container repositioning, CIC, CAx, SOC, trade imbalance).\n\n---\n\n## Question 1: Container Imbalance (5 Marks) ⭐⭐⭐\nAn Indian exporter based in Tiruppur ships garments regularly to the USA. However, the shipping line reports frequent shortages of empty containers at Chennai port, while surplus empty containers are lying idle at US west coast ports. This situation has increased export lead times and freight rates.\n\n**Q1.1 Identify the logistics issue in the above case.**\n**Container imbalance** (container inventory imbalance) on the India–USA trade lane: a **shortage of empty containers at the export origin (Chennai)** and a **surplus of idle empties at the import destination (US West Coast)**. The exporter has cargo but no box; boxes exist but in the wrong place. It calls for **empty container repositioning** and leads to **Container Imbalance Charges**. (Chennai's CAx would be persistently **below 0.5**, US West Coast ports **above 0.5**.)\n\n**Q1.2 Explain two causes of this issue.**\n1. **Trade imbalance** — the US imports far more from India/Asia (garments, consumer goods) than it exports back on the same lane; full containers arrive in the US, are emptied and have no return cargo, so they accumulate there, while Indian ports run short.\n2. **Slow/costly repositioning and storage economics** — an empty box occupies the same slot as a full one and nobody pays for it, so lines delay repositioning or prioritise higher-paying lanes (e.g. China); long dwell times, port congestion and labour disruption at US West Coast ports (ILWU–PMA negotiations) keep boxes stuck. Other valid causes: demand surges/peak season, lack of carrier cooperation (no pooling), outdated planning tools, inland empties not returned quickly.\n\n**Q1.3 Mention one operational impact on exporters.**\n**Longer export lead times and missed shipment dates** — the Tiruppur exporter must wait for equipment, so finished garments are **stockpiled** (tying up cash and warehouse space), orders are delivered late (risking cancellation, contract penalties, or expensive air freight for seasonal fashion), and it pays **higher freight rates plus CIC/peak surcharges**, squeezing margins.\n\n---\n\n## Question 2: Container Repositioning Strategy (5 Marks) ⭐⭐⭐\nA global shipping line operates routes between China–Europe–India. After peak season, it observes excess empty containers in European ports and shortages in Indian ports. To manage this, the company decides to move empty containers without cargo.\n\n**Q2.1 What is the process described above?**\n**Empty Container Repositioning (ECR)** — moving empty containers from surplus locations (European ports) to deficit locations (Indian ports) without cargo, so that they can be loaded with export cargo there.\n\n**Q2.2 Why is this process necessary?**\n- **Trade imbalance**: Europe imports more from Asia than it exports, so empties pile up there while Indian exporters need boxes.\n- To **meet export demand and protect service/revenue** in India: without boxes the line loses bookings and customers (CIMS factors: cost of customers, impact on brand, loss of revenue).\n- **Storage costs**: after ~5 free days, storage can cost up to $100 per box per day; idle boxes in Europe earn nothing and congest yards.\n- To keep vessels **utilised** on the next headhaul and maintain the container network's balance (avoid buying or leasing new boxes).\n\n**Q2.3 State two costs involved in this process.**\n1. **Ocean freight / slot cost** — the empty occupies a vessel slot that could carry paying cargo (opportunity cost) plus fuel.\n2. **Handling and port charges** — terminal handling (lift-on/lift-off) at both ends, **empty port handling**, transhipment.\nAlso: drayage/inland haulage of empties to and from depots, storage/yard rent at CFS or depot while waiting, repair and cleaning, documentation, and the administrative/planning cost. (Industry-wide ECR costs **$15–20 billion a year**.)\n\n---\n\n## Question 3: Container Imbalance Charges (5 Marks) ⭐⭐⭐\nAn Indian importer notices an additional fee labelled \"Container Imbalance Charge (CIC)\" on the freight invoice for shipments arriving from Europe. The shipping line explains that this charge is applicable on certain trade lanes.\n\n**Q3.1 What is Container Imbalance Charge?**\nA **surcharge levied by the shipping line/carrier** on top of base ocean freight to **compensate for the cost of repositioning empty containers** between countries/regions where there is a trade imbalance. It covers the cost of moving the box back (or onward) empty, plus drayage, transhipment and empty storage. It is charged per container (per TEU/FEU) on specific lanes.\n\n**Q3.2 Why do shipping lines levy this charge?**\n- Imports into India from this lane create **inbound boxes that may not find export cargo locally** (or the line must reposition empties to where they are needed); repositioning earns no revenue.\n- To **recover the cost** of empty repositioning ($15–20 bn industry-wide), storage (beyond free days) and handling of empties.\n- To **price the imbalance into the lane** that causes it, and discourage it (signal to balance flows).\n- Rate volatility and pandemic-era shortages made such surcharges common.\n\n**Q3.3 Who ultimately bears this cost?**\nThe **cargo interest** — here the **Indian importer (consignee)**, because the PPT's rule is that the **consignee pays when it creates the imbalance by receiving more containers than it ships**; if the shipper sent more than it received, the shipper would pay. Under the sale contract, whether the importer can pass it to the exporter depends on the Incoterm (e.g. under CIF/CFR the seller pays freight but destination surcharges are often for the buyer's account). In the end, the cost flows into the landed cost and is borne by **consumers** through higher prices. Always check the contract terms for such surcharges.\n\n---\n\n## Question 4: Decision-Making in Container Repositioning (5 Marks) ⭐⭐⭐\nA shipping company must decide whether to reposition empty containers from Dubai to Mumbai or lease containers locally in India during the export peak season.\n\n**Q4.1 Identify two factors influencing this decision.**\n1. **Cost comparison** — repositioning cost (Dubai→Mumbai ocean slot, handling at both ends, drayage, port charges) vs **local leasing cost** (per-diem lease rate × days of use + pick-up/drop-off charges + off-hire location fees) in peak season, when lease rates rise.\n2. **Time and availability** — transit time Dubai→Mumbai (≈3–5 days sailing + port time) and vessel space vs how fast leased boxes are available in India; missing peak bookings costs revenue and customers.\nOther factors: number of boxes needed and for how long; whether boxes will return loaded (lane balance afterwards); container type/condition needed (reefers, high cubes); the opportunity cost of using Dubai's surplus elsewhere; storage costs in Dubai if not moved; vessel slot availability on the Dubai–Mumbai service; lessor's drop-off locations; the line's own fleet utilisation/ROI.\n\n**Q4.2 Suggest the better option with justification.**\n**Reposition from Dubai to Mumbai** — recommended in most cases because:\n- Dubai–Mumbai is a **short, cheap feeder leg** (a few days, frequent services) and the line already **owns** the idle boxes — repositioning uses sunk assets and stops Dubai's storage/idle costs.\n- **Peak-season leasing** in India is expensive and scarce (high per diem and pick-up charges) and leased boxes must be returned/off-hired later, often with drop-off fees.\n- The line keeps **control of equipment quality** and avoids lessor terms.\n\n**Leasing locally is better** when: the repositioning cost per box (including handling at both ends) exceeds the total lease cost for the period; vessel space from Dubai is not available in time; demand is **short-term or uncertain** (lease short, avoid owning surplus later); or specialised boxes are needed. A practical answer: **reposition the base requirement from Dubai** and **lease locally (one-way/short-term) for the peak top-up**, using street turns and depot-direct moves in India to stretch supply.\n\n*Illustration (assumed figures):* repositioning ≈ $250/box (slot $150 + two lifts $60 + drayage $40) vs leasing ≈ $3/day × 60 days + $150 pick-up = $330/box → reposition. If peak slot rates jump to $350, repositioning ≈ $450 > $330 → lease.\n\n---\n\n## Question 5: Impact of Container Imbalance on Supply Chain (5 Marks) ⭐⭐⭐\nDuring the COVID-19 pandemic, exporters faced severe container shortages and high freight rates across Asian ports.\n\n**Q5.1 Explain how container imbalance contributed to this situation.**\n- **Demand shock**: in 2020–21 consumers in the US and Europe shifted spending from services to goods; **imports from Asia soared**, so full containers flowed west.\n- **Boxes stuck at the wrong end**: Western ports and inland depots were congested (labour shortages, quarantine, warehouse capacity), dwell times rose sharply, and empties were not returned; the US/EU had little cargo to send back — so empties **accumulated in the West** while **Asian ports ran out**.\n- **Carriers rushed empties back to Asia**, some shipping **~75% of containers from the US/Europe to Asia empty**, but repositioning could not keep pace.\n- **Capacity cuts and blank sailings** in early 2020, port closures in China, and fewer box manufacturing outputs reduced supply further.\n- With boxes scarce in Asia, **spot rates rose to records** (Asia–Europe/US rates up several-fold in 2021) and carriers added surcharges (CIC, peak-season, equipment imbalance); Asian CAx values stayed below 0.5.\n\n**Q5.2 Mention two supply chain impacts.**\n1. **Delays and longer lead times** — exporters waited weeks for equipment and vessel space; products reached shelves late; inventory shortages and stock-outs (and contract penalties for missed deadlines).\n2. **Sharply higher logistics costs** — record freight rates and surcharges raised landed costs and squeezed margins, especially for low-value goods (e.g. furniture, garments); some shipments became uneconomic.\nAlso: cash-flow stress from stockpiled goods, production slowdowns due to missing inputs (bullwhip), reduced reliability and predictability of global shipping, and more fuel use/emissions from empty moves.\n"}, {"slug": "p4-insurance-general-average-problems", "label": "Insurance & GA Problems", "tag": "P4", "group": "focus", "title": "Insurance & General Average Problems — Full Solutions", "md": "# Insurance & General Average Problems — Full Solutions\nThe **exact problems** from the course file *\"Insurance and contract problems.docx\"* 📘 — Que 1 to Que 7 (General Average) and Que 9 (cargo insurance claim); the file has no Que 8. The file gives the questions and value tables only, so every calculation and explanation below is a worked model answer. Theory: [Marine Insurance & General Average](ch06-marine-insurance-general-average.md).\n\n## Method you will use every time ⭐⭐⭐\n1. **Total GA loss** = all GA sacrifices + all GA expenditures.\n2. **Total contributory value (CV)** = ship + all cargo (+ freight at risk, if given).\n3. **GA rate** = GA loss ÷ CV × 100.\n4. **Contribution** of each party = its value × GA rate.\n5. **Net settlement** = amount made good to that party − its contribution (+ receives, − pays). Net positions add to **zero**.\n\n> **Who is \"made good\" what?** The cargo owner whose goods were sacrificed is made good the value of the sacrifice. The **shipowner** is made good the **expenditures it paid** (firefighting, towage, salvage, repairs, security), because it paid them on behalf of the whole adventure. Showing this full settlement table earns the top marks.\n> **Valuation note:** the given cargo values are used as contributory values (as the questions intend). Strictly, under YAR Rule XVII contributory value = arrived sound value + amount made good; mention this in one line if asked.\n\n---\n\n## Que 1. Fire on a Container Ship — MV Pacific Star (Singapore → Rotterdam) ⭐⭐⭐\nThe vessel catches fire; to stop it spreading, the captain orders several containers flooded with seawater. Extraordinary sacrifices and expenses: electronics damaged by seawater $300,000; firefighting expenses $150,000; emergency towing $250,000. The shipowner declares General Average.\n\n| Particulars | Value ($) |\n|---|---|\n| Ship value | 9,000,000 |\n| Electronics cargo | 2,500,000 |\n| Textile cargo | 1,500,000 |\n| Furniture cargo | 1,000,000 |\n\n**Q1.1 Calculate the total General Average loss.**\nGA loss = 300,000 (seawater damage to electronics — sacrifice) + 150,000 (firefighting) + 250,000 (towing) = **$700,000**.\n\n**Q1.2 Determine the General Average contribution percentage.**\nTotal contributory value = 9,000,000 + 2,500,000 + 1,500,000 + 1,000,000 = **$14,000,000**.\nGA rate = 700,000 ÷ 14,000,000 × 100 = **5%**.\n\n**Q1.3 Calculate the contribution of the ship owner, electronics, textile and furniture cargo owners.**\n| Party | Value ($) | × 5% = Contribution ($) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Ship owner | 9,000,000 | **450,000** |\n| Electronics cargo owner | 2,500,000 | **125,000** |\n| Textile cargo owner | 1,500,000 | **75,000** |\n| Furniture cargo owner | 1,000,000 | **50,000** |\n| **Total** | 14,000,000 | **700,000** ✔ |\n\n**Q1.4 Determine the final settlement payable to the electronics cargo owner.**\nElectronics owner is made good its sacrifice $300,000 and pays its contribution $125,000:\nNet = 300,000 − 125,000 = **$175,000 receivable** by the electronics cargo owner.\n\nFull settlement (check):\n| Party | Made good ($) | Contribution ($) | Net ($) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Ship owner (paid firefighting + towing) | 400,000 | 450,000 | **pays 50,000** |\n| Electronics | 300,000 | 125,000 | **receives 175,000** |\n| Textile | 0 | 75,000 | pays 75,000 |\n| Furniture | 0 | 50,000 | pays 50,000 |\n| **Total** | 700,000 | 700,000 | **0** ✔ |\n\n---\n\n## Que 2. Cyclone in the Arabian Sea — wheat jettisoned ⭐⭐⭐\nTo stabilise the vessel the captain orders part of the wheat thrown overboard. Losses: wheat jettisoned $250,000; tug assistance $120,000; emergency repairs $180,000.\n\n| Item | Value ($) |\n|---|---|\n| Ship | 7,000,000 |\n| Wheat | 1,800,000 |\n| Rice | 1,200,000 |\n| Sugar | 2,000,000 |\n\n**Q2.1 Calculate the total GA loss.**\nGA loss = 250,000 + 120,000 + 180,000 = **$550,000**.\n\n**Q2.2 Calculate the contribution percentage.**\nCV = 7,000,000 + 1,800,000 + 1,200,000 + 2,000,000 = **$12,000,000**.\nGA rate = 550,000 ÷ 12,000,000 × 100 = **4.5833%** (≈ 4.58%).\n\n**Q2.3 Calculate the liability of each stakeholder.**\n| Party | Value ($) | Contribution @ 4.5833% ($) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Ship | 7,000,000 | **320,833.33** |\n| Wheat | 1,800,000 | **82,500.00** |\n| Rice | 1,200,000 | **55,000.00** |\n| Sugar | 2,000,000 | **91,666.67** |\n| **Total** | 12,000,000 | **550,000.00** ✔ |\n\n**Q2.4 Calculate the compensation payable to the wheat owner.**\nNet compensation = jettison made good − own contribution = 250,000 − 82,500 = **$167,500**.\n(Shipowner: made good 120,000 + 180,000 = 300,000; contribution 320,833.33 → **pays $20,833.33** net. Rice pays 55,000; sugar pays 91,666.67. Net check: +167,500 − 20,833.33 − 55,000 − 91,666.67 = 0 ✔.)\n*Note:* emergency repairs count as GA here because they were needed for the common safety (YAR Rule XIV — temporary repairs to allow the voyage to be completed); permanent repairs of accidental damage would be particular average.\n\n---\n\n## Que 3. Pirate Attack — Gulf of Aden ⭐⭐⭐\nThe captain hires private security and an emergency naval escort. Expenses: security payment $200,000; naval escort $150,000; hull repairs $250,000. No cargo was sacrificed, so every stakeholder simply contributes according to the GA percentage.\n\n| Item | Value ($) |\n|---|---|\n| Ship | 10,000,000 |\n| Machinery | 3,000,000 |\n| Chemicals | 2,000,000 |\n| Steel | 1,000,000 |\n\n**Q3.1 Calculate the total contributory value of the ship and all cargo.**\nCV = 10,000,000 + 3,000,000 + 2,000,000 + 1,000,000 = **$16,000,000**.\n\n**Q3.2 Determine the total General Average loss.**\nGA loss = 200,000 + 150,000 + 250,000 = **$600,000**.\n\n**Q3.3 Calculate the General Average contribution percentage.**\nGA rate = 600,000 ÷ 16,000,000 × 100 = **3.75%**.\n\n**Q3.4 Calculate the GA contribution payable by (a) the ship owner, (b) machinery, (c) chemicals and (d) steel cargo owners.**\n| Party | Value ($) | Contribution @ 3.75% ($) |\n|---|---|---|\n| (a) Ship owner | 10,000,000 | **375,000** |\n| (b) Machinery | 3,000,000 | **112,500** |\n| (c) Chemicals | 2,000,000 | **75,000** |\n| (d) Steel | 1,000,000 | **37,500** |\n| **Total** | 16,000,000 | **600,000** ✔ |\nThe shipowner paid all $600,000, contributes $375,000 itself, and **receives $225,000** from cargo (112,500 + 75,000 + 37,500).\n\n**Q3.5 Explain why the expenses for private security, naval escort and hull repairs qualify as General Average expenses.**\n- **Real and imminent common peril**: an armed pirate attack threatens the ship, crew and every cargo (hijack, ransom, total loss).\n- **Extraordinary**: armed guards and a naval escort are not part of ordinary voyage costs.\n- **Intentional and reasonable**: the master deliberately incurred them as a prudent response.\n- **For the common safety**, and **successful** — ship and cargo were saved.\n- **Hull repairs** qualify where they are repairs of damage caused by the GA act (e.g. evasive manoeuvres, defensive measures) or temporary repairs needed for the common safety to complete the voyage (YAR Rule XIV); permanent repair of damage caused by the pirates' gunfire itself would usually be particular average — so state that the question treats them as GA. (Ransom payments are also commonly treated as GA by English courts — *Masefield v Amlin*, 2011.)\n\n**Q3.6 If the machinery cargo owner has a Marine Cargo Insurance Policy, explain how the GA contribution would typically be settled.**\nThe average adjuster asks for **GA security** before releasing the machinery: the owner signs a **GA bond**, and its **cargo insurer issues a GA guarantee** (instead of a cash deposit of $112,500). The cargo is released promptly. When the adjustment is final, the **insurer pays the $112,500 contribution** directly to the adjuster/shipowner — GA contributions are covered under ICC (A), (B) and (C) — subject to the policy limits (and proportionately if under-insured). The owner bears only any deductible.\n\n**Q3.7 Differentiate between General Average and Particular Average with reference to this case.**\n- **General Average**: the security payment, naval escort and (GA-qualifying) repairs were **deliberately incurred to protect everyone** from the pirates, so ship, machinery, chemicals and steel all share them at 3.75%.\n- **Particular Average**: if, say, a pirate bullet had **accidentally** damaged one chemicals container, or the ship's own equipment was damaged by gunfire with no deliberate act for common safety, that loss would fall **only on its owner** (claimed from its own cargo or H&M insurer), not shared.\n\n---\n\n## Que 4. Engine Failure and Salvage — tanker in the Indian Ocean ⭐⭐⭐\nThe captain hires ocean salvage services and transfers part of the oil cargo to another vessel. Expenses: salvage $400,000; temporary cargo transfer $180,000; engine repairs $220,000. Oil transferred suffers damage worth $350,000.\n\n| Item | Value ($) |\n|---|---|\n| Ship | 8,000,000 |\n| Crude Oil | 5,000,000 |\n| Lubricants | 1,500,000 |\n\n**Q4.1 Calculate the total contributory value of the ship and cargo.**\nCV = 8,000,000 + 5,000,000 + 1,500,000 = **$14,500,000**.\n\n**Q4.2 Calculate the total General Average loss.**\nGA loss = 400,000 + 180,000 + 220,000 + 350,000 (damage to crude during the transfer — a GA sacrifice) = **$1,150,000**.\n\n**Q4.3 Determine the GA contribution percentage.**\nGA rate = 1,150,000 ÷ 14,500,000 × 100 = **7.931%** (≈ 7.93%).\n\n**Q4.4 Calculate the GA contribution payable by (a) the ship owner, (b) crude oil and (c) lubricants cargo owners.**\n| Party | Value ($) | Contribution @ 7.931% ($) |\n|---|---|---|\n| (a) Ship owner | 8,000,000 | **634,482.76** |\n| (b) Crude oil | 5,000,000 | **396,551.72** |\n| (c) Lubricants | 1,500,000 | **118,965.52** |\n| **Total** | 14,500,000 | **1,150,000.00** ✔ |\n\n**Q4.5 Determine the net compensation payable (or receivable) by the crude oil cargo owner after adjusting for its GA contribution.**\nMade good for damaged oil = $350,000; contribution = $396,551.72.\nNet = 350,000 − 396,551.72 = **−$46,551.72** → the crude oil owner **pays a net $46,551.72**. (Its share of the common burden exceeds its own sacrifice because crude is a large share of the saved value.)\n\nFull settlement:\n| Party | Made good ($) | Contribution ($) | Net ($) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Ship owner (paid salvage + transfer + repairs = 800,000) | 800,000 | 634,482.76 | **receives 165,517.24** |\n| Crude oil | 350,000 | 396,551.72 | **pays 46,551.72** |\n| Lubricants | 0 | 118,965.52 | **pays 118,965.52** |\n| **Total** | 1,150,000 | 1,150,000 | **0** ✔ |\n\n**Q4.6 Explain why salvage, temporary cargo transfer and emergency engine repairs qualify as GA expenses.**\n- **Salvage operation**: with a total engine failure the tanker was drifting in peril (grounding, breaking up, pollution); salvage services were engaged **for the common safety** of ship and cargo — allowed in GA under YAR Rule VI.\n- **Temporary cargo transfer (lightering)**: deliberately transferring oil to another vessel to lighten/stabilise the ship and preserve ship and cargo is an **extraordinary expenditure** for common safety (YAR Rules VIII/X), and damage to oil during the transfer is a **GA sacrifice**.\n- **Emergency engine repairs**: temporary repairs carried out to enable the ship to proceed safely and complete the voyage for the benefit of all (YAR Rule XIV, limited to the saving of other GA costs). The breakdown itself is particular average; the emergency/temporary repair cost for the common safety is GA.\nAll are extraordinary, intentional, reasonable, for common safety and successful.\n\n**Q4.7 If the ship owner carries Hull and Machinery (H&M) insurance and the cargo owners have Marine Cargo Insurance, explain how the losses and liabilities would generally be settled.**\n- An **average adjuster** prepares the GA adjustment; cargo is released against **GA bonds + insurers' GA guarantees**.\n- **H&M insurers** reimburse the shipowner's share of GA ($634,482.76) and its GA expenditures not recovered from cargo, plus particular-average damage to the engine (the breakdown repair beyond GA), subject to the deductible.\n- **Cargo insurers** pay the crude and lubricants owners' **GA contributions** ($396,551.72 and $118,965.52) and indemnify the crude owner for its damaged oil (then recover via the GA adjustment by subrogation).\n- If the breakdown resulted from the shipowner's failure to exercise due diligence (unseaworthiness), cargo insurers may refuse/recover contributions and the shipowner turns to its **P&I club**.\n\n**Q4.8 Discuss the importance of General Average in protecting the interests of all parties involved in international shipping.**\nIt lets the master act **immediately** in an emergency (salvage, lightering) without negotiating with each owner; it **spreads the cost fairly** so the party whose property was sacrificed is not ruined while others benefit; it gives **legal certainty** through the York-Antwerp Rules in every B/L; it links neatly to **insurance**, so traders are protected; it protects the **environment and crew** by encouraging timely salvage; and it maintains confidence in shipping as a shared venture, especially on mega-ships carrying thousands of consignments.\n\n---\n\n## Que 5. Grounding of a Car Carrier ⭐⭐⭐\nA vehicle carrier grounds in heavy fog. To refloat it, several luxury vehicles are removed using floating cranes. Extraordinary losses: vehicle damage $500,000; floating crane $250,000; towage $350,000; repairs $300,000.\n\n| Item | Value ($) |\n|---|---|\n| Ship | 12,000,000 |\n| Luxury Cars | 6,000,000 |\n| Auto Parts | 2,000,000 |\n\n**Q5.1 Calculate the total contributory value of the ship and cargo.**\nCV = 12,000,000 + 6,000,000 + 2,000,000 = **$20,000,000**.\n\n**Q5.2 Determine the total General Average loss.**\nGA loss = 500,000 + 250,000 + 350,000 + 300,000 = **$1,400,000**.\n\n**Q5.3 Calculate the GA contribution percentage.**\nGA rate = 1,400,000 ÷ 20,000,000 × 100 = **7%**.\n\n**Q5.4 Calculate the GA contribution payable by (a) the ship owner, (b) luxury car and (c) auto parts cargo owners.**\n| Party | Value ($) | Contribution @ 7% ($) |\n|---|---|---|\n| (a) Ship owner | 12,000,000 | **840,000** |\n| (b) Luxury cars | 6,000,000 | **420,000** |\n| (c) Auto parts | 2,000,000 | **140,000** |\n| **Total** | 20,000,000 | **1,400,000** ✔ |\n\n**Q5.5 Calculate the net compensation receivable by the luxury car cargo owner after deducting its GA contribution.**\nNet = 500,000 − 420,000 = **$80,000 receivable**.\n(Shipowner: made good 250,000 + 350,000 + 300,000 = 900,000; contributes 840,000 → **receives $60,000**. Auto parts **pays $140,000** = 80,000 + 60,000 ✔.)\n\n**Q5.6 Explain why floating crane charges, towage, emergency repairs and damage to vehicles during refloating are treated as GA.**\n- **Floating crane charges** — hired deliberately to remove cargo and **lighten the grounded ship** so it could be refloated: extraordinary expenditure for common safety (YAR Rule VIII — expenses of lightening a stranded ship).\n- **Towage** — to pull the ship off and to safety: extraordinary expenditure for common safety (salvage/towage, Rule VI).\n- **Emergency repairs** — temporary repairs needed to make the ship safe to continue the voyage for everyone's benefit (Rule XIV).\n- **Damage to the vehicles during refloating** — damage caused **by the GA act itself** (handling by cranes in an emergency) is a GA sacrifice (Rules VIII/C — direct consequence of the GA act).\nIn each case there was a real common peril (a grounded ship can break up), the act was intentional and reasonable, and ship and cargo were saved.\n\n**Q5.7 Assume the luxury cars were not insured. Explain the financial consequences for the cargo owner.**\n- It must provide **GA security itself** — usually a **cash deposit** (adjusters often ask for a high percentage of cargo value, e.g. 10–20% or more = $0.6–1.2 million) or a bank guarantee — before the carrier releases the cars; until then the carrier exercises a **lien**.\n- Its cash is locked up until the adjustment is finalised (possibly years); it pays **storage, demurrage and delays**, and may lose sales/customers.\n- It eventually receives its net $80,000 only after the adjustment and collection from others — and bears the risk of other parties failing to pay.\n- Any **particular-average** damage (accidental, e.g. from the grounding impact itself) is unrecoverable, as are adjuster fees for its account and legal costs.\n- If the cars had been insured (ICC A), the insurer would have posted a guarantee, paid the $420,000 contribution and the sacrifice, and the owner would have suffered almost nothing.\n\n**Q5.8 Explain the role of the General Average Adjuster in this case and describe the steps followed in determining each party's liability.**\nRole: an independent expert appointed by the shipowner to decide what is GA and to prepare the **adjustment** fairly for all parties under the YAR and the B/L.\nSteps:\n1. Receive the **GA declaration** and the casualty facts (master's report, logs, survey reports of grounding, cargo damage).\n2. Collect **GA security** from each cargo interest (GA bond + insurer's guarantee or cash deposit) and commercial invoices.\n3. Classify each loss as **GA or particular average** (crane, towage, repairs, vehicle damage → GA).\n4. Establish **values**: ship's sound value at the end of the voyage, cargo arrived values (+ amounts made good), freight at risk → **contributory values** ($20 m).\n5. Total the **GA loss** ($1.4 m), adding allowable commission and interest (YAR Rules XX–XXI).\n6. Compute the **GA rate** (7%) and **each party's contribution**.\n7. Prepare the **adjustment statement** with amounts made good and net balances (cars +80,000; ship +60,000; parts −140,000).\n8. **Collect and distribute** balances, release security.\n\n**Q5.9 From the perspective of international logistics, discuss why General Average clauses are included in Bills of Lading and marine insurance contracts.**\nThe B/L is the contract of carriage with every cargo owner; the GA clause binds all of them in advance to share GA under the **York-Antwerp Rules**, at a named place of adjustment, and gives the carrier a **lien for GA security** — essential when a ship carries thousands of consignments of different nationalities. Insurance contracts include GA/salvage cover (all ICCs) so traders are protected and cargo is released promptly against guarantees. Together they ensure **fair loss-sharing, quick emergency decisions by the master, predictability across legal systems, fast cargo release, and lower disputes** — supporting the reliability of global trade.\n\n---\n\n## Que 6. Refrigerated mangoes, Mumbai → Dubai (₹) ⭐⭐⭐\nMachinery breakdown at sea; emergency power supply arranged to prevent total spoilage, costing **₹18,00,000** (GA expenditure). Ship value = ₹5 crore; total cargo value = ₹7 crore; freight at risk = ₹1.5 crore.\n\n**Q6.1 Calculate the total contributory value.**\nCV = 5 + 7 + 1.5 = **₹13.5 crore** (₹13,50,00,000).\n\n**Q6.2 Determine the General Average contribution rate.**\nGA rate = 18,00,000 ÷ 13,50,00,000 × 100 = **1.3333%** (1⅓%).\n\n**Q6.3 Calculate the GA contribution payable by cargo owners.**\nCargo = ₹7,00,00,000 × 1.3333% = **₹9,33,333** (₹9.33 lakh).\n(For completeness: ship ₹5 crore × 1.3333% = ₹6,66,667; freight ₹1.5 crore × 1.3333% = ₹2,00,000; total ₹18,00,000 ✔. The shipowner, who paid ₹18 lakh, recovers ₹9,33,333 from cargo; the freight share ₹2,00,000 is borne by whoever has the freight at risk — normally the carrier.)\n\n---\n\n## Que 7. General Average Contribution — automobile parts, Mumbai → Singapore (₹) ⭐⭐⭐\nEngine-room fire; to save the voyage the master hires emergency towing costing **₹36,00,000**, declared GA. Ship value = ₹12 crore; total cargo value = ₹18 crore; freight at risk = ₹3 crore.\n\n**Q7.1 Calculate the total contributory value.**\nCV = 12 + 18 + 3 = **₹33 crore**.\n\n**Q7.2 Determine the General Average contribution rate.**\nGA rate = 36,00,000 ÷ 33,00,00,000 × 100 = **1.0909%** (≈ 1.09%).\n\n**Q7.3 Calculate the GA contribution payable by cargo owners.**\nCargo = ₹18 crore × 1.0909% = **₹19,63,636** (₹19.64 lakh).\n\n**Q7.4 Calculate the GA contribution payable by the shipowner.**\nShip = ₹12 crore × 1.0909% = **₹13,09,091** (₹13.09 lakh).\nFreight at risk = ₹3 crore × 1.0909% = ₹3,27,273. If the freight at risk belongs to the shipowner (freight payable on delivery, earned by the carrier), the shipowner's total share = 13,09,091 + 3,27,273 = **₹16,36,364**. Check: 19,63,636 + 13,09,091 + 3,27,273 = ₹36,00,000 ✔.\n\n---\n\n## Que 9. Cargo insurance claim — 800 LED TVs, Chennai → Hamburg ⭐⭐⭐\nCIF value per TV = ₹42,000. Rough seas damage 12% of the cargo. The marine policy covers 95% of the cargo value. Deductible = 3% of the total cargo value.\n\n**Q9.1 Calculate the total value of the shipment.**\nTotal CIF value = 800 × 42,000 = **₹3,36,00,000** (₹3.36 crore).\n\n**Q9.2 Calculate the value of cargo damaged.**\n12% × 3,36,00,000 = **₹40,32,000** (96 TVs × 42,000).\n\n**Q9.3 Determine the insurance claim payable.**\n- Covered loss (95% of the damaged value) = 95% × 40,32,000 = ₹38,30,400.\n- Deductible = 3% × total cargo value = 3% × 3,36,00,000 = ₹10,08,000.\n- **Claim payable = 38,30,400 − 10,08,000 = ₹28,22,400.**\n\n**Q9.4 Calculate the uninsured loss borne by the exporter.**\nUninsured loss = damaged value − claim = 40,32,000 − 28,22,400 = **₹12,09,600**\n(= 5% uninsured portion ₹2,01,600 + deductible ₹10,08,000).\n\n> **Alternative convention** (deductible applied before the 95% cover): (40,32,000 − 10,08,000) × 95% = ₹28,72,800 claim; uninsured loss ₹11,59,200. State your method. Also note: a policy covering only 95% of value is **under-insured** relative to the usual 110% of CIF; with 110% cover the exporter would have recovered the full damage less the deductible. Heavy-weather damage is covered under ICC (A) (all risks); ICC (B) covers it only where sea water entered the vessel/container, and ICC (C) does **not** cover it.\n"}, {"slug": "p5-freight-costing-numericals", "label": "Freight Costing Numericals", "tag": "P5", "group": "focus", "title": "Freight Costing Numericals — Sea, Air, Road", "md": "# Freight Costing Numericals — Sea, Air, Road\nEvery worked example in the *Maritime and logistics costing* PPT 📘 and the *Air security and cost* PDF 📘, solved step by step in exam layout. Section E adds **practice problems** in the same style (marked *practice*, not from the zip) so you can drill the methods. Theory: [Maritime & Logistics Costing](ch04-maritime-logistics-costing.md) and [Air Freight Pricing & Security](ch05-air-freight-pricing-security.md).\n\n## Formula sheet ⭐⭐⭐\n| What | Formula |\n|---|---|\n| Distance rate | Freight = rate per mile/km × distance |\n| Weight rate | Freight = rate per unit weight × weight |\n| DIM weight (inches → lb) | L × W × H (in) ÷ 139 (domestic) or ÷ 166 (international) |\n| Air volumetric weight (cm → kg) | L × W × H (cm) ÷ 6,000 = kg ⇔ m³ × 166.67 (≈167) |\n| Volume (cm → m³) | L × W × H (cm) ÷ 1,000,000 |\n| Volumetric weight (given factor) | Volume (m³) × conversion factor (kg/m³) |\n| Ocean W/M | 1 m³ = 1,000 kg (1 revenue ton = greater of tonnes or m³) |\n| Chargeable weight | The **higher** of actual weight and volumetric weight (air: round **up** to next 0.5 kg) |\n| LTL density | Weight (lb) ÷ (L × W × H in ft) = lb/ft³ |\n| Total freight | Base freight + surcharges + additional costs |\n| CAF (as %) | CAF % × (base + BAF) |\n| Air carrier liability (Montreal) | 22 SDR × kg × SDR rate |\n\n---\n\n## A. Freight rate method examples (PPT) 📘\n\n**Q1. Per-mile rate: the rate is $2 per mile and the distance is 500 miles. Find the freight.**\nFreight rate = rate per mile × total distance = 2 × 500 = **$1,000**.\n\n**Q2. Weight-based rate: the rate is $0.50 per pound and the shipment weighs 2,000 pounds. Find the freight.**\nFreight rate = rate per unit weight × total weight = 0.50 × 2,000 = **$1,000**.\n\n**Q3. Dimensional weight: a package measures 24 in × 18 in × 12 in; DIM factor 139. Find the dimensional weight.**\nDIM weight = L × W × H ÷ DIM factor = 24 × 18 × 12 ÷ 139 = 5,184 ÷ 139 = **37.3 lb** (the PPT shows 37.2 lb). Freight is charged on this DIM weight unless the **actual weight is greater**, in which case actual weight is the chargeable weight. (With the international factor 166: 5,184 ÷ 166 = 31.2 lb.)\n\n**Q4. Flat rate: a flat rate of $1,200 is agreed between two cities. What is the freight?**\nFreight = flat rate = **$1,200**, regardless of weight or distance.\n\n**Q5. Zone-based pricing: shipping from Zone 1 to Zone 5 has a predefined rate of $50 for a package size. What is the freight?**\nFreight = rate for zone pair = **$50**.\n\n**Q6. Freight class (LTL density): a shipment weighs 500 lb and measures 4 ft × 4 ft × 4 ft. Find the density.**\nDensity = weight ÷ (L × W × H) = 500 ÷ (4 × 4 × 4) = 500 ÷ 64 = **7.81 lb per cubic foot**. The NMFC freight class (50–500) is then read from a density table — 7.81 lb/ft³ falls in the 6–8 lb/ft³ band, which is **class 175** on the standard 13-band NMFC density scale (8–10 lb/ft³ would be class 125). Lower density → higher class → higher rate.\n\n---\n\n## B. Ocean freight calculation examples (PPT) 📘\n\n**Q7. Volumetric weight: total cargo volume is 30 m³ and the carrier's conversion factor is 200 kg/m³. Find the volumetric weight.**\nVolumetric weight = volume × conversion factor = 30 m³ × 200 kg/m³ = **6,000 kg**. The chargeable weight is the greater of this and the actual weight.\n\n**Q8. Base freight: cargo volume is 125 m³ and the carrier's base rate is $50 per cubic metre or ton. Find the base freight.**\nBase freight = 125 × $50 = **$6,250**. (Ocean conversion: 1 m³ = 1,000 kg = 1 ton, so the rate applies per m³ or per tonne, whichever gives more revenue tons.)\n\n**Q9. Canada → US example: an importer ships 50 packages; total weight 5,000 kg; volume 20 m³; base rate $4 per kg; conversion factor 200 kg/m³. Calculate the freight cost.** ⭐⭐⭐\n- Step 1: Volumetric weight = 20 m³ × 200 kg/m³ = **4,000 kg**.\n- Step 2: Chargeable weight = max(actual 5,000 kg, volumetric 4,000 kg) = **5,000 kg** (actual is higher).\n- Step 3: Freight = 5,000 kg × $4/kg = **$20,000** (no surcharges or additional costs assumed).\n\n**Q10. Step-by-step FCL example: a 40-ft container of garments from Shanghai to Los Angeles, March 2025. Build the total estimated freight.** ⭐⭐⭐\n| Cost component | Working | Amount (USD) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Base ocean freight (Shanghai–LA) | given | 2,100 |\n| BAF | given | 180 |\n| CAF | 5% × (base + BAF) = 5% × (2,100 + 180) = 5% × 2,280 | 114 |\n| THC at origin (Shanghai) | given | 145 |\n| THC at destination (LA) | given | 190 |\n| Documentation / B/L fee | given | 75 |\n| ISPS security charge | given | 12 |\n| PSS (seasonal) | given | 150 |\n| **Total estimated freight** | 2,100 + 180 + 114 + 145 + 190 + 75 + 12 + 150 | **$2,966** |\nNot included: customs duties, inland haulage, demurrage, warehousing. Note that surcharges and handling add **$866, i.e. 41%** on top of the base rate, and the base rate is only **71%** of the total.\n\n**Q11. Comparing two routes: Case 1 — India → UAE (short haul), FCL 20' ceramic tiles Mumbai → Jebel Ali, base $500, total $820. Case 2 — Vietnam → Germany (long haul with transhipment), FCL 40' electronics, base $2,900, transhipment (Singapore) $220, green surcharge $90, total $3,860. Analyse.** ⭐⭐\n- Case 1: non-base charges = 820 − 500 = **$320** (64% on top of base; base = 61% of total).\n- Case 2: listed extras = 220 + 90 = $310 → base + extras = $3,210; other charges (BAF, THCs, docs, etc.) = 3,860 − 3,210 = **$650**. Total extras = 3,860 − 2,900 = $960 (33% on top of base; base = 75% of total).\n- Lesson (PPT): **transit time, routing (transhipment) and port fees** can change the bottom line dramatically; on short routes fixed port/handling costs dominate, on long routes base freight dominates. Per-TEU, Case 2 costs $1,930/TEU vs $820/TEU for Case 1.\n\n**Q12. Air vs sea rule of thumb: a shipment is 0.4 m³ and weighs 80 kg. Which mode is likely cheaper and why?**\nBelow **0.5 m³ and 100 kg** air may be competitive, because **sea freight has a minimum chargeable volume of 1 m³** — this shipment pays for 1 m³ by sea, while by air it pays for max(80, 0.4 × 167 = 66.8) = **80 kg**. Above 0.5 m³ and 100 kg, sea is generally cheaper (but up to 43 days from China).\n\n---\n\n## C. Air freight volumetric examples 📘\n\n**Q13. PPT example: 1 m³ of goods weighs 100 kg. What is the chargeable weight by air? What if it weighs 500 kg?**\nVolumetric weight = 1,000,000 cm³ ÷ 6,000 = 166.67 → **167 kg**. Actual 100 kg < 167 kg → chargeable **167 kg**. If the 1 m³ weighs 500 kg → actual weight is higher → chargeable **500 kg**.\n\n**Q14. Air security & cost PDF example: a piece weighs 150 kg and measures 120 cm × 80 cm × 50 cm. Find the volumetric weight and chargeable weight.** ⭐⭐⭐\n- Volume = 1.2 m × 0.80 m × 0.50 m = **0.48 m³**.\n- Volumetric weight = 0.48 × 167 = 80.16 ≈ **80 kg**; or 120 × 80 × 50 ÷ 6,000 = 480,000 ÷ 6,000 = **80 kg**.\n- Chargeable weight = max(150, 80) = **150 kg** — the actual weight exceeds the volume equivalent.\n- Freight = 150 kg × agreed tariff rate per kg for that weight break (origin → destination), plus surcharges (fuel, war risk) and the origin/destination charges listed in the PDF.\n\n**Q15. Air carrier liability (PDF): the Montreal Convention limits liability to 22 SDR/kg, and 1 SDR = US$1.43493. What is the limit per kg in US$?**\n22 × 1.43493 = **US$31.57 per kg** (US$31.56846). For the 150 kg piece in Q14 the carrier's maximum liability = 150 × 31.56846 = **US$4,735.27**, which may be far below the cargo's value — hence **cargo insurance** on a small percentage of declared value, ideally door-to-door. (Note: from 28 Dec 2019 the Montreal limit was revised to 22 SDR; it is revised every five years and rose to 26 SDR from Dec 2024.)\n\n---\n\n## D. Container capacity checks (Containerization PPT data) 📘\n\n**Q16. How many cartons of 60 cm × 40 cm × 40 cm can theoretically fit in a 20-ft container (internal 5.9 m × 2.35 m × 2.4 m)?**\nCarton volume = 0.6 × 0.4 × 0.4 = 0.096 m³. By dimensions: 5.9 ÷ 0.6 = 9 (9.83), 2.35 ÷ 0.4 = 5 (5.875), 2.4 ÷ 0.4 = 6 → 9 × 5 × 6 = **270 cartons** (25.9 m³). Rotating (length 0.4 along 5.9 m: 14; width 0.6 across 2.35: 3; height 6) = 14 × 3 × 6 = 252. Best simple layout = **270**. By volume alone 33.2 ÷ 0.096 = 345, so stowage factor ≈ 78%. Check payload too: if a carton weighs 120 kg, 270 × 120 = 32,400 kg > 28,180 kg → **weight-limited** to 234 cartons. *(practice built on PPT container data)*\n\n---\n\n## E. Practice problems (not from the zip — same methods) \n\n**Q17. Air: 3 cartons, each 60 × 50 × 40 cm and 18 kg. Rate $4.20/kg; fuel surcharge $0.80/kg on chargeable weight. Find the charge.**\nVolumetric per carton = 60 × 50 × 40 ÷ 6,000 = 120,000 ÷ 6,000 = 20 kg → total 60 kg. Actual = 3 × 18 = 54 kg. Chargeable = **60 kg**. Freight = 60 × 4.20 = $252; FSC = 60 × 0.80 = $48; **total $300**.\n\n**Q18. Ocean LCL (W/M): a shipment of 12 m³ weighing 4,500 kg; rate $60 per revenue ton. Find freight. What if it were 6 m³ weighing 8,000 kg?**\nRevenue tons = max(12 m³, 4.5 t) = 12 → freight = 12 × 60 = **$720** (volume-based). Second case: max(6, 8) = 8 RT → 8 × 60 = **$480** (weight-based).\n\n**Q19. Cylinder pricing rule (Air security PDF): a drum 40 cm in diameter and 120 cm long weighing 25 kg. Find the chargeable weight.**\nUnusual shapes are charged on the **box they fit in**, not the true volume: 40 × 40 × 120 = 192,000 cm³ ÷ 6,000 = **32 kg** > 25 kg → chargeable **32 kg**. (True cylinder volume would give only 25.1 kg — the rectilinear rule costs the shipper 7 kg more.)\n\n**Q20. Rounding rule: a consignment's volumetric weight is 80.16 kg and actual weight 78 kg. What is the chargeable weight?**\nChargeable weight is always **rounded up to the next half kilogram** → 80.16 → **80.5 kg**.\n\n**Q21. Road FTL vs LTL: LTL rate ₹2,000 per pallet; FTL (33 pallets) ₹45,000. At how many pallets does FTL become cheaper?**\nBreak-even = 45,000 ÷ 2,000 = 22.5 → from **23 pallets** FTL is cheaper (and avoids hub handling and trans-shipment).\n\n**Q22. Inches DIM for the Vibrant Video speaker carton (30 × 18 × 18 in, 28 lb) — domestic factor 139.**\n30 × 18 × 18 = 9,720 in³ ÷ 139 = **69.9 lb** DIM vs 28 lb actual → parcel carriers would bill ≈ 70 lb: bulky, light goods are priced on space, which is why the KPI case uses LTL/TL rather than parcel service.\n"}, {"slug": "p6-kpi-book-cases", "label": "KPI Book Cases", "tag": "P6", "group": "focus", "title": "KPI Book Cases — Vibrant Video & Bob's Custom BBQs (Full Solutions)", "md": "# KPI Book Cases — Vibrant Video & Bob's Custom BBQs (Full Solutions)\nThe two **exact book cases** in *KPIs book cases.pdf* 📘 (Case 11.1 and 11.2, source: Brian J. Gibson, Ph.D.), with every calculation worked out. Use the tables in [Transportation KPIs, Freight Terms & Scorecards](ch08-transportation-kpis-scorecards.md) — especially **Table 11.4** for the FOB terms. The book prints \"speaker\" twice in questions 2–5 and \"SSE\" in question 6; read Q4–Q5 as the **receivers** and Q6 as **V²** (the textbook's typos).\n\n---\n\n## Case 11.1 — Vibrant Video ⭐⭐⭐\nVibrant Video (V²) makes home theatre projection systems. It assembles Ultra HD projectors in **Portland, Oregon** and sells them online and through specialty electronics stores. The most popular model retails for **$4,995**, and V² bundles the projector with high-fidelity audio components bought from external suppliers. Because of delivery problems with Pacific Rim suppliers and carriers, V² will buy from suppliers closer to Portland: **speakers** from an electronics company in **Tijuana, Mexico**, and **receivers** from an audio lab in **Manchester, New Hampshire**. The speaker supplier offers two shipping choices under **FOB Destination, Freight Collect and Allowed**. The receiver supplier sells only **FOB Origin, Freight Collect**. The transportation director must evaluate the delivery options and decide.\n\n| | Speakers | Receiver |\n|---|---|---|\n| Manufactured in | Tijuana, Mexico | Manchester, New Hampshire |\n| V² purchase price | **$175 per set** | **$225 per unit** |\n| Weight | 28 pounds | 5 pounds |\n| Dimensions | 30\" L × 18\" W × 18\" H | 18\" L × 8\" W × 4\" H |\n| Characteristics | Sturdy, bulky, not easily damaged | Compact, vibration sensitive, theft risk |\n| Freight terms | FOB Destination, Freight Collect and Allowed | FOB Origin, Freight Collect |\n| **Option 1** | Weekly **LTL** delivery, **200 units**, **$2,485** per delivery | Weekly **ground** delivery, **200 units**, **$2,169** per delivery |\n| **Option 2** | Twice-per-month **TL** delivery, **400 units**, **$2,946** per delivery | Twice-per-week **airfreight** delivery, **100 units**, **$2,411** per delivery |\n\n**Q1. What responsibilities, control and costs does V² bear under each of the FOB terms offered?**\nUsing Table 11.4:\n\n| | Speakers — **FOB Destination, Freight Collect & Allowed** | Receivers — **FOB Origin, Freight Collect** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Owns goods in transit | **Supplier (seller)** — title and risk pass at V²'s dock in Portland | **V² (buyer)** — title and risk pass at the Manchester shipping point |\n| Files freight claims (loss/damage) | Supplier | **V²** |\n| Selects and pays the carrier | **V²** (freight collect) — V² **controls** mode, carrier and schedule | **V²** — full control |\n| Ultimately bears freight cost | **Supplier** — V² pays the carrier, then **deducts the freight from its payment** to the supplier (\"allowed\") | **V²** |\n| Best when | Buyer has greater influence with carriers | Buyer has greater influence with carriers |\n**In short:** for speakers V² has **control without risk or (ultimate) cost** — it chooses and pays the carrier, the supplier owns the goods and handles claims, and the freight is recovered by deduction. For receivers V² has **full control, full risk and full cost** — it owns the goods from Manchester, files any claims (important for a theft-prone, vibration-sensitive product) and pays all freight. V² should insure the receivers in transit.\n\n**Q2. What is the delivery cost and landed cost per unit for each speaker delivery option?**\nDelivery cost per unit = cost per delivery ÷ units per delivery; landed cost per unit = purchase price + delivery cost per unit.\n\n| Speakers | Option 1 — weekly LTL | Option 2 — twice-monthly TL |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cost per delivery | $2,485 | $2,946 |\n| Units per delivery | 200 | 400 |\n| **Delivery cost per unit** | 2,485 ÷ 200 = **$12.43** ($12.425) | 2,946 ÷ 400 = **$7.37** ($7.365) |\n| **Landed cost per unit** | 175 + 12.425 = **$187.43** | 175 + 7.365 = **$182.37** |\n| Deliveries per year | 52 | 24 |\n| Annual freight / units | 52 × 2,485 = $129,220 for 10,400 units | 24 × 2,946 = $70,704 for 9,600 units |\nOption 2 saves **$5.06 per unit** (≈ $52,600 a year on 10,400 units). Because the freight is **allowed** (deducted from the supplier's invoice), V²'s net outlay per speaker is the $175 price either way in the short run; but the landed cost is still the right basis for the decision — freight is a real supply chain cost the supplier will build into future prices, and V² pays it up front.\n\n**Q3. Which delivery option do you recommend for the speakers?**\n**Option 2 — twice-per-month truckload.**\n- Lowest delivery and landed cost: $7.37 vs $12.43 per unit (−41%).\n- Speakers are **sturdy, bulky and not easily damaged**, so the slower, less frequent TL service carries little damage risk; TL also avoids LTL terminal handling (less damage, faster, more reliable transit from Tijuana).\n- Bulky product → LTL rates rise with cube; a full truck uses the space efficiently.\n- Trade-off: larger order size raises average cycle inventory from 100 to 200 sets (200 ÷ 2 vs 400 ÷ 2). Extra 100 sets × $175 = $17,500 of inventory; at a **25% carrying-cost rate (assumption)** ≈ $4,375 a year — far less than the ≈ $52,000 freight saving. Check warehouse space for bulky cartons (each 30 × 18 × 18 in = 5.6 ft³; 400 sets ≈ 2,250 ft³).\n- Cross-border Mexico → US: TL gives one customs crossing per load and simpler security (C-TPAT).\n\n**Q4. What is the delivery cost and landed cost per unit for each receiver delivery option?**\n| Receivers | Option 1 — weekly ground | Option 2 — twice-weekly airfreight |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cost per delivery | $2,169 | $2,411 |\n| Units per delivery | 200 | 100 |\n| **Delivery cost per unit** | 2,169 ÷ 200 = **$10.85** ($10.845) | 2,411 ÷ 100 = **$24.11** |\n| **Landed cost per unit** | 225 + 10.845 = **$235.85** | 225 + 24.11 = **$249.11** |\n| Deliveries per year | 52 | 104 |\n| Annual freight (10,400 units) | 52 × 2,169 = **$112,788** | 104 × 2,411 = **$250,744** |\nOption 1 saves **$13.27 per unit**, about **$137,956 a year**.\n\n**Q5. Which delivery option do you recommend for the receivers?**\n**Option 1 — weekly ground delivery**, with safeguards.\n- Landed cost $235.85 vs $249.11: airfreight more than doubles the freight bill for the same annual volume.\n- Under FOB Origin, Freight Collect, V² bears the **risk** — so manage the receiver's weaknesses (vibration sensitivity, theft risk) through **protective packaging** (foam, shock indicators), a **carrier with a good loss-and-damage record**, sealed/secure trailers, tracking, and **cargo insurance**.\n- Inventory: weekly lots of 200 give average stock of 100 units ($22,500) vs 50 ($11,250) with air; extra carrying cost at 25% ≈ $2,800 a year — tiny vs the $138,000 freight saving. Receivers are **compact**, so holding a week's stock needs little space.\n- Choose air (Option 2) only if damage/theft by ground proves high (each lost receiver costs $225 plus lost projector sales), or for urgent replenishment — a hybrid with **expedited air as a backup** is sensible.\n\n**Q6. What other supply chain issues and costs must V² take into consideration when making these transportation decisions?**\n- **Inventory carrying cost and storage space** (order size vs frequency) and the **total cost** view (freight + inventory + handling + loss/damage).\n- **Transit time and reliability** (variability) and their effect on **safety stock** and assembly schedules; matching speaker and receiver arrivals to projector assembly (bundles need all three).\n- **Loss, damage and theft** risk and claims administration (V² files claims for receivers); **insurance**.\n- **Supplier relationships and freight terms** — the \"allowed\" arrangement could change; negotiate FOB terms and freight allowances.\n- **Cross-border issues** for Tijuana: customs brokerage, USMCA origin rules, duties, C-TPAT security, border delays, currency (MXN).\n- **Carrier selection and performance** — use a scorecard (on-time, damage, billing accuracy) and contract rates; fuel surcharges.\n- **Demand variability and forecasting**; customer service levels online and in stores; stock-out costs (a missing $175 speaker blocks a $4,995 sale).\n- **Packaging, handling and receiving** labour at Portland (TL vs LTL unloading).\n- **Sustainability** — TL and ground produce far less CO₂ than LTL multi-stop routes and air.\n- **Administrative costs** — number of deliveries to schedule, receive, invoice and pay (52 + 24 vs 52 + 104 transactions).\n\n---\n\n## Case 11.2 — Bob's Custom BBQs ⭐⭐⭐\nBob's Custom BBQs, a manufacturer of outdoor grills, uses three primary carriers to move grills from its Texas factory to home improvement retailers in the United States and Canada. The owner, **Bob Flambeau**, wants to evaluate the three carriers over a three-month period.\n\n| Performance criteria | Allied Transport | Bestway Freight | Certaint Carriers |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| On-time delivery | 99.5% | 98.7% | 98.2% |\n| Loads with damage claims | 0.9% | 1.6% | 0.4% |\n| Customer satisfaction ratings | 4.6 | 4.2 | 3.9 |\n| Billing accuracy | 99.3% | 99.6% | 98.2% |\n| Loads rejected | 1.3% | 2.1% | 0.9% |\n\nThe transportation manager's scorecard:\n| Performance criteria | Weight factor | Performance evaluation | Potential score |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| On-time delivery | 30 | >98.5% = 5; 96.01–98.5% = 4; 93–96% = 2; <93% = 0 | 150 |\n| Loads with damage claims | 30 | <0.5% = 5; 0.5–1% = 4; 1.01–2% = 2; >2% = 0 | 150 |\n| Customer satisfaction ratings | 20 | >4.5 = 5; 4.01–4.5 = 4; 3–4 = 2; <3 = 0 | 100 |\n| Billing accuracy | 10 | >99% = 5; 97.01–99% = 4; 95–97% = 2; <95% = 0 | 50 |\n| Loads rejected | 10 | <1% = 5; 1%–2% = 4; 2.01–3% = 2; >3% = 0 | 50 |\n| **Total** | 100 | | **500** |\n\n**Q7. Calculate the performance score for each of the three carriers.**\nCategory score = weight × points.\n\n| Criteria (weight) | Allied Transport | Bestway Freight | Certaint Carriers |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| On-time delivery (30) | 99.5% > 98.5% → 5 → **150** | 98.7% > 98.5% → 5 → **150** | 98.2% (96.01–98.5) → 4 → **120** |\n| Damage claims (30) | 0.9% (0.5–1) → 4 → **120** | 1.6% (1.01–2) → 2 → **60** | 0.4% (< 0.5) → 5 → **150** |\n| Customer satisfaction (20) | 4.6 > 4.5 → 5 → **100** | 4.2 (4.01–4.5) → 4 → **80** | 3.9 (3–4) → 2 → **40** |\n| Billing accuracy (10) | 99.3% > 99 → 5 → **50** | 99.6% > 99 → 5 → **50** | 98.2% (97.01–99) → 4 → **40** |\n| Loads rejected (10) | 1.3% (1–2) → 4 → **40** | 2.1% (2.01–3) → 2 → **20** | 0.9% (< 1) → 5 → **50** |\n| **Total (out of 500)** | **460 (92%)** | **360 (72%)** | **400 (80%)** |\nRanking: **Allied 460 > Certaint 400 > Bestway 360.**\n\n**Q8. Which carrier would you recommend that Bob consider for elimination? Why?**\n**Bestway Freight (360/500).** It has the lowest total score and is worst on the two most damaging operational metrics after on-time delivery: **damage claims 1.6%** (4× Certaint's rate; weight 30) and **loads rejected 2.1%** (the highest; it refuses Bob's loads, forcing re-tendering at higher cost). Its customer satisfaction (4.2) is also middling. Its strengths (on-time 98.7%, billing 99.6%) do not offset damage, which hurts retailers and Bob's brand. Before eliminating, Bob should share the scorecard and check volumes/lanes — but on this evidence Bestway's freight should move to Allied and Certaint. (Allied is the best all-round carrier; Certaint is the safest for damage.)\n\n**Q9. If Bob decides to keep all three carriers, what should each of them work to improve?**\n- **Allied Transport (460)** — **damage claims** (0.9% → below 0.5% for full points: better load securement, handling training, equipment) and **loads rejected** (1.3% → below 1%: commit capacity, honour tenders via a contract/dedicated capacity).\n- **Bestway Freight (360)** — **damage claims** (1.6% is serious: root-cause analysis, packaging/blocking & bracing, driver training, equipment), **loads rejected** (2.1%: capacity planning and tender acceptance), and **customer satisfaction** (4.2 → above 4.5: communication, appointment compliance at retailers).\n- **Certaint Carriers (400)** — **customer satisfaction** (3.9 is the lowest: driver courtesy, delivery appointments, proactive communication, proof of delivery), **on-time delivery** (98.2% → above 98.5%) and **billing accuracy** (98.2% → above 99%: audit invoices, EDI 210 electronic billing).\nBob should hold quarterly reviews, set targets and reward improvement with more freight volume.\n\n---\n\n## Pre-read worked example — Table 11.8 scorecard ⭐⭐\n**Q10. Using Table 11.8, show how the carrier's total score of 405 is obtained and interpret it.**\nOn-time delivery: 96.7% falls in 96.01–98% → 4 points × weight 35 = **140**. Loss & damage: 0.6% in 0.5–1% → 4 × 30 = **120**. Billing accuracy: 98.1% in 97–99% → 3 × 15 = **45**. Equipment condition: acceptable (safe & clean) → 5 × 5 = **25**. Customer service: outstanding survey feedback → superior 5 × 15 = **75**. **Total = 140 + 120 + 45 + 25 + 75 = 405** of a possible 500 (81%). The carrier is good but should improve **on-time delivery** (to > 98%) and **billing accuracy** (to > 99%), where it loses 35 and 30 points respectively.\n"}, {"slug": "ch01-outsourcing-contracts", "label": "Outsourcing & Contracts", "tag": "01", "group": "chapters", "title": "Outsourcing, Contracts & Logistics Partnerships", "md": "# Outsourcing, Contracts & Logistics Partnerships\nExam topic 1 of 8 📘. The course file (\"KPIs, outsourcing and contract problems\") is a problem set of 13 questions, so the paper will mostly ask you to **apply** the ideas below to a short case: weighted vendor scoring, risk matrices, SLAs, hidden costs, contract clauses, negotiation (BATNA), sourcing strategy, ethics, force majeure and the financial case for outsourcing. This page gives you the theory each problem needs; the full worked answers are on [Outsourcing & Contract Problems](p1-outsourcing-contract-problems.md).\n\n---\n\n## 1. What outsourcing is ⭐⭐⭐\n**Outsourcing** is contracting an external provider to perform an activity (a process, a service or the manufacture of a component) that the firm could do, or used to do, in-house. Ownership of the *activity* moves out of the firm; accountability for the *outcome* to the customer does not.\n\n**Offshoring** is moving an activity to another country. It is about **location**, not ownership.\n\n| | Same country | Another country |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Done in-house (own entity)** | In-house / insourcing | **Captive offshoring** (own subsidiary abroad, e.g. own development centre in Vietnam) |\n| **Done by a third party** | **Domestic (onshore) outsourcing** (e.g. hire another Indian IT firm) | **Offshore outsourcing** (third party abroad) |\n\n- **Nearshoring**: moving work to a nearby country (same time zone/region), e.g. US → Mexico, Western Europe → Poland. Shorter lead time, lower freight, lower geopolitical and currency risk, higher unit cost than far-shore.\n- **Reshoring / backshoring**: bringing work back home. **Friend-shoring**: sourcing from politically aligned countries (China+1).\n- **Insourcing**: bringing a previously outsourced activity back in-house.\n\n### Why firms outsource ⭐⭐\n1. **Cost** — labour arbitrage, supplier's economies of scale, variable instead of fixed cost.\n2. **Focus on core competence** (Prahalad & Hamel) — keep what differentiates you, outsource the rest.\n3. **Access to capability and technology** that the firm lacks (cold chain, WMS, customs expertise).\n4. **Flexibility / scalability** — capacity up or down with demand (peak season).\n5. **Speed to market** and **global reach** (3PL networks, freight forwarders with offices worldwide).\n6. **Risk sharing** — the provider carries assets and some operational risk.\n7. **Capital release** — no investment in warehouses, trucks, IT (asset-light).\n\n### Risks and disadvantages ⭐⭐\nLoss of control and visibility; **hidden costs** (see §5); quality failure; IP leakage; dependence/lock-in; communication, culture and time-zone gaps; regulatory non-compliance; currency and political risk; reputational/ethical risk (child labour, unsafe factories); hollowing-out of skills; transition disruption; supplier failure.\n\n### Make-or-buy (in-house vs outsource) decision factors\nStrategic importance (core vs non-core), total cost (not just price), capability and capacity, quality, control of IP, volume stability, supplier market (number of capable suppliers), risk, time to implement, and reversibility.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Outsourcing in logistics: 1PL to 5PL ⭐⭐\n| Level | Who | What they do |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1PL** | The shipper itself | Own trucks and warehouses |\n| **2PL** | Asset-based carrier | Moves goods: shipping line, airline, trucking company, railway |\n| **3PL** | Third-party logistics provider | Integrated, contract-based services: warehousing, transport, freight forwarding, customs, value-added services (kitting, labelling). E.g. DHL Supply Chain, TCI, Mahindra Logistics |\n| **4PL** | Lead logistics provider / integrator | Manages the whole supply chain and the 3PLs on the client's behalf; usually non-asset, information and management-led (Accenture concept) |\n| **5PL** | Network aggregator | Plans and runs the supply network for e-commerce/multiple clients, aggregating demand across chains |\n\nOther logistics intermediaries: **freight forwarder** (arranges carriage, documents, consolidation), **NVOCC** (issues own bill of lading, buys space from lines), **customs broker / CHA**, **IATA cargo agent** (issues HAWB).\n\n---\n\n## 3. The outsourcing process (life cycle) ⭐⭐\n1. **Strategic assessment** — what to outsource and why; build the business case (TCO, risk).\n2. **Specification** — scope of work, service levels, volumes, KPIs.\n3. **Supplier search & shortlisting** — RFI → RFP/RFQ.\n4. **Supplier evaluation & selection** — weighted scoring, site audits, references.\n5. **Negotiation & contracting** — price, SLA, penalties/incentives, clauses.\n6. **Transition** — knowledge transfer, IT integration, parallel run.\n7. **Relationship & performance management** — KPIs, scorecards, governance meetings, audits.\n8. **Renewal, re-tender or exit** — exit plan, re-insourcing.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Supplier / vendor selection: the weighted scoring model ⭐⭐⭐\nSteps (used in Problem Q1 and Q9):\n1. Choose criteria (cost, quality, delivery, technology, sustainability, risk…).\n2. Assign **weights** that add up to 100% (or 100 points) to reflect strategy.\n3. Rate each supplier on each criterion (e.g. 1–10).\n4. **Weighted score = Σ (weight × rating)**; divide by 100 if weights are in points.\n5. Rank; choose the highest; run a **sensitivity analysis** (change a weight and see if the ranking flips).\n\n> When one weight increases, the others must be re-scaled so that the total stays 100. Either reduce them **proportionally** or take the increase from one named criterion; state which you did.\n\nOther selection tools: **Total Cost of Ownership (TCO)**, **total landed cost** (price + freight + duty/tariff + insurance + inventory in transit + quality cost), **AHP** (pairwise comparison), **categorical/rating method**, **Kraljic matrix** (profit impact × supply risk → leverage, strategic, bottleneck, non-critical items).\n\n### Total landed cost ⭐⭐⭐\n**Landed cost per unit = unit price + freight + insurance + duties/tariffs + customs/clearance + handling + in-transit inventory carrying cost + cost of quality (defects, rework)**\n\nIn-transit carrying cost ≈ unit value × carrying-cost rate × (lead time ÷ 365). A low unit price far away can lose to a nearshore supplier once tariff, lead time, quality and risk are priced in.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Hidden costs of outsourcing ⭐⭐⭐\nCosts that are not in the supplier's contract price but arise *because* you outsourced:\n- **Search and selection** — RFP, supplier audits, travel.\n- **Transition** — knowledge transfer, parallel running, severance, ERP/EDI integration.\n- **Governance / contract management** — vendor managers, review meetings, legal.\n- **Quality assurance** — incoming inspection, audits, rework, returns.\n- **Coordination & communication** — travel, time-zone overlap, translation.\n- **Risk costs** — buffer inventory, expediting, dual sourcing, insurance.\n- **Exit / switching** costs.\n\nWhy they are ignored: they are spread across many departments' budgets; they occur after the decision; procurement is rewarded on price; they are hard to estimate; vendors' proposals only show price. **Total outsourcing cost = contract value + hidden costs**; hidden costs of 5–15% of contract value are common.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Outsourcing risk assessment: the risk matrix ⭐⭐⭐\n**Risk score = Probability × Impact** (each typically rated 1–5). Plot on a 5×5 heat map:\n- **High (15–25)** — red: mitigate immediately, possibly avoid.\n- **Medium (8–14)** — amber: mitigate and monitor.\n- **Low (1–7)** — green: accept and monitor.\n\nFour responses (the **4 Ts**): **Treat/mitigate** (controls, audits, dual sourcing), **Transfer** (contract clauses, insurance, hedging), **Tolerate/accept**, **Terminate/avoid** (don't outsource that activity).\n\nWhat can be **transferred by contract**: quality failure (warranty, rejection & replacement at supplier's cost, liquidated damages), delay (LDs, penalty), regulatory non-compliance (compliance warranty + indemnity), IP leakage (NDA, IP ownership & indemnity clause, audit rights). Currency risk is transferred with a **currency clause** (price in buyer's currency, or a band/adjustment) or hedged with forwards. A strike is usually **force majeure** — transferred back to the buyer unless the contract excludes it, so mitigate with dual sourcing.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Service Level Agreements (SLAs) and KPIs ⭐⭐⭐\nAn **SLA** is the part of the contract that defines the measurable level of service, how it is measured, and what happens if it is missed or beaten. A good SLA element has:\n1. **KPI and definition** (formula, what counts).\n2. **Target** (and sometimes a minimum floor).\n3. **Measurement method** — data source, frequency, who measures (WMS report, audit, cycle count, customer portal).\n4. **Reporting** — monthly dashboard, quarterly business review.\n5. **Penalty / service credit** for missing the target (e.g. 1% of monthly fee per 0.1% shortfall, capped).\n6. **Incentive / bonus** for exceeding it (gain-share).\n7. **Exclusions** (force majeure, buyer-caused delays) and **escalation**.\n\nGood KPIs are **SMART** (specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, time-bound) and few (5–10). Balance **cost, quality, time, flexibility, safety, customer**.\n\nCommon warehouse/transport KPIs (formulas):\n| KPI | Formula | Typical target |\n|---|---|---|\n| Order accuracy | Correct orders ÷ total orders shipped × 100 | ≥ 99–99.5% |\n| Inventory accuracy | Correct records ÷ records counted × 100 | ≥ 99.5% |\n| Damage rate | Damaged orders ÷ orders shipped × 100 | ≤ 0.5% |\n| Complaint rate | Complaints ÷ orders × 100 | ≤ 0.2% |\n| Order fulfilment (dock-to-ship) time | Average hours from order release to dispatch | ≤ 24 h |\n| On-time delivery | On-time deliveries ÷ total deliveries | ≥ 95–98% |\n| OTIF | Orders on time and in full ÷ total orders | ≥ 95% |\n| Perfect order | % orders on time × in full × damage-free × correct documents | ≥ 90–95% |\n| Safety (LTIFR) | Lost-time injuries × 1,000,000 ÷ hours worked | → 0 |\n\nSee also [Transportation KPIs & Scorecards](ch08-transportation-kpis-scorecards.md).\n\n---\n\n## 8. The outsourcing contract: key clauses ⭐⭐⭐\n| Clause | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| **Scope of work / statement of work** | Exactly what will be delivered |\n| **Price & payment terms** | Rates, indexation (fuel, currency), invoicing, payment days |\n| **SLA & KPIs** | Targets, measurement, service credits, bonuses |\n| **Liquidated damages (LDs)** | A **pre-agreed, genuine pre-estimate** of loss payable for a defined breach (e.g. ₹X per day of delay). No need to prove actual loss; must not be a \"penalty\" (in India: Indian Contract Act s.74 — reasonable compensation up to the named amount) |\n| **Force majeure** | Excuses a party from performance (usually suspension, not damages) when an event **beyond its reasonable control**, unforeseeable and unavoidable (war, natural disaster, epidemic, government lockdown, sometimes strikes) prevents performance; requires **prompt notice**, proof and mitigation; often allows termination if it lasts > X days |\n| **Termination** | For cause (material breach not cured within a **cure period**, insolvency, repeated SLA failure), for convenience (with notice and fee), and on prolonged force majeure |\n| **Warranties & indemnities** | Supplier promises quality/compliance; indemnifies buyer for third-party claims, IP infringement, regulatory fines |\n| **Limitation of liability** | Caps damages (e.g. 12 months' fees); excludes indirect loss |\n| **Confidentiality / NDA & IP** | Ownership of designs, data, improvements; no reuse |\n| **Audit rights** | Buyer can inspect sites, books, compliance (quality, ESG, labour) |\n| **Code of conduct / ESG clause** | No child or forced labour, safety, environment, anti-bribery; breach is material |\n| **Business continuity / disaster recovery** | Backup sites, dual sourcing, inventory buffers |\n| **Change control** | How scope and price changes are agreed |\n| **Governance & escalation** | Review meetings, escalation ladder |\n| **Dispute resolution & governing law** | Negotiation → mediation → arbitration (e.g. Arbitration & Conciliation Act 1996, SIAC/ICC), seat and law |\n| **Exit / transition-out** | Hand-back of data, assets, staff; transition support |\n| **Step-in rights** | Buyer (or nominee) can take over the service temporarily if the supplier fails |\n| **Insurance** | Supplier carries goods-in-transit, liability, cargo insurance |\n\n### Breach of contract: what you need before claiming damages\nProof of the **contract term**; proof of the **breach** (delivery records, GRNs, emails, ASN vs receipt); proof of **notice** to the supplier and opportunity to cure; proof of **loss** and **causation** (downtime logs, emergency purchase invoices, customer penalty notices); evidence you **mitigated** the loss; and that no force-majeure or buyer-caused excuse applies. Remedies: LDs or damages (Hadley v Baxendale — losses arising naturally or in the parties' contemplation), specific performance (rare), termination.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Negotiation: BATNA, ZOPA and trade-offs ⭐⭐\n- **BATNA** (Best Alternative To a Negotiated Agreement — Fisher & Ury, *Getting to Yes*): what you will do if this deal fails. The stronger your BATNA, the more power you have.\n- **Reservation price (walk-away point)**: worst acceptable term.\n- **ZOPA** (zone of possible agreement): overlap between the two reservation prices.\n- **Principled negotiation**: separate people from the problem; focus on **interests, not positions**; invent options for mutual gain; insist on objective criteria.\n- **Trade-offs / logrolling**: give on what you value less (e.g. volume commitment) to get what you value more (price, speed). Typical levers in freight contracts: price, transit time, volume commitment, contract length, payment terms, fuel/BAF indexation, penalties and bonuses, equipment guarantee, peak-season capacity.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Sourcing strategies: single vs multiple ⭐⭐\n| | Single sourcing | Multi-sourcing (dual/triple) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cost | Lowest (volume discounts, one relationship) | Higher (split volumes, more management) |\n| Quality | Consistent, deep collaboration | Variation between suppliers |\n| Relationship | Strategic partnership, joint development | Competitive, arm's-length |\n| Risk | **High** — single point of failure (earthquake, fire, strike, sanctions) | **Low** — disruption at one supplier covered by others |\n| Flexibility | Limited by one supplier's capacity | Flexible capacity |\n\nHybrids: **dual sourcing** (e.g. 70/30 split), **parallel sourcing** (different parts single-sourced to different suppliers who can back each other up), single supplier with **multiple plants in different countries**. **Resilience** = ability to absorb and recover from disruption; multi-sourcing, buffers, nearshoring, visibility and flexible contracts build it. Classic lessons: Toyota/Aisin brake-valve fire (1997), Ericsson vs Nokia after the Philips Albuquerque fire (2000), Japan earthquake 2011 (Renesas chips), COVID-19 (2020–21).\n\n---\n\n## 11. Ethics and sustainability in outsourcing ⭐⭐\nThe buyer is responsible for its supply chain even when work is outsourced (Rana Plaza 2013, Nike sweatshops 1990s, cobalt mining). Tools: **supplier code of conduct**, **ESG clauses**, **audit rights** (announced and unannounced, third-party SA8000/SMETA/WRAP audits), **corrective action plans (CAPs)**, **supplier development** (training, capability building, fair pricing so the supplier can comply), **grievance mechanisms**, and **traceability**. Frameworks: UN Guiding Principles on Business & Human Rights, ILO conventions (C138 minimum age, C182 worst forms of child labour), India's Child and Adolescent Labour Act 1986 (as amended 2016), EU CSDDD, the Indian BRSR reporting. Immediate termination can push children into worse work; responsible exit or **remediation** (remove child safely, pay schooling, employ an adult family member) is the recommended path unless the supplier refuses to cooperate.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Outsourcing financial evaluation ⭐⭐⭐\n- **First-year cost of outsourcing** = outsourcing price + one-off **transition cost** + annual **contract management** (governance) cost − any productivity savings in year 1.\n- **Annual saving (steady state)** = current in-house cost − (outsourcing price + contract management) (+ productivity gain).\n- **Payback period** = one-off investment (transition cost) ÷ annual saving.\n- Also check **NPV** over the contract term, sensitivity, and qualitative factors (control, quality, risk, strategic fit). Approve if savings are material, payback is short, and risks are manageable through the contract.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Root-cause analysis for outsourcing failures: the Fishbone ⭐⭐\nThe **Ishikawa (fishbone, cause-and-effect) diagram** puts the problem (e.g. \"temperature excursions & spoilage\") at the head and groups causes on the bones — the **6Ms**: **Man** (people/training), **Machine** (reefer units, data loggers), **Method** (SOPs, pre-cooling, loading), **Material** (packaging, gel packs), **Measurement** (no real-time monitoring, uncalibrated sensors), **Mother nature/Environment** (heat, traffic delays). Add **Management/Contract** (no SLA on temperature, lowest-price carrier). Use with **5 Whys** and Pareto analysis; then corrective (fix now) and preventive (stop recurrence) actions — **CAPA**.\n\n---\n\n## Self-test\n**Q1. Differentiate outsourcing and offshoring with an example.**\nOutsourcing is about **who** does the work (a third party); offshoring is about **where** (another country). An Indian software firm hiring another Indian IT company is domestic outsourcing; opening its own development centre in Vietnam is captive offshoring; hiring a Vietnamese vendor would be offshore outsourcing.\n\n**Q2. What is a weighted scoring model and what is its main weakness?**\nCriteria are weighted to total 100, suppliers are rated on each, and Σ weight × rating gives a comparable score. Weakness: weights and ratings are subjective, so the result can flip with small weight changes; always run a sensitivity analysis and back the ratings with data.\n\n**Q3. List five hidden costs of outsourcing.**\nSupplier search and audits, travel, ERP/IT integration, quality inspection and rework, contract management/governance (also transition, coordination, buffer stock, exit costs).\n\n**Q4. What is the difference between liquidated damages and a penalty?**\nLDs are a genuine pre-estimate of the loss from a breach, agreed in advance, payable without proving actual loss; a penalty is an amount designed to punish/deter, which courts may not enforce in full (India s.74 allows only reasonable compensation up to the stated sum).\n\n**Q5. Define BATNA and explain why it matters.**\nBest Alternative To a Negotiated Agreement — your fallback if talks fail. It sets your walk-away point and your bargaining power; improving it (getting another quote) strengthens your position.\n\n**Q6. When does force majeure apply?**\nWhen performance is prevented by an event beyond the party's reasonable control that was not foreseeable or avoidable, which is listed in (or fits) the clause, the party gave timely notice, and it tried to mitigate. Effect: performance suspended, no damages; prolonged FM may allow termination.\n\n**Q7. Explain 3PL vs 4PL.**\nA 3PL performs logistics operations (warehousing, transport) under contract, often with its own assets; a 4PL is an integrator that designs and manages the whole supply chain and coordinates several 3PLs on the client's behalf, usually without owning assets.\n\n**Q8. What makes a good SLA?**\nClear KPI definitions, realistic targets, an agreed measurement method and data source, reporting frequency, penalties and incentives, exclusions, and a governance/escalation process.\n"}, {"slug": "ch02-incoterms", "label": "Incoterms® 2020", "tag": "02", "group": "chapters", "title": "Incoterms® 2020 — Terms of Trade", "md": "# Incoterms® 2020 — Terms of Trade\nExam topic 2 of 8 📘 (*Incoterms* PPT + 12 problems in *IL problems for practice*). You must be able to (1) explain why Incoterms exist, (2) classify all 11 rules, (3) state each rule's delivery/risk point, cost split, insurance, and who clears customs, and (4) **pick the right rule for a case and justify it**. Worked case answers: [Incoterms Problems](p2-incoterms-problems.md).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why Incoterms are needed ⭐⭐⭐\nThe terms of trade in the contract of sale decide **which tasks are the exporter's and which are the importer's** 📘. An international shipment involves many tasks (packing, loading, export clearance, main carriage, insurance, import clearance, duties, delivery); dividing them afresh for every deal would be a daunting task, it is impossible to foresee everything that could go wrong in transit, and the result would be **misunderstandings, disputes and litigation**. Incoterms settle, in three letters, the **tasks (responsibilities), costs and risks** borne by buyers and sellers 📘.\n\nIncoterms answer five questions 📘:\n1. Which **tasks** the exporter performs.\n2. Which tasks the importer performs.\n3. Which activities are **paid** by the exporter.\n4. Which activities are paid by the importer.\n5. **When the transfer of responsibility (risk)** for the goods takes place.\n\n### Purpose and importance 📘\n- **Purpose**: transmission of **risks** in transporting the goods; allocation of **logistics costs** between seller and buyer; the **documents and customs formalities** for export and import.\n- **Importance**: widely used and internationally known; they prevent confusion by clarifying obligations. Knowing them: (1) brings **clarity and certainty**, (2) **reduces costs** (fewer delays), (3) **reduces risks** (both sides know who bears what at each stage), (4) **simplifies communication**.\n\n### History ⭐⭐\nPublished by the **International Chamber of Commerce (ICC)**, Paris, in **1936**; revised in **1953, 1967, 1976, 1980, 1990, 2000, 2010 and 2020** 📘 (the current version is **Incoterms® 2020**, in force from 1 January 2020). The ICC's mission: \"to make business work for everyone, every day, everywhere\" by promoting open international trade 📘. Incoterms are used in all the documents of an international transaction (contract, invoice, L/C, insurance, B/L).\n\n### Transfer of risk vs transfer of title ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Transfer of responsibility (risk)** is dictated by the Incoterms rule and coincides with **delivery**, a point clearly stated in each rule 📘.\n- **Transfer of title (ownership)** is **not** covered by Incoterms; it usually passes when the importer has paid, obtained the original bill of lading, accepted a draft or done whatever the contract of sale says 📘.\n\n### What Incoterms do NOT deal with ⭐⭐⭐\nWhether there is a contract of sale at all; the specification of the goods; the time, place, method or currency of **payment** of the price; remedies for breach; most consequences of delay and other breaches; the effect of sanctions; the imposition of tariffs; export/import prohibitions; **force majeure or hardship**; intellectual property rights; and the method, venue or law of dispute resolution 📘. They also do **not** decide transfer of title or the price 📘.\n\n### How to write an Incoterm correctly ⭐⭐\n**[Rule] [named place or port] Incoterms® 2020** — e.g. *FCA Warehouse 3, Shanghai, Incoterms® 2020* 📘. Never just \"FOB\". Be precise about the place (a terminal, not just a city) because risk passes there.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The 11 rules and their classification ⭐⭐⭐\n| Group (by letter) | Any mode or modes of transport (7) | Sea and inland waterway only (4) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **E — departure** | **EXW** Ex Works | — |\n| **F — main carriage unpaid** (buyer pays main freight) | **FCA** Free Carrier | **FAS** Free Alongside Ship, **FOB** Free On Board |\n| **C — main carriage paid** (seller pays main freight; risk passes at origin) | **CPT** Carriage Paid To, **CIP** Carriage and Insurance Paid To | **CFR** Cost and Freight, **CIF** Cost, Insurance and Freight |\n| **D — arrival** (seller bears risk to destination) | **DAP** Delivered at Place, **DPU** Delivered at Place Unloaded, **DDP** Delivered Duty Paid | — |\n\n- **Freight collect** terms (buyer pays main carriage): EXW, FCA, FAS, FOB. **Freight prepaid** terms: CFR, CIF, CPT, CIP, DAP, DPU, DDP 📘 (IncoDocs chart).\n- Air freight usually uses **FCA, CPT, CIP** — risk passes at the airport cargo terminal 📘.\n- Maritime-only **FAS, FOB, CFR, CIF** suit **bulk or non-containerised** cargo where risk passes at the port of loading/on board the vessel 📘.\n- The 7 rules suitable for road (and multimodal, containerised or air) are EXW, FCA, CPT, CIP, DAP, DPU, DDP 📘.\n\n**Memory aid**: the seller's obligation grows **E → F → C → D**: EXW (minimum for seller) … DDP (maximum for seller).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Each rule in detail ⭐⭐⭐\nThe course slides rate each rule's risk, insurance and freight cost 📘; the table adds delivery point and customs.\n\n| Rule | Delivery / risk transfer point | Main carriage paid by | Insurance | Export clearance | Import clearance & duty | Course rating 📘 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **EXW** | Goods made available at **seller's premises**, not loaded | Buyer | Not required (buyer's interest) | **Buyer** | Buyer | Seller risk lowest, buyer highest; buyer bears most cost |\n| **FCA** | Handed to **buyer's carrier** at named place (seller's premises → seller loads; elsewhere → ready for unloading) | Buyer | Not required | **Seller** | Buyer | Seller risk low; buyer high; buyer bears most cost |\n| **FAS** | **Alongside the ship** at named port of shipment | Buyer | Not required | Seller | Buyer | Seller risk low; buyer high; buyer bears most cost |\n| **FOB** | **On board the vessel** nominated by buyer at port of shipment | Buyer | Not required | Seller | Buyer | Seller medium, buyer medium; buyer bears most cost |\n| **CFR** | **On board the vessel** at port of shipment (risk) — seller pays freight to destination port | Seller | Not required | Seller | Buyer | Seller medium, buyer medium; seller bears most cost |\n| **CIF** | **On board** at port of shipment (risk) — seller pays freight + insurance to destination port | Seller | **Seller buys minimum cover: ICC (C), 110%** | Seller | Buyer | Insurance yes, only for the main (sea) carriage |\n| **CPT** | Handed to the **first carrier** (risk) — seller pays carriage to named destination | Seller | Not required | Seller | Buyer | Seller low, buyer high; seller bears most cost |\n| **CIP** | Handed to the **first carrier** (risk) — seller pays carriage + insurance to destination | Seller | **Seller buys all-risks cover: ICC (A), 110%** | Seller | Buyer | Insurance yes, only during transit |\n| **DAP** | At **named destination place**, on arriving means of transport, **ready for unloading** | Seller | Not required (seller's own interest) | Seller | **Buyer** | Seller risk high, buyer low |\n| **DPU** | At named destination, **unloaded** from the means of transport | Seller | Not required | Seller | Buyer | Seller risk highest, buyer lowest; **only rule where seller unloads** |\n| **DDP** | At named destination, ready for unloading, **import-cleared and duty paid** | Seller | Not required | Seller | **Seller** | Maximum responsibility on seller |\n\n### Key points per rule 📘📗\n- **EXW** — seller's only obligation is to make goods available at its premises, suitably packed. Buyer loads, clears export, carries, insures, clears import. **Problem**: the buyer, a foreigner, often cannot legally file the export declaration in the seller's country (in India the exporter's IEC and shipping bill are needed), and the seller does not get proof of export for GST/duty refunds → **use FCA instead**.\n- **FCA** — sits between seller-friendly EXW and buyer-friendly DAP/DDP 📘. Best for **containerised, air and road** freight 📘. Seller clears export. 2020 change: parties can agree the buyer's carrier issues an **on-board bill of lading** to the seller (needed for letters of credit).\n- **FAS** — seller places goods alongside the buyer's vessel (on the quay or a barge); used for bulk, heavy-lift and break-bulk cargo.\n- **FOB** — seller loads goods on the buyer's nominated vessel; from then all costs and risks are the buyer's 📘. Standard for **bulk commodities** 📘 (coal, grain, ore, coffee in bulk). **Not suitable for containers** — the seller loses control at the container yard days before loading, yet still bears risk until \"on board\". Use FCA.\n- **CFR / CIF** — **two critical points**: risk passes at the port of **shipment** (on board), but costs pass at the port of **destination**. So in CFR/CIF, risk transfers **before** costs 📘. CIF insurance is minimum cover (**Institute Cargo Clauses C**) 📘 — buyer may ask for more.\n- **CPT / CIP** — the multimodal versions of CFR / CIF: risk passes when goods are handed to the **first carrier**; seller pays to the named destination. **CIP** requires higher cover (**ICC A, all risks**) 📘 — best for **high-value goods** 📘.\n- **DAP** — popular for sellers who want to offer flexibility while managing costs and risks 📘; seller delivers to buyer's named place, buyer unloads, clears import and pays duty. Replaced DAF, DES, DDU (2010).\n- **DPU** — formerly **DAT** (Delivered at Terminal) in 2010; renamed in 2020 because the place need not be a terminal 📘. The only rule requiring the seller to **unload** 📘 — good for heavy/oversized cargo when the seller controls unloading equipment.\n- **DDP** — seller does everything, including import clearance and duties/taxes (VAT/GST) 📘. **Avoid DDP if the seller is not experienced with the destination country's customs** 📘 or cannot register for import VAT there.\n\n### Summary table from the course 📘\n| Term | Risk transfer | Best for |\n|---|---|---|\n| EXW | At seller's door | Experienced buyers wanting full control |\n| FCA | Upon delivery to carrier | Containerised, air or road (versatile) |\n| FOB | On board vessel | Non-containerised sea freight |\n| CIF | On board vessel | Sea freight; seller pays insurance/freight |\n| CIP | To first carrier | Any mode; high insurance requirements |\n| DAP | At destination place | Seller handles delivery, buyer handles import |\n| DDP | At buyer's location | Maximum convenience for buyer |\n\n### The IncoDocs responsibility matrix (who pays) 📘\n| Obligation | EXW | FCA | FAS | FOB | CFR | CIF | CPT | CIP | DAP | DPU | DDP |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Export packaging | S | S | S | S | S | S | S | S | S | S | S |\n| Loading charges (at origin) | B | S | S | S | S | S | S | S | S | S | S |\n| Delivery to port/place | B | S | S | S | S | S | S | S | S | S | S |\n| Export duty, taxes & customs clearance | B | S | S | S | S | S | S | S | S | S | S |\n| Origin terminal charges | B | B | S | S | S | S | S | S | S | S | S |\n| Loading on carriage | B | B | B | S | S | S | S | S | S | S | S |\n| Carriage charges | B | B | B | B | S | S | S | S | S | S | S |\n| Insurance | Neg. | Neg. | Neg. | Neg. | Neg. | **S*** | Neg. | **S**** | Neg. | Neg. | Neg. |\n| Destination terminal charges | B | B | B | B | B | B | S | S | S | S | S |\n| Delivery to destination | B | B | B | B | B | B | B | B | S | S | S |\n| Unloading at destination | B | B | B | B | B | B | B | B | B | **S** | B |\n| Import duty, taxes & customs clearance | B | B | B | B | B | B | B | B | B | B | **S** |\nS = seller, B = buyer, Neg. = negotiable (not an obligation). *CIF: minimum cover (ICC C). **CIP: all-risks cover (ICC A). (Terminal charges under C terms depend on the carriage contract; treat the chart as the course's reference.)\n\n---\n\n## 4. Main changes in Incoterms® 2020 (vs 2010) ⭐⭐\n1. **DAT renamed DPU** (Delivered at Place Unloaded) — the place of unloading can be any place, not only a terminal.\n2. **Different insurance levels**: CIF stays at ICC (C) minimum; **CIP raised to ICC (A)** all risks.\n3. **FCA and on-board bill of lading** — buyer can instruct its carrier to issue an on-board B/L to the seller.\n4. **Own means of transport allowed** in FCA, DAP, DPU and DDP (no third-party carrier needed).\n5. **Security-related requirements and costs** allocated explicitly (A4/B4, A7/B7).\n6. **Costs listed in one place** (article A9/B9) and the articles reordered (A1–A10 seller, B1–B10 buyer), with delivery and risk now at A2/A3.\n7. More explanatory notes for users.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Selecting the optimal Incoterm ⭐⭐⭐\nBalance **cost, risk and control**, tailored to the transport mode and the logistics expertise of each party 📘. Key factors 📘:\n1. **Mode of transport** — for containerised, air or rail freight avoid maritime-only FOB/CIF; use FCA, CPT or CIP.\n2. **Risk transfer vs cost transfer** — under C terms risk passes before costs (on board at origin) while the seller pays freight to destination.\n3. **Control and logistics capabilities** — buyer with a strong forwarder → EXW/FCA; seller better equipped → DAP/DDP.\n4. **Customs expertise** — the party more familiar with local rules should clear customs; avoid DDP if the seller doesn't know the destination's customs.\n5. **Insurance requirements** — CIP (ICC A) gives higher cover than CIF (ICC C).\n\nRecommended by scenario 📘: containerised → **FCA** (risk passes at delivery to carrier, not at loading on vessel); maximum buyer convenience → **DDP**; maximum buyer control (minimum seller obligation) → **EXW**; high-value goods → **CIP**; non-containerised sea freight/bulk → **FOB**. An exporter wanting to grow sales should offer the importer the most **customer-friendly** (D or C) quote 📘.\n\n### Quick decision flow 📗\n1. Is the cargo bulk/break-bulk loaded directly on a ship? → **FAS / FOB / CFR / CIF**. Otherwise (containers, air, road, rail, multimodal) → **EXW / FCA / CPT / CIP / DAP / DPU / DDP**.\n2. Who arranges and pays the main carriage? Buyer → **F** (or E); seller → **C** or **D**.\n3. If the seller pays freight, where should risk pass? At origin (seller wants low risk) → **C**; at destination (buyer wants delivered service) → **D**.\n4. Who must insure? Seller (by contract) → **CIF** (sea) or **CIP** (any mode).\n5. Who unloads? Seller → **DPU**.\n6. Who clears import and pays duty? Seller → **DDP**; buyer → everything else.\n\n### Common mistakes ⭐⭐\n- Using **FOB or CIF for containers or air** (risk stays with seller after it has lost control; carrier receipts don't evidence \"on board\"). Use FCA/CPT/CIP.\n- Using **EXW for exports** where the buyer cannot clear export.\n- Vague places (\"FOB China\"); forgetting the version year.\n- Thinking Incoterms transfer **ownership** or set **payment** terms.\n- Choosing **DDP** without being able to register for import VAT/GST or clear customs in the buyer's country.\n- Assuming CIF/CIP insurance fully protects the buyer — CIF cover is minimum (ICC C).\n\n### Incoterms and other documents\n- **Letter of credit**: C terms need an on-board B/L as evidence of shipment; FCA 2020's on-board B/L option fixes the L/C problem.\n- **Customs valuation**: India assesses import duty on **CIF value** (assessable value); exports are recorded on **FOB value**.\n- **Insurance**: 110% of the CIF/CIP value is the standard minimum insured sum (contract value + 10% for expected profit).\n\n---\n\n## Self-test\n**Q1. What is an Incoterm and who publishes it?**\nA three-letter trade term published by the International Chamber of Commerce (first in 1936, now Incoterms® 2020) that defines the tasks, costs and risks of seller and buyer in delivering goods under a sale contract, including the point where risk transfers and who handles export/import formalities.\n\n**Q2. Classify the 11 Incoterms by mode of transport.**\nAny mode (7): EXW, FCA, CPT, CIP, DAP, DPU, DDP. Sea and inland waterway (4): FAS, FOB, CFR, CIF.\n\n**Q3. Why is FCA preferred over FOB for container shipments?**\nContainer cargo is handed over at an inland depot or container yard well before it is loaded on the ship. Under FOB the seller keeps the risk until the goods are on board, though it no longer controls them; under FCA risk passes when the goods reach the carrier, which matches the real hand-over. FCA also works for every mode.\n\n**Q4. What is the difference between CIF and CIP on insurance?**\nBoth oblige the seller to insure for at least 110% of contract value, but CIF requires only minimum cover (Institute Cargo Clauses C, named perils) and is sea-only, while CIP (2020) requires all-risks cover (ICC A) and works for any mode.\n\n**Q5. Which is the only Incoterm that requires the seller to unload, and what did it replace?**\nDPU (Delivered at Place Unloaded); it replaced DAT (Delivered at Terminal) in 2020.\n\n**Q6. Name five things Incoterms do not cover.**\nTransfer of title, payment terms and price, remedies for breach, force majeure, tariffs/sanctions/prohibitions, IP rights, dispute resolution, and whether a contract exists.\n\n**Q7. In CFR, when do risk and cost transfer?**\nRisk transfers when the goods are on board the vessel at the port of shipment; the seller pays the freight up to the named port of destination. Two critical points: risk passes at origin, cost at destination.\n\n**Q8. Which rule places the maximum and the minimum obligation on the seller?**\nMaximum: DDP (seller delivers, clears import and pays duties). Minimum: EXW (seller only makes goods available at its premises).\n"}, {"slug": "ch03-containerization-container-imbalance", "label": "Containerization & Imbalance", "tag": "03", "group": "chapters", "title": "Containerization & Container Imbalance", "md": "# Containerization & Container Imbalance\nExam topic 3 of 8 📘 (*Containerization and container imbalance* PPT, 47 slides, plus the Omega 2019 research article it cites for innovative strategies). Expect: definitions and objectives, container types, the container imbalance problem (causes, effects, CAx, empty container repositioning, CIC charges, SOC), and five short case problems — solved on [Container Imbalance Problems](p3-container-imbalance-problems.md).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Containerization ⭐⭐⭐\n**Containerization** is the process of transporting cargo in **containers standardised by the ISO** (International Organization for Standardization) 📘. Containers have specific dimensions and can be loaded, stacked and transported easily across **multimodal** transport 📘. \"The heart of the global economy is a rectangular metal box, 40 ft long, 8 ft wide and 8½ ft tall called a freight container\" 📘.\n\n- **Intermodal transportation**: moving goods in a **single load unit** (container, semi-trailer or swap body) using multiple modes, without handling the goods themselves when changing modes 📘.\n- ISO standardised container ratings, dimensions and terminology 📘 (ISO 668 sizes, ISO 6346 coding/marking, ISO 1496 specifications).\n- Containerization **changed the structure of ports** — loading/unloading moved from manual break-bulk handling to gantry cranes and container terminals 📘.\n- The key idea: **goods are not shifted out of the container**, so movement across modes is smooth 📘; containers are strong enough for repeated use 📘.\n\n**History** 📗: Malcolm McLean's *Ideal X* carried 58 containers from Newark to Houston on **26 April 1956**; loading cost fell from about $5.86/ton (break-bulk) to $0.16/ton. ISO standards followed in 1968–70. India's first container ship call was at Cochin (1973); JNPT (Nhava Sheva, 1989) is India's largest container port. Today about 60% of seaborne trade by value moves in containers.\n\n### Why containerization ⭐⭐\nIt made shipping **easy and international**; enables intermodal transport (ship, aircraft, train or truck) 📘; standard boxes are easily loaded/packed, so **port handling cost falls** 📘; reduces **shipping time, damage and pilferage** 📘; increases the **speed of loading and unloading ships** 📘 (turnaround from weeks to hours); cheaper insurance; economies of scale (ships of 24,000 TEU).\n\n### Objectives of containerization ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n1. **Protection** of goods from extreme weather (tightly closed).\n2. A **standard box** for transport across all modes worldwide.\n3. Easier **loading and unloading**.\n4. Serves as **temporary storage** from which goods can be moved when needed.\n5. Minimises **theft and pilferage** (locked, sealed; only authorised access to storage areas).\n6. Easier handling with **hooks/corner castings** that cranes use to pick and place the container on ship, train or truck.\n7. **Reduces handling** requirements of the goods.\n\n### Basic types by size ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\nMade of high-tensile, anti-corrosion (Corten) steel 📘.\n\n| | 20-footer (1 TEU) | 40-footer (2 TEU = 1 FEU) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Capacity | **33.2 m³** | **67.7 m³** |\n| Max payload | **28,180 kg** | **28,750 kg** |\n| Internal length | **5.9 m** | **12.03 m** |\n| Internal width | **2.35 m** | **2.35 m** |\n| Internal height | **2.4 m** | **2.4 m** |\n\n📗 Also: **40' High Cube** (9'6\" tall, ~76 m³), 45' HC; external width 8 ft (2.44 m). **TEU** = twenty-foot equivalent unit, the standard measure of ship/port capacity; **FEU** = forty-foot equivalent unit.\n\n### Types of containers ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n1. **General cargo container** — general cargo (chemicals, food, furniture); no temperature control, no perishables.\n2. **Thermal container** — temperature-controlled environment; walls of low heat-transfer material (e.g. polystyrene foam):\n   - a. **Refrigerated (reefer)** — meat, fish (built-in refrigeration unit, plugged in on ship/yard).\n   - b. **Insulated** — vegetables and fruits, using dry ice as the cooling medium.\n   - c. **Ventilated** — openings in the sides let air circulate; tea leaves, coffee beans.\n3. **Dry cargo containers** — the most used; coal, grains, metals (dry, non-temperature-sensitive cargo).\n4. **Flat containers / flat racks** — strong base, collapsible ends; odd-size cargo such as large pipes or army tanks.\n5. **Bulk containers** — large, with holes (hatches) at the top like water tankers; free-flowing dry cargo (cement, ores, grains).\n6. **Garment containers** — racks, rails and hangers so garments hang and are not twisted or damaged.\n7. **Tanks / liquid containers** — stainless steel, top openings for loading/unloading; e.g. milk.\n8. **Gas containers** — thick stainless-steel walls; gaseous substances like liquid oxygen.\n9. **Open-top container** — goods taller than the container height (loaded by crane from above, tarpaulin cover).\n10. **Car carriers** — cars over long distances; sides often collapsible to avoid damage.\n11. **Special-purpose containers** — specialised cargo such as weapons.\n12. **Open-side container** — when cargo needs a broader loading area, e.g. long rail bars.\n13. **Double-door container** — the widest possible loading/unloading area (doors at both ends).\n14. **Tunnel storage container** — opens at both ends for quick loading/unloading.\nPictured in the PPT: dry, flat rack, open top, side open, refrigerated ISO (reefer) and **half-height** containers (for heavy, dense cargo like stones/ore).\n\n### Key terms 📘\n- **Cargo** — goods being transported.\n- **Carrier** — a company that transports goods or people; responsible for safe and timely delivery; uses trucks, vans, railroads, aircraft or ships.\n- **Container** — a sealed, rigid, reusable box designed to be easy to fill and empty and to carry goods without reloading.\n- **Shipper** — the person/company that owns the goods; responsible for packaging, labelling and monitoring; hires carriers.\n- 📗 **FCL/LCL** (full / less than container load), **CFS** (container freight station, where LCL is consolidated), **CY** (container yard), **ICD** (inland container depot / dry port), **COC** (carrier-owned container) vs **SOC** (shipper-owned container), **demurrage** (charge for keeping a container inside the port/terminal beyond free days) vs **detention** (charge for keeping the carrier's container outside the terminal beyond free days), **VGM** (verified gross mass, SOLAS 2016), **headhaul/backhaul** (loaded vs return direction).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Container imbalance — the problem ⭐⭐⭐\n**Container imbalance** occurs when there is a **surplus of cargo to be shipped and a lack of containers** in one place, while too many **empty containers sit idle** in other places 📘. In simple terms: you have cargo to ship but no container to carry it 📘.\n\n- When goods move from A to B, the same box should carry goods back; **if imports match exports, no imbalance occurs** 📘. When a country has nothing to send back, containers remain empty at its ports 📘; eventually they pile up beyond port capacity.\n- The biggest problem: **multimodal containers sitting empty inland** 📘.\n- **Moving an empty container is like driving an empty truck — it doesn't pay** 📘.\n- A container will only be available if a full one was offloaded nearby 📘 — an age-old problem that COVID-19 brought into the limelight 📘.\n- It costs shippers heavy **container imbalance charges (CIC)** and can cause big monetary losses 📘.\n\n### The threefold aggravation of stockpiling 📘\nWhen containers do not arrive, manufacturers stockpile finished goods:\n1. **Suppliers can't fill orders on time.**\n2. **Stockpiling eats up cash flow** as orders remain unpaid.\n3. **Manufacturers run out of storage space** — they must slow production (layoffs) or rent more space (unnecessary expense).\n\n### Causes of container imbalance ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n1. **Higher shipping rates** — shippers hold back; solution: smaller shipments or cheaper destinations.\n2. **Space issues** — containers too heavy/overfilled are left at port; choose the right container type and size.\n3. **Using one freight forwarder for multiple destinations** — goods pass through multiple vendors, costs rise; use multiple forwarders to balance shipments.\n4. **Cargo weight** — too light or too heavy cargo moves slower, delays loading/unloading.\n5. **Container shortage** — directly linked: fewer boxes means cargo piles up at ports/warehouses.\n\nUnderlying structural causes 📗 (the \"big picture\", also in the PPT's empty-repositioning section): **trade imbalance** (Asia exports far more than it imports from the West — Europe/US ports accumulate empties, Asian ports run short), seasonal peaks, demand shocks (COVID), port congestion and long dwell times, carriers' reluctance to share boxes, poor forecasting and outdated planning, and blank sailings.\n\n### Effects of container imbalance (2023) ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n- **Suppliers couldn't fill orders on time** → disrupted cash flow, unpaid orders.\n- **Higher shipping costs** — prices surge; importers/exporters delay shipments until a cheaper box is available, which further disturbs flows.\n- **Delayed supply chains** — products take longer to reach shelves.\n- **Environmental damage** — more empties stored or repositioned: more fuel, space and resources.\n- **Disrupted global commerce** — reliability and predictability of shipping falls.\n- **Inventory shortages** — shippers can't meet customer expectations and deadlines.\n- **Contract penalties** for missing customer deadlines.\n\n### Industry issues in 2023 (reading only) 📘\n1. **US West Coast tension** — ILWU–PMA labour negotiations caused uncertainty; cargo shifted to East and Gulf Coast ports, causing congestion there in 2022.\n2. **Imbalances in the EU** — idle containers due to low consumer confidence; congestion, shortage of space at ports, warehouses, yards.\n3. **Excess retail inventory** — inflation cut spending; retailers clear stock before ordering; containers don't move.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Container Availability Index (CAx) ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\nThe **CAx** (published by **Container xChange**) is a metric giving **real-time insight into the supply and demand of containers** in a region or trade route. It measures availability as the ratio between containers in circulation and current demand, built from data on availability, location and demand supplied by container owners, leasing companies and other stakeholders.\n\nHow to read it:\n- **CAx = 0.5** → the number of full containers **leaving and entering** a port in a week is the same (balanced).\n- **CAx > 0.5** → **more full containers enter** than leave → empties accumulate → **surplus**.\n- **CAx < 0.5** → **more full containers leave** than enter → **shortage**.\n- Low CAx for several weeks = persistent shortage; high CAx over an extended period = excess.\n- Example in the PPT: **India** has seen a decline in demand for outbound containers, giving a **higher CAx** (more surplus) overall.\n\nUse: shipping lines, logistics providers and shippers can plan proactively, allocate resources and optimise container distribution.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Empty Container Repositioning (ECR) ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\nIn an ideal world every container is full on every journey; in reality a carrier often has **surplus empties at one port (e.g. London)** and **not enough at another (e.g. Shanghai)**, so it moves the empties **without cargo** — **empty container repositioning**.\n\n- An empty container takes **the same slot on a ship as a full one**, so moving it costs about the same — and nobody pays for it; the cost comes out of the carrier's profit.\n- The average container spends **56% of its lifespan idle or empty** — more than it spends carrying cargo.\n- About **170 million** containers are shipped globally a year, of which **50 million are empty**.\n- Imbalance makes around **10% of global container assets** empty at any time, and empties account for about **20.5% of port handlings**; repositioning costs the industry **$15–20 billion a year**, plus drayage, transhipment and empty storage costs.\n\n### What causes empty container repositioning ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n1. **Trade imbalances** — a port importing more than it exports accumulates empties; one exporting more runs out. Most common: **West ↔ Asia** (European and American ports surplus, Asian ports shortage).\n2. **Storage costs** — about **5 free days** at a port, then up to **$100 per day**; often cheaper to move the empty than wait for cargo.\n3. **Time pressure** — when export demand surges in one region, carriers rush empties there. In 2020–21, US/EU consumers shifted spending from services to goods; imports soared and some carriers shipped **75% of their containers from the US and Europe to Asia empty**.\n4. **Outdated logistics planning** — many lines still plan repositioning on **Excel**; AI tools exist but adoption is slow.\n5. **Lack of cooperation between carriers** — Carrier A lacks boxes in Sweden while Carrier B repositions its surplus Swedish empties to the US; sharing would save both money and emissions, but competitors refuse to share data, so container pools are hard to set up.\n\nECR drivers listed in the PPT 📘: trade inefficiencies; revenue generation; lease and manufacturing costs; **headhaul volume** (difference between outbound and inbound freight volume); unreliable forecasting.\n\n### Levels of repositioning (Omega 2019) 📗\n**Global** (between ports/regions, by ship), **regional** (between shippers, consignees, ICDs and ports in one area) and **local** (between inland depots and surrounding customers). After an importer empties a box, it can: go back to the port for global repositioning; go by rail back to the owner; go to a depot (**depot-direct**) for future demand; or go **directly to a nearby exporter** — a **street turn**.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Container Imbalance Charges (CIC) ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n**CIC** (also container imbalance surcharge, equipment imbalance surcharge) are charges levied by shipping lines to **compensate for repositioning empty containers** between countries where there is a trade imbalance. Repositioning includes drayage, transhipment at terminals and empty warehousing while boxes wait.\n\n**Who pays?** The carrier may impose it on the **consignee or the shipper**:\n- the **local consignee** pays if it creates the imbalance by **receiving more containers than it ships**;\n- the **shipper** pays if it is **sending out more containers than it receives**.\nAlways review the shipping contract's terms for such extra charges. Ultimately the cost is passed to the **cargo owners and end consumers** through freight rates and prices.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Strategies to avoid / reduce container imbalance ⭐⭐⭐\n**From the PPT** 📘: **Digitalization** (track boxes online, know where surpluses will be); **research market forecasts** (avoid delays and congestion); **Container Availability Index (CAx)**; **empty container repositioning**; **virtual container pools** for container exchange; **leasing SOC containers** for one-way moves.\n\n**Innovative strategies from the Omega paper** (Kuzmicz & Pesch 2019, *Approaches to empty container repositioning problems in the context of Eurasian intermodal transportation*) 📘📗:\n| Strategy | What it is | Benefits | Barriers |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Street turns** | Empty box moved **directly from an importer to a nearby exporter** without returning to the port/depot | Saves two empty trips, drayage, gate fees, emissions (case studies: ~2% network cost saving) | Strict **time windows**, matching container type/size, owner's permission, insurance and liability, inspection |\n| **Container sharing / pooling** | Carriers (or shippers) share boxes — horizontal collaboration; \"grey box\" pools | Fewer empty moves; uses backhaul opportunities (e.g. Norway wood industry: −15% empty distance, −6–7% cost) | Competitors reluctant to share data; branding; routing optimisation often cheaper |\n| **Container substitution** | Meet demand with a different type, e.g. **two 20' requests with one 40'** | Uses available surplus types | No well-defined substitution rules; handling equipment and cargo limits |\n| **Connectainers** | 20' boxes that **join to form a 40'** (and split again) in ~30 minutes | Flexibility between 20'/40' demand; same dimensions and handling gear | New, not widespread |\n| **Foldable (collapsible) containers** | Fold when empty: e.g. **4 folded inside a 5th** (Fallpac), **6-in-1** bundles; save up to **75% of storage space**; cheaper than standard if price is ≤ 50% higher | Fewer slots, lifts and yard space for empties (could cut port handlings up to 80%) | Purchase cost, folding labour, durability/leak fears, sunk investment in standard fleet |\nAlso: **RFID/IoT tracking** for visibility; **optimisation models** (network-flow, linear/stochastic programming) for repositioning; **depot-direct** moves; **one-way leasing**; **SOC**; dynamic pricing (discounts for backhaul cargo); triangulation of routes.\n\n### Shipper-owned containers (SOC) and Container xChange 📘\nLeasing **SOC** containers for one-way moves avoids CIC. Benefits: more **independence, control and flexibility** (choose reliable partners); avoid high carrier rates, **demurrage and detention**; choose exactly the containers and their condition; adapt to a changing industry; access to many SOC partners on platforms such as Container xChange. Finding SOCs takes time (background checks, negotiating terms, legal agreements), especially during imbalances.\n\n### Factors that influence carriers' Container Inventory Management Strategies (CIMS) 📘\nTable 4 of *The Global Impact of Container Inventory Imbalance and the Factors that Influence Container Inventory Management Strategies* (marked **important** in the PPT):\n\n| No. | Factor | Short name |\n|---|---|---|\n| Q1 | Strength of retaining customers despite non-availability of containers | Cost of customers |\n| Q2 | Impact on brand name of inconsistent container availability | Impact on brand |\n| Q3 | Threat from container shortage to sustainability of service | Threat on service |\n| Q4 | Confidence to perform budgeted exports/imports | Loss of revenue |\n| Q5 | Comfort on freight (slot cost) incurred on empty repositioning | Empty slot cost |\n| Q6 | Port handling cost on empty repositioning | Empty port handling |\n| Q7 | High rent at CFS or port for storing containers | Cost of rent |\n| Q8 | Comfort on empty container handling cost at CFS | Cost of yard |\n| Q9 | Possibility of achieving ROI on containers owned by the line | ROI |\n| Q10 | Repair and painting cost due to rust from long storage | Wear and tare cost |\n| Q11 | Container idle time at a location | Minimum idle time |\n| Q12 | Vessel under-utilisation at ports due to non-availability of containers | Vessel under-utilization |\n\n---\n\n## Self-test\n**Q1. Define containerization and list its objectives.**\nTransporting cargo in ISO-standardised containers that can be loaded, stacked and moved across modes without handling the goods. Objectives: weather protection, a standard box for all modes, easier loading/unloading, temporary storage, less theft and pilferage, crane handling by hooks/corner castings, and less handling of goods.\n\n**Q2. Give the dimensions and capacity of a 20-ft and a 40-ft container (as per the PPT).**\n20 ft: 33.2 m³, max payload 28,180 kg, internal 5.9 × 2.35 × 2.4 m. 40 ft: 67.7 m³, max payload 28,750 kg, internal 12.03 × 2.35 × 2.4 m.\n\n**Q3. What is container imbalance? Explain its causes.**\nA mismatch where some locations have surplus empty containers and others have cargo but no boxes. Causes: trade imbalance, high shipping rates, space issues, using one forwarder for many destinations, cargo weight, container shortage, plus storage costs, demand surges, outdated planning and lack of carrier cooperation.\n\n**Q4. How is the Container Availability Index interpreted?**\n0.5 = balanced (as many full boxes in as out); above 0.5 = more full boxes arrive than leave → surplus; below 0.5 = more full boxes leave → shortage. Sustained low values mean shortage, sustained high values mean excess.\n\n**Q5. What is a street turn?**\nReusing an import container emptied at a consignee directly for a nearby exporter's load, without returning it to the port or depot — saving two empty trips.\n\n**Q6. Who pays the Container Imbalance Charge?**\nThe party that creates the imbalance: the consignee if it receives more containers than it ships, the shipper if it ships out more than it receives; ultimately the cargo owners and consumers.\n\n**Q7. Name five innovative solutions to empty container repositioning.**\nStreet turns, container sharing/pooling, container substitution, connectainers, foldable containers (plus SOC leasing, digital tracking, optimisation models, virtual pools).\n\n**Q8. Distinguish demurrage and detention.**\nDemurrage is charged for the container staying inside the port/terminal beyond the free time; detention is charged for keeping the carrier's container outside the terminal (at the shipper's or consignee's premises) beyond the free time.\n"}, {"slug": "ch04-maritime-logistics-costing", "label": "Maritime & Logistics Costing", "tag": "04", "group": "chapters", "title": "Maritime & Logistics Costing", "md": "# Maritime & Logistics Costing\nExam topic 4 of 8 📘 (*Maritime and logistics costing* PPT, 58 slides). Learn the **7 cost stages**, how sea, air, road and rail are priced, FCL rate types, the actors who set rates, the factors and surcharges (BAF, CAF, THC, PSS, EBS, GRI, green fees), the **six methods of calculating freight**, and the step-by-step **ocean freight calculation**. Every worked example from the PPT is solved in full on [Freight Costing Numericals](p5-freight-costing-numericals.md).\n\n---\n\n## 1. The 7 steps in calculating shipping costs ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\nWhatever the destination or mode (sea, air, road, rail), costs follow the same **seven stages** of the transport process:\n1. **Export transport (collection)** — pick-up from the supplier to the port/airport/hub.\n2. **Export customs clearance.**\n3. **Goods handling in the country of origin** (terminal handling, loading, stuffing).\n4. **Freight** (the main carriage).\n5. **Import customs clearance.**\n6. **Goods handling at destination.**\n7. **Import transport (delivery)** to the consignee.\nLink with Incoterms: the Incoterm decides which of these seven the seller pays and which the buyer pays.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Cost by mode ⭐⭐⭐\n\n### Sea freight 📘\nTwo options: **LCL** and **FCL**.\n- **LCL (Less than Container Load)**: general cargo for smaller deliveries where **several importers share a container**; best for **< 15 m³**. Freight is priced on **volume (m³)**, the space the goods take — the greater the volume, the higher the price. LCL prices vary much less, because the ocean freight is a small part of the total; the bigger costs are **collection, export and import handling, and delivery** (consolidation at CFS, deconsolidation).\n- **FCL (Full Container Load)**: better when shipping **> 15 m³** — you fill the whole container. FCL price varies considerably with **supply and demand** (half-empty ships on a route → lower price, and vice versa), and with exchange rates, oil prices, traffic, war-risk charges and seasonality.\n- **Pick-up location** matters: collection several hundred km inland in China costs much more than from a coastal city with its own port (Shanghai).\n\n### Air freight 📘\nChosen when **speed** is crucial; transit typically **3–12 days** door to door depending on service level and origin. Two types: **standard air freight** and **courier services**.\n- Standard air freight is priced on **volumetric (dimensional) weight** using the airline's **DIM factor**: volume in cm³ ÷ **6,000** = kg, i.e. about **166.67 kg per m³**. Example: 1 m³ weighing 100 kg → chargeable **167 kg**; 1 m³ weighing 500 kg → chargeable **500 kg** (actual weight is higher).\n- Price also depends on the **route** (supply and demand), the forwarder's **negotiating power** on volume, **currency**, **fuel surcharges** and **transit time** (faster = dearer).\n\n### Sea vs air rule of thumb ⭐⭐ 📘\nIf the shipment is **< 0.5 m³ and < 100 kg**, air may be competitive, because **sea freight has a minimum chargeable volume of 1 m³**. Above **0.5 m³ and 100 kg**, sea is generally cheaper — but transit can be **up to 43 days** from places like China.\n\n### Road freight 📘\n- **LTL (Less than Truckload)**: typically **1–2 pallets**; you buy pallet spaces and share the truck with other importers. A small truck collects → hub warehouse → larger export truck → local warehouse at destination → small truck delivers.\n- **FTL (Full Truckload)**: you buy the whole truck — typically **33 pallet spaces or about 90 m³** for a standard curtain-sider. No handling or trans-shipment cost, as the truck runs **directly from supplier to you**; slightly shorter transit.\n- Consignments (groupage) also exist. Forwarders have **fixed LTL rates by volume, weight and distance**; each country is divided into **zones**, and price depends on start and end zones; plus **fuel surcharges** and special handling charges.\n\n### Rail freight 📘\n**Cheaper than air, faster than sea, greener than road** (e.g. China–Europe rail). Costs depend on destination and demand; you need the cargo size and total volume; compare total freight cost against the cost per m³ to find the cheapest mode.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Why ocean freight cost calculation matters ⭐⭐ 📘\nOcean freight directly affects **retail prices, product margins and delivery schedules**; bad estimates cause unexpected charges, customs issues and damaged supplier relationships. **UNCTAD's Review of Maritime Transport 2024**: ocean freight costs rose **15–25%** globally between 2020 and 2023 (port congestion, fuel inflation, climate-related delays). Especially critical for **SMEs**, **JIT retailers** and **e-commerce brands using DDP**. Freight pricing also affects **trade equity** — developing nations often pay more per unit because of inefficient port access or container imbalances.\n\n---\n\n## 4. FCL rate types ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n| Rate type | Description | Details |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Spot** | One-time price set for a specific shipment at a particular moment | Varies with vessel space, market demand and current conditions |\n| **By validity period** | Rate agreed for a set period (weeks to a year) | Good for regular shippers who want to avoid volatility |\n| **Contract** | Agreement between customer and line on a rate for a **determined volume** over an agreed period | Price stability for the customer; assured business for the line |\n| **All-in** | Includes all costs of transport, at various service levels (possibly door-to-door) | Simple, transparent single rate; easy planning and budgeting |\n\n## 5. Actors in maritime freight rates ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n| Actor | Description | Functions |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Carriers (shipping lines)** | Operate the cargo ships | Provide sea transport; **control space supply and set freight rates**; responsible for cargo safety and timely arrival |\n| **Non-Vessel Operators (NVOs)** | Intermediaries that do **not own ships** | Link shippers and lines; negotiate rates, coordinate transport, manage documentation |\n| **NVOCC (Non-Vessel Operating Common Carrier)** | Like NVOs but **issue their own bills of lading** and take more responsibility for cargo | Integrated services: consolidation, tracking, customs; **buy space from lines and resell under their own B/L** |\n| **Freight forwarders** | Organise and coordinate international shipments | Multimodal (sea, land, air); negotiate rates; prepare export documents; arrange customs clearance at destination |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Factors that influence maritime freight rates ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n**Economic**: supply and demand of ships and equipment (containers, chassis); **peak season** (holiday rush raises rates); **global economic cycles** (recessions lower demand and rates).\n**Logistic**: **operating costs** (fuel, labour, maintenance, security); **itineraries, distances and navigation routes**; **port infrastructure** (efficiency and capacity affect transit time and cost); **loading/unloading capacity**.\n**Regulatory and political**: **government regulations** (safety, environment); **international trade agreements** (shift trade flows); **geopolitical conflicts** (route disruption, higher insurance).\n**Environmental**: **emission regulations** (cleaner tech investment); **weather** (storms, hurricanes); **technological innovations** (fuel-efficient vessels, fleet management can lower costs).\n\n### Other factors (12) ⭐⭐ 📘\n1. **Fuel prices** — bunker fuel is a major cost; carriers add fuel surcharges when oil surges.\n2. **Port fees** — docking, handling, storage; vary by port efficiency, infrastructure and location; congested or modernising ports charge more.\n3. **Surcharges** — BAF (fuel), CAF (currency), **emergency risk surcharge** (piracy, war), **peak season surcharge**.\n4. **Currency exchange rates.**\n5. **Demand and supply dynamics** (peaks raise rates; overcapacity lowers them).\n6. **Environmental regulations** — IMO sulphur cap (IMO 2020: 0.5% sulphur) → costlier fuels passed on.\n7. **Geopolitical and economic factors** — rerouting, longer transit, security.\n8. **Insurance** — varies with nature and value of goods.\n9. **Container handling and haulage** — moving boxes to/from ports and loading them is a big share of cost.\n10. **Carrier charges** — container use, documentation, booking fees: the foundational costs.\n11. **Type and size of container** — dry boxes cheapest; reefers, flat racks cost more; larger containers cheaper per unit for bulk goods.\n12. **Special handling** — hazardous or special cargo needs extra procedures, equipment and precautions.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Surcharges and fees — know every abbreviation ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n| Code | Name | What it covers |\n|---|---|---|\n| **BAF** | Bunker Adjustment Factor | Fluctuating **fuel** costs; updated monthly or quarterly |\n| **CAF** | Currency Adjustment Factor | **Exchange-rate** risk, e.g. USD contracts with EUR/RMB suppliers; often a % of base freight (+BAF) |\n| **THC** | Terminal Handling Charge | Loading/unloading and equipment handling at **origin and/or destination** terminals |\n| **PSS** | Peak Season Surcharge | High-demand seasons, e.g. **August–October** for holiday goods |\n| **EBS** | Emergency Bunker Surcharge | Fuel crises/geopolitical disruptions (Red Sea crisis, Suez delays) |\n| **GRI** | General Rate Increase | Temporary carrier-announced price hikes on volatile lanes |\n| **Green / carbon fees** | Sustainability fees (2025+) | IMO 2023 rules, **CII** (Carbon Intensity Indicator), EU ETS — green premiums for cleaner fuel/retrofits |\n| **ISPS** | International Ship and Port Facility Security charge | Port/ship security (ISPS Code) |\n| Others | Documentation/B/L fee, CFS charges, war-risk/emergency risk surcharge, CIC (container imbalance), transhipment fee | |\n\n### Key components of ocean freight charges ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n1. **Base ocean freight rate** — core charge port to port; quoted **per container for FCL** and **per ton or per m³ for LCL**. Depends on route (Asia–Europe vs Trans-Pacific), vessel type and space, container size (20', 40', high cube) and seasonality. Published via forwarders, NVOCCs, or portals (Maersk Spot, Hapag-Lloyd Quick Quotes).\n2. **Surcharges** — BAF/fuel, security surcharge, PSS.\n3. **Handling charges** — loading and unloading along the chain (THC).\n4. **Additional costs** — CAF, CFS charges.\n5. **Additional service-based costs** — pre-shipment inspection, customs clearance, inland haulage, cargo insurance, last-mile delivery.\n\n**Total freight charges = base freight + surcharges + additional costs** 📘.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Methods of calculating freight rates ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n| # | Method | Formula | Used in |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Per mile / per km** | Rate per mile × total distance | Trucking, road |\n| 2 | **Weight-based** | Rate per unit weight × total weight | Air and rail |\n| 3 | **Volume-based (dimensional weight)** | DIM weight (lb) = L × W × H (in) ÷ DIM factor; charge on the higher of DIM and actual weight | Light, bulky parcels |\n| 4 | **Flat rate** | One fixed charge regardless of distance/weight | Specific routes/services |\n| 5 | **Zone-based** | Rate for the origin–destination zone pair | USPS, couriers |\n| 6 | **Freight class (LTL)** | Density (lb/ft³) = weight ÷ (L × W × H in ft) → NMFC class **50–500** | US LTL carriers |\n- DIM factor: **139 in³/lb domestic**, **166 in³/lb international** (FedEx SmartPost, DHL use 166) 📘. Conversions: 1 lb = 0.453 kg; 1 inch = 0.0254 m 📘.\n- Freight class is based on **density, stowability, handling and liability** 📘.\n\n## 9. How to calculate ocean freight charges (LCL) — 5 steps ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n1. **Weight and dimensions**: package volume (m³) = L × W × H (cm) ÷ 1,000,000; total volume = package volume × number of packages (add them if sizes differ); total weight likewise.\n2. **Chargeable weight** = the **greater** of actual weight and **volumetric weight**, where volumetric weight = volume (m³) × carrier's conversion factor (kg/m³). (Note: when total weight exceeds **1,000 kg**, actual weight is typically used for LCL.)\n3. **Research freight rates** — base rate per kg or per m³ plus port handling and fuel surcharges.\n4. **Base freight** = chargeable weight × base rate. **For ocean freight the conversion factor is 1,000 kg per m³ (1 m³ = 1 tonne)** — the **W/M (weight or measure)** or **revenue ton** rule.\n5. **Total freight** = base freight + surcharges + additional costs; confirm with a quote.\n\n### Tips to save on ocean freight 📘\nGather correct shipment details (weight, volume, type, dimensions, origin/destination, FCL vs LCL); obtain and compare several quotes (or use a digital forwarder); **consolidate** shipments; **plan early** (avoid urgent, expensive routes); **leverage technology** (forwarder software).\n\n### Real-time quote platforms (2025) 📘\n**Freightos** (instant FCL/LCL), **Flexport** (digital forwarding with breakdowns), **Maersk Spot**, **Shipwaves** (emerging markets), **Cogoport** (AI-enhanced lanes, predictive pricing); often with analytics, carbon calculators and customs support.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Technologies affecting freight costs (2023–25) ⭐⭐ 📘\n1. **Digital twins and AI forecasting** (Singapore, Antwerp) — forecast dwell time and congestion → more accurate pricing.\n2. **Blockchain smart contracts** (TradeLens, GSBN) — faster, tamper-proof billing, fewer errors/fraud.\n3. **Greener fuels and ESG metrics** — under IMO, **EU Fit for 55** and **BIMCO**: biofuel blends, methanol, wind-assisted propulsion; costs passed to shippers as carbon fees.\n\n## 11. Evolution of maritime freight rates ⭐⭐ 📘\n- **Pre-pandemic** — relatively stable with seasonal swings; **strategic alliances** kept balance and avoided overcapacity.\n- **US–China trade war (2018)** — tariffs restructured supply chains; firms shifted outside China, raising demand and rates on other routes.\n- **Pandemic (2020–21)** — demand drop and lockdowns first cut rates; then the consumer goods boom caused capacity shortages and **record rates**.\n- **Post-pandemic 2022–23** — rates stabilised but stayed high: disruptions, container shortages, port labour shortages, rising operating costs.\n- **Russia–Ukraine conflict (2022)** — sanctions and Black Sea uncertainty; diversion to longer, safer routes raised costs.\n- **Red Sea crisis (2024)** — Houthi attacks cut traffic through **Bab el-Mandeb and Suez**; ships diverted via the **Cape of Good Hope**, adding time and cost.\n- **Sustainability** — stricter rules and cleaner fuels raise costs; **EU ETS** for shipping adds cost passed to consumers.\n\n### Alliances (additional reading) 📘\nAlliances let lines optimise routes and share capacity. Example: **2M alliance (Maersk + MSC, 2015)** shaped rate structures through service consolidation and shared capacity, lowering operating costs; **dissolution** of alliances (2M ended in 2025) can create uncertainty and price swings.\n\n---\n\n## Self-test\n**Q1. List the seven cost stages in any shipment.**\nExport transport (collection), export customs clearance, handling at origin, freight, import customs clearance, handling at destination, import transport (delivery).\n\n**Q2. When should a shipper choose LCL over FCL?**\nFor shipments under about 15 m³; LCL shares a container and is priced per m³ (W/M), while FCL is better above 15 m³ because you pay for the whole box but avoid consolidation handling.\n\n**Q3. What is chargeable weight?**\nThe weight used to price the shipment: the greater of actual gross weight and volumetric (dimensional) weight. For air, volumetric kg = cm³ ÷ 6,000 (167 kg/m³); for ocean LCL, 1 m³ = 1,000 kg.\n\n**Q4. Explain BAF and CAF.**\nBAF (Bunker Adjustment Factor) adjusts freight for fuel price changes; CAF (Currency Adjustment Factor) compensates for exchange-rate fluctuations, often as a percentage of base freight plus BAF.\n\n**Q5. Differentiate NVOCC and freight forwarder.**\nAn NVOCC buys space from lines and resells it under its own bill of lading, acting as a carrier to the shipper; a freight forwarder arranges and coordinates shipments as the shipper's agent across modes, negotiating rates, preparing documents and arranging clearance.\n\n**Q6. What are the four FCL rate types?**\nSpot, by validity period, contract (volume-based) and all-in.\n\n**Q7. Why did freight rates spike in 2021 and again in 2024?**\n2021: post-COVID demand surge for goods with congestion and container shortages; 2024: Red Sea/Houthi attacks forced Suez traffic around the Cape of Good Hope, adding time, fuel and insurance.\n"}, {"slug": "ch05-air-freight-pricing-security", "label": "Air Security & Cost", "tag": "05", "group": "chapters", "title": "Air Freight Pricing & Air Cargo Security", "md": "# Air Freight Pricing & Air Cargo Security\nExam topic 5 of 8 📘 (*Air security and cost* PDF — 4 pages from the textbook *Freight transport* chapter \"Air transport\", pp. 447–450). Two parts: (A) the **basic principles of air freight price calculation** (7 steps), additional origin/destination charges, carrier liability; (B) **air cargo security** risks, controls and regulations. Numericals are on [Freight Costing Numericals](p5-freight-costing-numericals.md) (Q13–Q15, Q17–Q20). Air industry structure is on [Air Transportation](ch07-air-transportation.md).\n\n---\n\n## A. Basic principles of air freight price calculation ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n\n### Step 1 — Measure the cargo\nMeasure dimensions **in centimetres to the most extreme points** of each piece: **length × width × height = cargo volume**.\n- **Cylindrical or unusually shaped items** are charged on the volume of the **box the item would fit inside exactly**, not the true volume — all calculations are **cubic and rectilinear**.\n- If part of the cargo **sticks out** (a pipe, an aerial), the **entire additional cubic area** that the extension consumes is charged, however small the protrusion.\n\n### Step 2 — Weight or measure (whichever is higher)\nAs in sea freight, air uses a **weight or measure** system: the airline charges on the **higher** of actual weight and volume weight. Industry relationship:\n- **6 m³ = 1 metric tonne**, i.e. **6,000 cm³ = 1 kg**\n- therefore **1 m³ = 166.67 kg** of chargeable weight.\n- Some carriers use **5,000 cm³/kg** (common with couriers/express) — the method is the same, only the divisor changes.\n\n### Step 3 — Rate × chargeable weight\nAir freight = agreed **tariff rate per kg** × **chargeable weight**, which is **always rounded up to the next half kilogram**.\n**Example (PDF):** 150 kg, 120 × 80 × 50 cm → volume 0.48 m³ → 0.48 × 167 = **80 kg** (or 120 × 80 × 50 ÷ 6,000 = 80 kg) → chargeable = **150 kg** (actual weight exceeds volume equivalent).\n\n### Step 4 — Look up the tariff\nFind the agreed rate for that chargeable weight from origin airport to destination airport on the **schedule of rates** from the forwarder or airline.\n\n### Step 5 — Rate tariffs and weight breaks\nRates can be agreed in advance with forwarders or airlines. There is **always a minimum chargeable weight (minimum charge)**, and generally the **cost per kg falls as chargeable weight rises** (weight breaks). 📗 IATA/TACT structure: **M** (minimum charge), **N** (normal rate, under 45 kg), **Q45, Q100, Q300, Q500, Q1000** (quantity rates), plus **SCR** (specific commodity rates, lower, for named commodities on named routes) and **class rates** (surcharges or reductions as a % of N, e.g. live animals, valuables higher; newspapers, books lower). Always check whether a higher weight break at a lower rate gives a lower total (\"**break-point**\" rule).\n\n### Step 6 — Surcharges\nAirlines may add a **fuel surcharge**, **war-risk premium** and, today, a **security surcharge (screening)** depending on the destination and the surcharges in force at the time.\n\n### Step 7 — Other charges outside the airport-to-airport rate ⭐⭐⭐\nThe rate covers only carriage **from one airport to another**. Also budget for:\n- **At origin**: (i) transport from the consignor's premises to the forwarder's warehouse and on to the airport; (ii) **origin airport handling**; (iii) forwarder's **documentation** costs; (iv) **customs clearance** if applicable; (v) **security screening** if required.\n- **At destination**: (i) **destination airport handling**; (ii) forwarder's documentation costs; (iii) **customs clearance**, which may include **duties and taxes**; (iv) security screening if required; (v) transport from the destination airport to the **consignee**.\n\n### Carrier's liability and insurance ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n- The basic liability air carriers accept is **very limited**: the **Montreal Convention 1999 (revised 2019)** limits cargo liability to **22 Special Drawing Rights (SDR) per kg** (IMF publishes daily SDR values). At the time of the text, **1 SDR = US$1.43493**, so **22 SDR = US$31.56846 per kg**. (📗 Since 28 Dec 2024 the limit is 26 SDR/kg after ICAO's 5-yearly review. The older **Warsaw Convention 1929** limit was 250 gold francs ≈ 17 SDR/kg.)\n- This may not cover many cargoes' value, so **insure the cargo in transit** — premium = a small percentage of the **declared value**; buy full **door-to-door** insurance, because the carrier's liability covers only the flight.\n- Use an **IATA-registered cargo agent** experienced with the destination country or cargo type; check **restrictions** on what may be flown (dangerous goods).\n- **Charters**: large consignments or a whole freighter need specialist charter agents; pricing is negotiated but uses the same principles; single charters are often priced on a **round-trip basis** and can be very expensive vs standard per-kg rates.\n\n---\n\n## B. Air cargo security ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n\n### Major risks\n1. **Explosive or incendiary devices** placed inside air cargo before loading.\n2. **Undeclared or undetected hazardous material** on board.\n3. **Smuggling of contraband** inside air cargo.\n4. **Theft** from air cargo.\n5. **Hijacking or sabotage** by people with access to the aircraft.\n\n### How the risks are limited\n- Implementing or improving **cargo screening and inspection** (X-ray, explosive trace detection, sniffer dogs, physical search).\n- Improving the **physical security of air cargo facilities**.\n- **\"Known shipper\" programmes** (and regulated agents / known consignors).\n- Improving **staff security training**.\n- **Restricting access** to aircraft and air cargo facilities.\n- Using the latest security technology: **tamper-proof seals**, **blast-resistant hard-sided cargo containers (HULDs)**, **biometric data** for personnel, improved **screening systems**.\n\n### Key regulations and milestones ⭐⭐⭐\n| Year | Rule | What it requires |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Jan 2007** | **EU Regulation 831/06** | Regulated agents and airlines must obtain specific factual information (including **financial information such as bank account details**) about clients consigning air cargo; those clients must allow **compliance inspectors to audit** them — replacing self-regulation, which the EU judged unsatisfactory |\n| UK (DfT, 2009) | Screening rule | All cargo on domestic or international flights from the UK must be **screened** to a standard that reasonably ensures it carries no prohibited article (incendiary/explosive device); by the airline or a **regulated agent**; once screened, it must be stored and transported **securely** until loaded. **Unscreened = \"unknown\" cargo; screened and protected = \"known\" cargo** |\n| **2010** | **Yemen printer-cartridge bomb plot** | A consignment from Yemen to the US was found to contain a bomb — triggered tighter rules |\n| **From 2012** | **US TSA** | **All cargo loaded on passenger aircraft must be screened for explosives** (100% screening) |\n| **Since 2012** | EU | All cargo **bound for the EU** must be screened **at origin** |\n| **2012** | US–EU | **Mutual recognition** of each other's air cargo security regimes |\n| **Since 2014** | EU **ACC3** (Air Cargo or Mail Carrier operating into the Union from a Third Country Airport) | Carriers serving the EU must be ACC3-compliant: an **EU national airline/authority inspects and approves** the security screening of non-EU airlines at origin; since 2014 all carriers' cargo security at departure airports is **inspected and approved by independent validators** (EU aviation security validation) |\n\n📗 Related: ICAO **Annex 17** (security), the **secure supply chain** concept (regulated agent → known consignor → account consignor), **PLACI / ICS2** (pre-loading advance cargo information — data filed before loading so authorities can risk-assess), and in India the **BCAS** (Bureau of Civil Aviation Security) Regulated Agent regime.\n\n### Why security raises cost ⭐⭐\nScreening equipment, labour and time (security surcharges per kg), secure storage, validation/audits, training, data filing, delays for unknown cargo, and rejected or re-screened shipments. Known-shipper status lowers these costs — a trade-off between **security and speed/cost**.\n\n---\n\n## Self-test\n**Q1. State the airline industry's weight–volume relationship.**\n6 m³ = 1 tonne, i.e. 6,000 cm³ = 1 kg, so 1 m³ = 166.67 kg chargeable; some carriers use 5,000 cm³/kg.\n\n**Q2. How is an unusually shaped item (a cylinder or a box with an aerial) measured?**\nOn the rectilinear box it fits in exactly, including the full cubic space consumed by any protrusion; true volume is not used.\n\n**Q3. What charges apply beyond the airport-to-airport rate?**\nOrigin: pick-up and transfer to forwarder and airport, origin handling, forwarder documentation, export customs, security screening. Destination: airport handling, documentation, import customs with duties/taxes, screening, delivery to consignee.\n\n**Q4. Why should air cargo be insured even though the airline is liable?**\nMontreal Convention liability is limited to 22 SDR/kg (≈US$31.57/kg at the textbook rate), often far below the cargo value, and it covers only the flight; door-to-door insurance covers the full value and all legs.\n\n**Q5. What are the five main air cargo security risks?**\nExplosive/incendiary devices in cargo, undeclared hazardous materials, smuggling of contraband, theft, hijacking or sabotage by insiders.\n\n**Q6. Explain \"known\" and \"unknown\" cargo.**\nKnown cargo has been screened (by an airline or regulated agent, or comes from a validated known consignor) and kept secure until loading; unknown cargo is unscreened and must be screened before it can fly.\n\n**Q7. What is ACC3?**\nThe EU's designation for carriers flying cargo into the EU from third-country airports; since 2014 they must have their origin screening inspected/validated and approved, so EU-bound cargo is screened at origin to EU standards.\n"}, {"slug": "ch06-marine-insurance-general-average", "label": "Insurance & General Average", "tag": "06", "group": "chapters", "title": "Marine Insurance, General Average & Contracts of Carriage", "md": "# Marine Insurance, General Average & Contracts of Carriage\nExam topic 6 of 8 📘 (*Insurance and contract problems* — eight numericals on **General Average** and one on a **cargo insurance claim**). The course file has only problems, so this page builds the theory you need to answer the \"explain\" parts of those questions: insurance principles, policy types, Institute Cargo Clauses, types of loss, **General Average vs Particular Average**, the **York-Antwerp Rules**, the role of the **average adjuster**, H&M and P&I insurance, and the carriage-contract regimes that put GA clauses in bills of lading. Full solutions: [Insurance & General Average Problems](p4-insurance-general-average-problems.md).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Marine insurance — basics ⭐⭐⭐\n**Marine insurance** is a contract by which the insurer undertakes to **indemnify** the assured against **marine losses** — losses incident to a marine adventure (Indian **Marine Insurance Act, 1963**, based on the UK MIA 1906). It covers the **hull** (ship), **cargo** and **freight** at risk, and liabilities.\n\n### The three interests in a marine adventure ⭐⭐⭐\n| Interest | Who owns it | Insurance |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Ship (hull & machinery)** | Shipowner | **Hull & Machinery (H&M)** policy |\n| **Cargo** | Cargo owner(s) — shipper/consignee depending on Incoterm | **Marine cargo** policy (ICC A/B/C) |\n| **Freight (at risk)** | Usually the carrier/shipowner (freight payable on delivery is lost if the cargo is lost) | Freight insurance / included in H&M |\nPlus **liabilities** to third parties (collision, pollution, crew injury, cargo claims) — covered by **P&I (Protection & Indemnity) clubs**, mutual associations of shipowners.\n\n### Principles of marine insurance ⭐⭐⭐\n1. **Utmost good faith (uberrimae fidei)** — disclose all material facts (cargo nature, packing, route).\n2. **Insurable interest** — the assured must stand to lose financially; in cargo, it must exist **at the time of loss** (who bears risk depends on the Incoterm).\n3. **Indemnity** — restore the assured to its pre-loss financial position, no profit (agreed value in valued policies, usually CIF + 10%).\n4. **Subrogation** — after paying, the insurer takes over the assured's rights against the party at fault (carrier, stevedore).\n5. **Contribution** — if insured with several insurers, they share the loss.\n6. **Proximate cause** — the loss must be proximately caused by an insured peril.\n7. **Warranties** — conditions that must be strictly complied with (e.g. seaworthiness, legality).\n8. **Mitigation (sue and labour)** — the assured must take reasonable steps to minimise loss; costs are recoverable.\n\n### Types of cargo policies 📗\n**Voyage policy** (one voyage), **time policy** (a period, usual for hulls), **mixed policy**, **valued** (value agreed) vs **unvalued** policy, **open cover / floating policy** (all shipments over a period declared as made — used by regular exporters), **blanket policy**, **warehouse-to-warehouse** cover (transit clause: from leaving the origin warehouse until delivery at the final warehouse, max 60 days after discharge).\n\n### Institute Cargo Clauses (ICC 2009) ⭐⭐⭐\n| | ICC (A) | ICC (B) | ICC (C) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Basis | **All risks** subject to exclusions | Named perils (wider) | Named perils (**minimum**) |\n| Fire/explosion, vessel stranded/sunk/capsized, collision, overturning of land conveyance, discharge at port of distress | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ |\n| **General average sacrifice**, **jettison** | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ |\n| Earthquake, volcano, lightning | ✔ | ✔ | ✘ |\n| Washing overboard; entry of sea/lake/river water into vessel, container or storage | ✔ | ✔ | ✘ |\n| Total loss of a package dropped during loading/unloading | ✔ | ✔ | ✘ |\n| Theft, pilferage, non-delivery, malicious damage, rough handling, rain, etc. | ✔ | ✘ | ✘ |\n| Used by Incoterms | **CIP** minimum (2020) | — | **CIF** minimum |\n**Exclusions in all**: wilful misconduct, ordinary leakage/wear and tear, **insufficient packing**, **inherent vice**, **delay**, insolvency of the owners/operators of the vessel, nuclear; and war and strikes unless the **Institute War Clauses** and **Institute Strikes Clauses** are added. Specialised: **ICC (Air)**, **Institute Frozen Food Clauses**, Commodity Trade Clauses. **GA contributions and salvage charges are recoverable under all three ICCs.**\n\n---\n\n## 2. Types of marine losses ⭐⭐⭐\n```\nMarine losses\n├── Total loss\n│   ├── Actual total loss (goods destroyed, irretrievably lost, or ceased to be the thing insured)\n│   └── Constructive total loss (CTL) — loss reasonably abandoned because saving/repair would cost more than its value\n│       (assured gives \"notice of abandonment\")\n└── Partial loss\n    ├── Particular Average (PA) — accidental partial loss falling only on the interest that suffered it\n    ├── General Average (GA) — intentional sacrifice/expenditure for the common safety, shared by all interests\n    └── Particular charges / sue & labour, salvage charges\n```\n\n### General Average ⭐⭐⭐\n**General Average** is a principle of maritime law (dating to the **Rhodian Sea Law**, c. 800 BC — \"what is thrown overboard for the benefit of all shall be made good by the contribution of all\") under which a **loss arising from an extraordinary sacrifice or expenditure, intentionally and reasonably made or incurred, to preserve the ship and cargo from a common peril**, is **shared rateably** by all parties whose property was saved — ship, cargo and freight — in proportion to their **saved (contributory) values**.\n\n**Conditions for a valid GA act** (York-Antwerp Rule A & MIA s.66) ⭐⭐⭐:\n1. A **real and imminent peril** threatening the **whole adventure** (not just one interest).\n2. The sacrifice or expenditure is **extraordinary** (beyond the ship's ordinary duties/costs).\n3. It is **voluntary/intentional** (ordered, usually by the master) — not accidental.\n4. It is **reasonable** (prudent in the circumstances).\n5. It is made for the **common safety** of ship, cargo and freight.\n6. **Something is saved** (success: the property must be preserved, at least in part).\n7. The loss is the **direct consequence** of the GA act.\n\n**Examples of GA sacrifices**: jettison of cargo; flooding holds/containers to fight fire (water damage to cargo); cutting away masts/rigging; deliberately beaching/stranding the ship; damage to engines when forcing a stranded ship off.\n**Examples of GA expenditure**: salvage services for common safety; towage after breakdown; port-of-refuge costs (entering, discharging, storing, reloading cargo); emergency/temporary repairs needed to complete the voyage safely; hiring security escorts against an imminent pirate attack; lightering (transferring cargo to another vessel) to refloat; fuel and crew wages during detention at a port of refuge; firefighting expenses.\n\n### Particular Average ⭐⭐⭐\nAn **accidental partial loss** caused by an insured peril that falls **only on the owner of the damaged property** — there is no sacrifice for the common good and no sharing. Recovered from that owner's **own insurer** (cargo policy or H&M), subject to the policy terms and excess. Example: heavy weather damages some containers of TVs; a fire damages part of the cargo before any GA act; engine failure itself (the breakdown, as distinct from towage to save the voyage).\n\n### General Average vs Particular Average ⭐⭐⭐\n| Basis | General Average | Particular Average |\n|---|---|---|\n| Nature | **Intentional/voluntary** sacrifice or expenditure | **Accidental** loss from a peril |\n| Purpose | **Common safety** of the whole adventure | No common purpose; just a mishap |\n| Who bears | **All interests** (ship, cargo, freight) contribute rateably | Only the **owner of the damaged property** |\n| Basis of sharing | Contributory (saved) values | None (own loss) |\n| Recovery | GA adjustment; contributions recoverable from cargo/H&M insurers | Owner's own insurance claim |\n| Documents | GA bond, GA guarantee/deposit, adjuster's statement | Survey report, claim |\n| Governed by | York-Antwerp Rules (via B/L & charter party) | Policy terms, MIA |\n| Example | Jettison of wheat to stabilise ship in cyclone | Wheat damaged by seawater entering a cracked hold |\n\n---\n\n## 3. The York-Antwerp Rules (YAR) ⭐⭐\nInternational rules for adjusting GA, first agreed **1864 (Glasgow)**, named after York (1864) and Antwerp (1877); revised **1890, 1924, 1950, 1974, 1994, 2004, 2016** (current: **YAR 2016**, drafted by the Comité Maritime International). They are **not law**: they apply because the **bill of lading, charter party and insurance policies incorporate them** (\"General Average to be adjusted according to the York-Antwerp Rules 2016\"). Structure: a **Rule of Interpretation**, a **Rule Paramount** (only reasonably made sacrifices/expenditures allowed), **lettered Rules A–G** (general principles: Rule A definition; Rule C only direct losses; Rule D GA rights not affected by fault, though fault may give claims later; Rule E burden of proof on the claimant; Rule G adjustment at values at the time and place where the adventure ends) and **numbered Rules I–XXIII** (specific items: jettison, fire extinguishing, port of refuge, temporary repairs, salvage, commission, interest).\n\n---\n\n## 4. The General Average calculation method ⭐⭐⭐\n1. **Identify GA items** — sacrifices (value of cargo/ship property sacrificed) and expenditures (salvage, towage, cranes, security, temporary repairs). Exclude particular-average items and ordinary costs.\n2. **Total GA loss** = Σ GA sacrifices + Σ GA expenditures.\n3. **Total contributory value (CV)** = ship value + cargo values + freight at risk (each at its **arrived/saved value**, i.e. as at the end of the voyage, **plus amounts made good** for sacrificed property). In course problems the given values are taken as the contributory values.\n4. **GA contribution rate (%)** = Total GA loss ÷ Total contributory value × 100.\n5. **Each party's contribution** = its contributory value × GA rate.\n6. **Settlement (net position)** = amount **made good** to the party (its sacrifice/expenditure allowed in GA) − its **contribution**. Positive → receives; negative → pays. The sum of net positions is always **zero** — a useful check.\n\n> **Example logic**: if the master jettisons $250,000 of wheat, the wheat owner is *made good* $250,000 but also pays its own share of the GA (as a saved-value participant), so its net receipt is $250,000 − its contribution.\n\n### The General Average adjuster ⭐⭐⭐\nAn independent specialist (average adjuster) appointed by the shipowner to:\n1. Investigate the casualty and decide which losses and expenses qualify as GA under the YAR and the contract.\n2. **Collect GA security** from cargo interests **before cargo is released**: a **GA bond** (Lloyd's Average Bond, signed by the consignee — undertaking to pay its share) plus a **GA guarantee** from the cargo insurer, or a **cash deposit**; plus commercial invoices to value the cargo.\n3. Obtain surveys and values (ship's sound value, cargo's arrived values, freight at risk).\n4. Compute **contributory values**, **GA rate**, each party's **contribution**, the amounts **made good**, interest and commission.\n5. Issue the **GA adjustment (statement)** and settle balances among parties/insurers.\nGA adjustments can take months or years; until security is posted, the shipowner has a **lien on the cargo**.\n\n### Insurance and GA settlement ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Cargo owner with marine cargo insurance**: the insurer issues the **GA guarantee** so the cargo is released quickly; it pays the **GA contribution** (and salvage charges) under all ICCs (A/B/C), up to the insured value, and pays the owner's **sacrifice** (claimed from the insurer, which then recovers via the GA adjustment — subrogation). If the cargo is **under-insured**, the insurer pays only proportionately (average clause).\n- **Shipowner with H&M insurance**: the H&M insurer covers the ship's GA sacrifices and the ship's proportion of GA contribution, plus particular-average damage to the hull/machinery (repairs), subject to deductibles. P&I covers liabilities (e.g. cargo claims if the ship was unseaworthy).\n- **Uninsured cargo owner**: must post a **cash deposit** (often 10–50% of cargo value, or more) or a bank guarantee before taking delivery; pays its contribution itself; receives nothing for its own particular-average losses; faces delays, storage and demurrage, and working capital locked up — a major reason never to ship uninsured.\n- **Fault**: GA is shared even if the casualty was caused by the ship's fault (YAR Rule D); but if the carrier was **actionably at fault** (e.g. unseaworthy ship due to lack of due diligence), cargo can **refuse to contribute** or recover its contribution (Hague-Visby Art. III/IV; the *New Jason* clause in US trades).\n\n### Why GA clauses are in bills of lading and policies ⭐⭐⭐\nThe B/L is the **contract of carriage**; the GA clause makes all cargo interests agree in advance that GA will be **adjusted under the York-Antwerp Rules**, at a named place, and gives the carrier a **lien** on cargo for GA security. This ensures: equitable sharing of losses incurred for everyone's benefit; the master can act decisively in an emergency without negotiating with hundreds of cargo owners; a uniform international basis across different laws; quick release of cargo against security; insurers cover GA contributions so traders are protected; and legal certainty in multimodal, multi-party container shipping (a mega-ship may carry 20,000 TEU for thousands of cargo owners — e.g. *Maersk Honam* fire 2018, *Ever Given* grounding in Suez 2021, *MSC Flaminia* 2012 and *X-Press Pearl* 2021, all declared GA).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Salvage ⭐⭐\n**Salvage** is a voluntary service by a third party (salvor) that saves maritime property in danger; the reward is payable only on success (\"**no cure, no pay**\") under **Lloyd's Open Form (LOF)** and the **International Convention on Salvage 1989**; the reward is based on the saved value, skill, risk and danger. Salvage paid for the **common safety** is allowed in GA (YAR Rule VI). **SCOPIC** clause compensates salvors for environmental protection efforts even if property isn't saved.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Contracts of carriage and liability regimes ⭐⭐\n| Regime | Year | Scope / key feature |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Hague Rules** | 1924 | Carrier's minimum duties (seaworthiness by due diligence, care of cargo) and 17 defences (incl. **nautical fault**, fire); limit £100/package |\n| **Hague-Visby Rules** | 1968/1979 | Limit **666.67 SDR per package or 2 SDR per kg**, whichever is higher; container clause — India's **Carriage of Goods by Sea Act 1925** follows Hague(-Visby) |\n| **Hamburg Rules** | 1978 | Removed nautical-fault defence; 835 SDR/package or 2.5 SDR/kg |\n| **Rotterdam Rules** | 2008 | Door-to-door (maritime plus), e-documents; not yet in force |\n| **Montreal Convention (air)** | 1999 | 22 SDR/kg (26 SDR from Dec 2024) |\n| **CMR (road, Europe)** | 1956 | 8.33 SDR/kg |\nDocuments: **bill of lading** (receipt, evidence of contract, **document of title**), sea waybill (not a title document), charter parties (voyage, time, bareboat), air waybill (not negotiable), multimodal transport document (India: **Multimodal Transportation of Goods Act 1993**).\n\n### Contract terms that link to insurance\n- **Incoterms** decide who must insure (seller under CIF/CIP) and who holds insurable interest at the time of loss.\n- **Insured value** = CIF + 10% normally.\n- **Claims procedure**: notify carrier in writing (Hague-Visby: within **3 days** for non-apparent damage; Montreal: **14 days** for damage, 21 days for delay), joint survey, keep packing, file claim with documents (policy, invoice, packing list, B/L/AWB, survey report, letter of subrogation), recover from carrier within the time bar (**1 year** sea, 2 years air).\n- **Deductible/excess**: the first part of each claim borne by the assured (e.g. 3% of value) — reduces premium.\n\n---\n\n## Self-test\n**Q1. Define General Average and state its essential conditions.**\nGA is an extraordinary sacrifice or expenditure intentionally and reasonably made or incurred for the common safety, to preserve property in a common maritime adventure from peril; the loss is shared by all saved interests in proportion to their values. Conditions: real, imminent common peril; extraordinary; intentional; reasonable; for common safety; property saved; loss is a direct consequence.\n\n**Q2. Differentiate GA and PA.**\nGA is intentional and for common safety, shared by ship, cargo and freight in proportion to contributory values; PA is an accidental partial loss borne only by the owner of the damaged property and claimed from its own insurer.\n\n**Q3. What is the formula for the GA contribution rate?**\nGA rate = total GA loss ÷ total contributory value (ship + cargo + freight at risk) × 100; each party pays its CV × rate.\n\n**Q4. What do the York-Antwerp Rules do?**\nThey are the international rules for adjusting GA (latest YAR 2016), applied through incorporation in bills of lading, charter parties and insurance policies.\n\n**Q5. Who is a GA adjuster and what security does cargo provide?**\nAn independent specialist who determines what is GA, collects security, values the interests and prepares the adjustment. Cargo provides a GA bond plus an insurer's GA guarantee or a cash deposit before release.\n\n**Q6. Name the three Institute Cargo Clauses and which Incoterms use them.**\nICC (A) all risks — minimum for CIP; ICC (B) wider named perils; ICC (C) minimum named perils — minimum for CIF.\n\n**Q7. What happens to an uninsured cargo owner in a GA?**\nIt must pay its contribution and post cash security before release, absorbs its own losses, suffers delays and locked-up cash, and recovers its own sacrifice only through the GA adjustment, net of its contribution.\n"}, {"slug": "ch07-air-transportation", "label": "Air Transportation", "tag": "07", "group": "chapters", "title": "International Air Transportation", "md": "# International Air Transportation\nExam topic 7 of 8 📘 (*Air Transportation* PPT, 30 slides). Covers: what air freight is; types of air cargo services and routing; India's AAI and the IATA; terms (ULDs, AWB, HAWB, MAWB, hubs & spokes); advantages and disadvantages; what can and cannot fly; characteristics; industry structure (IATA, CASS, airlines, lessors, cargo agents, airport authorities); price factors; IE codes; the six types of services; aircraft types; the 15-step \"how air freight works\" flow; top cargo airlines and airports; and IATA manuals. Pricing and security are on [Air Freight Pricing & Security](ch05-air-freight-pricing-security.md).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Introduction ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n**Air freight** refers to goods moved by loading them in an aircraft and sending them to their destination via aerial routes. It is popular because it **ensures passage for goods to anywhere in the world an aircraft can fly to and land**. E-commerce customers (Amazon, Alibaba, eBay) increasingly expect swift, expedited delivery, which rapid air freight allows.\n\n### Types of air cargo services (by speed) 📘\n- **Express air service** — fastest, time-definite (next-flight-out, door to door).\n- **Standard air service** — normal scheduled service, a few days.\n- **Deferred air service** — slower and cheaper; the forwarder holds cargo to consolidate and fly when space is cheap.\n\n### Routing 📘\n- **Direct routing** — gives the shipper **more supply chain flexibility**; the extra **speed to market** benefits both production and distribution.\n- **Indirect routing** (via hubs/transfer points) — **most cost-effective** method in many cases and lets customers send freight to some of the **most remote areas** of the world.\n\n### Institutions 📘\n- **Airports Authority of India (AAI)** manages air cargo logistics in India; its **Department of Cargo** establishes, manages and develops cargo terminals at airports. 📗 (Many terminals are now run by AAI Cargo Logistics & Allied Services Co. — AAICLAS — and private operators such as Menzies, Celebi, WFS at PPP airports.)\n- **International Air Transport Association (IATA)** — a **non-political, commercial regulatory organisation** with powers to regulate the acts of its members. Main objective: **safe, regular and economical air transport** for the benefit of people worldwide, including threshold tariff rates.\n- 📗 **ICAO** (International Civil Aviation Organization, UN agency, Chicago Convention 1944) sets government standards; **DGCA** regulates civil aviation in India; **BCAS** handles aviation security; **CBIC/Customs** clears cargo (Air Cargo Complexes).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Terms in air logistics ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n- **Unit Load Devices (ULDs)** — \"the shipping containers of the skies\"; many are shaped to the **aircraft hold** and so are specific to certain aircraft. **IATA has a system for identifying ULDs** (📗 e.g. code *AKE 12345 AA*: type, serial number, owner — *AKE* = LD3 container). Types: **pallets** (with nets) and **containers** (LD3, LD7, etc.). ULD regulations matter because ULDs are a leading cause of ground damage to aircraft.\n- **Air Waybill (AWB)** — a **contract to transport goods by air**, issued by the carrier airline; it **limits the airline's liability** and details the goods carried. 📗 An AWB is **not a document of title** (non-negotiable), unlike an ocean B/L; it has an 11-digit number (3-digit airline prefix + 8 digits), 3 originals and several copies, and serves as receipt, contract evidence, freight bill, customs declaration support and insurance certificate (if insured).\n- **House Air Waybill (HAWB)** — issued by a **freight forwarding company entitled to do so** (e.g. an **IATA cargo agent**, which has the relevant insurances and, as issuer, assumes liability like an airline does under an AWB). Used mostly when **small shipments are consolidated** by the forwarder for onward shipment.\n- **Master Air Waybill (MAWB)** — the contract between the **freight forwarder and the carrier airline**; it details all the HAWBs consolidated in the shipment.\n- **Other documentation** — packing lists, commercial invoices, certificates of origin and other cargo- or country-specific documents may be required at origin and destination airports, by airlines, security services and customs.\n- **Air hubs and spokes** — because air transport has **high unit costs** (relatively low cargo volumes compared with other modes, long distances and high aircraft operating costs), carriers use **hubs** located strategically around the world near air cargo markets, employed on **major trade routes** (📗 e.g. FedEx at Memphis, UPS at Louisville, DHL at Leipzig/Cincinnati, Emirates at Dubai).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Advantages and disadvantages of air freight ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n| Advantages | Disadvantages |\n|---|---|\n| **Speed** — better customer service, increased production | **Risk** — human error, extreme weather, turbulence, cargo fires, hazardous materials, regulatory compliance issues, strikes, pandemics, natural disasters, political upheaval, conflicts, economic downturns |\n| **Reliability** — security against theft, less damage | **High operational costs** |\n| **Accuracy** — fast track & trace, loss prevention | **Limited capacity** — aircraft size, number of cargo planes, flight schedules, peak-season surges, geopolitical issues |\n| **Control** — reduced storage costs, manage perishability | **Dependent on weather** |\n| **Scheduling** — direct shipping and indirect shipping options | **Unfit for specific products** |\n\n### Products unfit for (or restricted in) air transport 📘\nFlammable liquids (gasoline, alcohol, acetone, some paints); flammable solids (matches, lighters, some powders); flammable gases (propane, butane, oxygen); explosives (fireworks, flares, dynamite, ammunition); aerosol cans; cigarette lighters and refills; pesticides, chemicals and infectious materials; radioactive materials (uranium, plutonium); corrosives (acids, mercury); **lithium batteries**; magnetised materials; perishables needing temperature control; dry ice, specimens or samples; detergents, bleaches, drain/oven cleaners; machinery with internal combustion engines. 📗 Many of these can fly as **dangerous goods** if classified, packed, marked, labelled and documented per the **IATA DGR** (9 hazard classes) with a Shipper's Declaration; some are forbidden entirely.\n\n### What moves by air 📘\n- **General cargo** — clothes, books, electronics, leather products, gems and jewellery: non-perishable, non-hazardous, minimal handling → easy and cost-effective by air.\n- **Urgent/high-value** — radiopharmaceuticals, high-value technology, scientific equipment, high-value commodities.\n- **Perishable food** — fresh fish, meat, vegetables, exotic fruits, cut flowers.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Characteristics of air transport ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n1. **Fastest and most expensive** mode — used for expensive/important cargo and passengers.\n2. Manufacture and operation of aircraft need detailed arrangements: **hangars, landing, fuelling, maintenance**.\n3. Connects places at **long distances**, spreading economic activity to them.\n4. Aircraft fly to **pre-decided schedules**.\n5. Aircraft fly through **specified air corridors**.\n6. **Not suitable for heavy goods and machinery** for commercial purposes.\n7. Suitable for **relief operations** in calamities and disasters.\n8. Air freight is usually **4–5 times the cost of road** transport and about **12–16 times the cost of sea** transport.\n9. Can help create **competitive advantage**.\n10. Can be used for **short shelf-life products** in a cost-effective manner.\n11. **Customs clearance** needs smooth integration of customs processes, inspection, cargo handling and warehousing so cargo moves out of the airport easily.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Structure of the industry ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n- **IATA and trade associations** — set standards of operation (safety, security, training, ULDs and many more) and provide services such as **CASS (Cargo Accounts Settlement System)**: a web-based system that lets airlines and freight forwarders **make only one payment a month** to, or receive one payment from, airlines/cargo agents — greatly reducing paperwork.\n- **Airlines** — own or lease and operate the aircraft carrying passengers and cargo (📗 combination carriers vs all-cargo carriers vs integrators like FedEx, UPS, DHL).\n- **Lessors** — own aircraft and lease them to airlines when owning is unfavourable for the airline.\n- **Cargo agents** — freight forwarders **licensed by IATA** to handle freight for customers; IATA sets standards, ensures they are insured and allows them to issue **HAWBs**.\n- **Airport authorities** — own or lease and operate the airport infrastructure.\n- 📗 Also: ground handlers/cargo terminal operators, customs brokers, GSAs (general sales agents), trucking (road feeder services).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Factors affecting the price of air freight ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n1. **Density of the product** — weight per unit volume; **low-density products are priced by volume, high-density by weight**; density affects available loading space.\n2. **Quantity of cargo** — higher volume → higher total transport cost (though lower per-kg rate at weight breaks).\n3. **Distance of travel** — drives variable costs (maintenance, fuel).\n4. **Shape of the product** — affects load handling; odd shapes need special care, machinery and vehicles.\n5. **Type of product** — determines storage and transport conditions; perishables need refrigerated freighters; pharma needs temperature and humidity control.\nPlus (pricing chapter): route supply and demand, forwarder buying power, currency, fuel and war-risk surcharges, security screening and transit-time level.\n\n---\n\n## 7. IE Code (Importer-Exporter Code) ⭐⭐ 📘📗\nThe PPT's IE CODES slides: in India, **no person or firm can import or export without an IEC** (except exempted categories). It is issued by the **Directorate General of Foreign Trade (DGFT)** under the Foreign Trade (Development & Regulation) Act 1992; it is a **10-digit code, now the same as the firm's PAN**; applied for online on the DGFT portal (fee ₹500); **lifetime validity**, but must be **updated annually (April–June)** or it is deactivated. It is quoted on shipping bills, bills of entry, AWBs and for bank remittances, and is needed for export benefits (RoDTEP, drawback). Exempt: personal-use imports, certain government bodies and charitable organisations.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Types of air services ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n1. **Air mail services** — the only option for shipping letters internationally.\n2. **Express airfreight services** — cargo with a **guaranteed predetermined delivery date** (DHL, FedEx, UPS).\n3. **Scheduled airfreight services** — offered on a **routine basis**; highly reliable and efficient, resulting in **relatively low-cost** delivery.\n4. **Charter airfreight services** — cargo that can only go on a **charter aircraft (private terminal)** because it is **too heavy, too bulky, or its destination is not served by scheduled service**.\n5. **Leased cargo aircraft services** — **\"dry\" lease** (aircraft only), **\"damp\" lease** (aircraft + some crew), **\"wet\" lease** or **ACMI lease** (Aircraft, Crew, Maintenance and Insurance provided by the lessor; lessee pays fuel, landing and handling).\n6. **Airfreight forwarder services** — forwarders contract with air carriers, **consolidate shipments, buy space on flights**, and arrange **intermodal surface transport**; some provide full supply chain management services.\n\n## 9. Types of aircraft ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n- **Passenger aircraft** — spare (belly) capacity used for expensive or important cargo, sometimes carried by an on-board courier. (📗 Belly cargo carries about half of world air cargo.)\n- **Quick-change (QC) aircraft** — quickly converted from all-cargo to all-passenger service using **palletised seat sections** (passengers by day, cargo by night).\n- **Combination (combi) aircraft** — carries **both cargo and passengers on the main deck** at the same time.\n- **Air freighters (cargo aircraft)** — dedicated to carrying cargo (📗 e.g. Boeing 747-8F, 777F, A330P2F; nose-door freighters for outsized cargo; Antonov An-124/An-225 for heavy charters).\n\n---\n\n## 10. How air freight works — the 15 steps ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n(Infographic by International Cargo Express.)\n1. **Source a supplier** that can comply with air transport.\n2. **Decide on Incoterms** (international commercial terms) — typically FCA, CPT or CIP.\n3. **Engage a freight forwarder.**\n4. **Provide commercial documents** (invoice, packing list, certificates).\n5. **Define the volumetric ratio** of your cargo (to calculate the cost of the air freight — chargeable weight).\n6. **Pack your cargo** complying with all **air freight security regulations**.\n7. **Review your route and airline options** with your freight forwarder.\n8. **Provide the documentation** to your forwarder to arrange your shipment.\n9. Once your flight is booked, an **air waybill** is produced and provided to you once your goods have departed.\n10. **Goods in transit.**\n11. The forwarder **commences customs clearance** for your cargo **prior to its arrival** (pre-arrival filing).\n12. **Cargo arrives** at destination.\n13. The **cargo terminal operator retrieves the cargo** from the aircraft and **stores it under customs bond** awaiting clearance and collection.\n14. The **transport operator collects** the cargo (you may need a tail-lift truck).\n15. The cargo is **delivered to its final destination**.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Top cargo airlines and airports (2019) ⭐ 📘\n**Top ten cargo airlines, 2019 (millions of FTKs — freight tonne-kilometres):** 1 FedEx 17,503 · 2 Qatar Airways 13,024 · 3 UPS Airlines 12,842 · 4 Emirates 12,052 · 5 Cathay Pacific 10,930 · 6 Korean Air 7,412 · 7 Lufthansa 7,226 · 8 Cargolux 7,180 · 9 Turkish Airlines 7,029 · 10 China Southern 6,825.\n\n**Top fifteen cargo airports, 2019 (metric tonnes loaded, '000s):** 1 Hong Kong (HKG) 4,809 · 2 Memphis (MEM) 4,323 · 3 Shanghai (PVG) 3,634 · 4 Louisville (SDF) 2,790 · 5 Incheon/Seoul (ICN) 2,764 · 6 Anchorage (ANC) 2,745 · 7 Dubai (DXB) 2,515 · 8 Tokyo Narita (NRT) 2,104 · 9 Paris CDG 2,102 · 10 Miami (MIA) 2,092 · 11 Los Angeles (LAX) 2,092 · 12 Frankfurt (FRA) 2,091 · 13 Singapore (SIN) 2,057 · 14 Beijing (PEK) 1,958 · 15 Guangzhou (CAN) 1,922.\nRead these as: integrator hubs (Memphis = FedEx, Louisville = UPS), the trans-Pacific technical stop (Anchorage), and Asian/Middle-East gateways dominate. 📗 India's largest cargo airports are Mumbai (BOM) and Delhi (DEL).\n\n---\n\n## 12. IATA — vision, mission, manuals ⭐⭐ 📘\n- **IATA** is the trade association for the world's airlines; it supports many areas of aviation activity and helps formulate industry policy on critical aviation issues. Founded 1945 (Havana), HQ Montreal.\n- **Vision**: working together to shape the future growth of a safe, secure and sustainable air transport industry that connects and enriches our world. **Mission**: to represent, lead and serve the airline industry.\n- **Numbers (PPT)**: **290 member airlines**, **400 strategic partners**, **100,000 IATA-accredited agents**, plus standards, manuals and guidelines.\n- **Services by area**: airlines, airports, air traffic management, freight forwarders, safety, governments & policy makers, ground handlers, industry partners, travel agents; priorities include environment and sustainability, diversity, commercial, effective operations.\n- **What are IATA manuals?** The regulations needed for preparing, handling or accepting different types of goods — live animals, airport handling, air cargo tariffs and rules.\n- **How are they developed?** From recommendations of governing bodies such as **ICAO** and standards built by member-airline delegates and industry associations collaborating with IATA.\n- **Why updated every year?** Over **350 changes** are made each year to keep manuals compliant with the newest regulations and trends.\n- **Why use IATA manuals?** Neutral organisation; 75+ years of experience; standardised guidelines; for and by the industry; updated regularly.\n- **Five key benefits** (users surveyed): **experience reduction in errors 87%**, **avoid fines on air cargo shipments 81%**, **avoid customs delays from incorrect labelling or documentation 79%**, **reduced risk of aircraft damage 79%**, **speed up shipment processing 70%**.\n\n### IATA manuals and regulations (knowledge only) 📘\n| Area | Manual | What it covers |\n|---|---|---|\n| Dangerous goods | **DGR** — Dangerous Goods Regulations | Safe, efficient carriage of dangerous goods; used daily by airlines, forwarders, ground handlers, shippers |\n| | **LBSR** — Lithium Battery Shipping Regulations | Packaging, labelling, marking of lithium batteries; failure can cause fire |\n| | **ISSR** — Infectious Substance Shipping Regulations | Plain-language guide to shipping infectious substances (lab samples) |\n| Special cargo | **PCR** — Perishable Cargo Regulations | Best practice for perishables; cold-chain failures often from poor documents, labels, packaging |\n| | **TCR** — Temperature Control Regulations | Shipping temperature-sensitive (healthcare) products |\n| | **LAR** — Live Animals Regulations | Animals arrive in good health |\n| | Compassionate Transportation manual | Human remains — funeral operators, airlines, forwarders |\n| Cargo operations | **ICHM** — IATA Cargo Handling Manual | No. 1 source for cargo operations standards |\n| | **ULDR** — ULD Regulations | ULDs are the leading cause of ground damage; safe ULD handling |\n| | **PLACI** — Pre-loading Advance Cargo Information | Harmonised advance cargo data filing (US, then EU, UK, Canada) |\n| Ground operations | **AHM** — Airport Handling Manual | Industry policies/standards above and below the wing |\n| | **IGOM** — IATA Ground Operations Manual | Standardised ground and ramp procedures |\n| | **BRM** — Baggage Reference Manual | Best practice to reduce mishandled bags |\n\n---\n\n## Self-test\n**Q1. Differentiate AWB, MAWB and HAWB.**\nAWB: the airline's contract of carriage and receipt, limiting its liability; non-negotiable. MAWB: the AWB issued by the airline to the forwarder for a consolidated shipment, listing the HAWBs. HAWB: issued by an IATA forwarder to each individual shipper in a consolidation; the forwarder assumes carrier-like liability.\n\n**Q2. What is a ULD?**\nA unit load device — a pallet or container shaped to the aircraft hold, identified by IATA's coding system, used to load cargo efficiently and safely.\n\n**Q3. Explain the hub-and-spoke system in air cargo.**\nBecause air cargo has high unit costs (low volumes, long distances, high aircraft costs), carriers feed cargo from many origins (spokes) into strategic hubs and consolidate it onto larger flights on major trade routes, raising load factors and reach.\n\n**Q4. List five advantages and five disadvantages of air freight.**\nAdvantages: speed, reliability/security, accuracy (track and trace), control (less storage, perishables), scheduling flexibility. Disadvantages: risk, high operating cost, limited capacity, weather dependence, unfit for some products.\n\n**Q5. Name the six types of air services.**\nAir mail, express airfreight, scheduled airfreight, charter airfreight, leased cargo aircraft (dry, damp, wet/ACMI), airfreight forwarder services.\n\n**Q6. What is CASS?**\nIATA's Cargo Accounts Settlement System: a web-based billing and settlement system that lets airlines and agents make or receive one payment a month instead of many.\n\n**Q7. What factors affect air freight price?**\nDensity, quantity, distance, shape and type of product (plus route demand, fuel and security surcharges, transit time).\n\n**Q8. How much more expensive is air than road and sea?**\nAbout 4–5 times road and 12–16 times sea freight.\n\n**Q9. What is an IEC?**\nThe 10-digit Importer-Exporter Code (same as PAN) issued by DGFT, mandatory for importing or exporting from India, valid for life but updated annually.\n"}, {"slug": "ch08-transportation-kpis-scorecards", "label": "KPIs & Carrier Scorecards", "tag": "08", "group": "chapters", "title": "Transportation KPIs, Freight Terms & Carrier Scorecards", "md": "# Transportation KPIs, Freight Terms & Carrier Scorecards\nExam topic 8 of 8 📘 — the **KPIs book case**. The zip has the book cases (*Case 11.1 Vibrant Video* and *Case 11.2 Bob's Custom BBQs*, from the transportation chapter of Coyle, Langley, Novack & Gibson, *Supply Chain Management: A Logistics Perspective*, Ch. 11) and the **pre-read material**: Table 11.4 (FOB freight terms), 11.5 (modal capabilities), 11.6 (performance ranking of modes), 11.7 (common transportation metrics) and 11.8 (transportation performance scorecard). This page teaches every table; the two cases are solved on [KPI Book Cases](p6-kpi-book-cases.md). Warehouse/outsourcing KPIs (order accuracy, inventory accuracy, damage and complaint rate) are in [Outsourcing Problems Que 13](p1-outsourcing-contract-problems.md).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Freight terms: FOB origin vs FOB destination ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\nIn US domestic practice \"FOB\" terms (different from Incoterms FOB) answer two separate questions:\n1. **FOB point** — where **title and risk** pass → *who owns the goods in transit and who files claims*: **FOB Origin** (buyer owns from the shipping point) or **FOB Destination** (seller owns until delivery).\n2. **Freight payment term** — *who pays the carrier and who ultimately bears the cost*: **Freight Collect** (buyer pays carrier), **Freight Prepaid** (seller pays carrier), plus **Charged Back** (seller prepays, then adds freight to the invoice) and **Collect & Allowed** (buyer pays carrier, then deducts it from the invoice).\n\n### Table 11.4 — Freight control and payment terms ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n| FOB term & freight payment responsibility | Who owns goods in transit? | Who handles freight claims? | Who selects and pays carrier? | Who ultimately bears freight costs? | Best used when ___ has greater influence with carrier |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **FOB Origin, Freight Collect** | Buyer | Buyer | Buyer | Buyer | **Buyer** |\n| **FOB Origin, Freight Prepaid** | Buyer | Buyer | Seller | Seller | **Seller** |\n| **FOB Origin, Freight Prepaid & Charged Back** | Buyer | Buyer | Seller | **Buyer** — seller adds freight costs to goods invoice | **Seller** |\n| **FOB Destination, Freight Prepaid** | Seller | Seller | Seller | Seller | **Seller** |\n| **FOB Destination, Freight Collect** | Seller | Seller | Buyer | Buyer | **Buyer** |\n| **FOB Destination, Freight Collect & Allowed** | Seller | Seller | Buyer | **Seller** — buyer deducts freight cost from goods payment | **Buyer** |\nSource: adapted from Bruce J. Riggs, \"The Traffic Manager in Physical Distribution Management\", 1968.\n\n**How to remember:** the FOB point decides **ownership + claims** (columns 1–2); the payment term decides **who pays the carrier** (column 3); \"charged back\" and \"allowed\" move the **ultimate cost** (column 4) to the other party. **Control** goes with whoever **selects** the carrier.\n\n**Link to Incoterms 📗:** FOB Origin, Freight Collect ≈ FCA/EXW (buyer controls and pays carriage, bears transit risk); FOB Destination, Freight Prepaid ≈ DAP (seller controls, pays and bears risk to destination); FOB Origin, Freight Prepaid ≈ CPT (seller pays freight, buyer bears transit risk).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Modal capabilities ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n### Table 11.5 — Comparison of modal capabilities\n| Mode | Strengths | Limitations | Primary role | Primary product characteristics | Example products |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Truck** | Accessible; fast and versatile; customer service | Limited capacity; high cost | Move smaller shipments in local, regional and national markets | High value; finished goods; low volume | Food, clothing, electronics, furniture |\n| **Rail** | High capacity; low cost | Accessibility; inconsistent service; damage rates | Move large shipments of domestic freight long distances | Low value; raw materials; high volume | Coal/coke, lumber/paper, grain, chemicals |\n| **Air** | Speed; freight protection; flexibility | Accessibility; high cost; low capacity | Move urgent shipments of domestic freight and smaller shipments of international freight | High value; finished goods; low volume; time sensitive | Computers, periodicals, pharmaceuticals, e-commerce deliveries |\n| **Water** | High capacity; low cost; international capabilities | Slow; accessibility | Move large domestic shipments via rivers and canals; large shipments of international freight via oceans | Low value; raw materials; bulk commodities; containerised finished goods | Crude oil, ores/minerals, farm products, clothing, electronics, toys |\n| **Pipeline** | In-transit storage; efficiency; low cost | Slow; limited network | Move large volumes of domestic freight long distances | Low value; liquid commodities; not time sensitive | Crude oil, petroleum, gasoline, natural gas |\nSource: Brian J. Gibson.\n\n### Table 11.6 — Performance ranking of modes ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n(1 = best to 5 = worst for service criteria; cost: 1 = lowest cost to 5 = highest cost)\n\n| Criteria | Truck | Air | Rail | Water | Pipeline |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Accessibility | **1** | 3 | 2 | 4 | 5 |\n| Transit time | 2 | **1** | 3 | 4 | 5 |\n| Reliability | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | **1** |\n| Security | 3 | 2 | 4 | 5 | **1** |\n| Cost | 4 | 5 | 3 | 2 | **1** |\nSource: Edward J. Bardi.\n\nRead-outs: truck wins **accessibility** (door-to-door), air wins **transit time** but is **costliest**, pipeline is most **reliable, secure and cheapest** but least accessible and slowest; water is cheap but slowest and least reliable among the others.\n\n### Mode and carrier selection criteria 📗\nTransit time and reliability (consistency), capability/capacity, accessibility, security (loss and damage), cost (rates and total landed cost), customer service, technology (tracking, EDI), financial stability, sustainability (emissions). Selection steps: problem recognition → search → choice (evaluate against criteria) → post-choice evaluation (**carrier scorecards**).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Transportation performance metrics (KPIs) ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n### Table 11.7 — Common transportation metrics\n| Metric | Formula | Typical target |\n|---|---|---|\n| **On-time delivery** | Total on-time deliveries ÷ total deliveries | **> 95%** |\n| **Transit time average** | Sum of transit times ÷ total deliveries | **Low variation around goal** |\n| **Damage rate** | Total units damaged ÷ total units shipped | **< 1%** |\n| **Shortage rate** | Total units lost or stolen ÷ total units shipped | **< 1%** |\n| **Billing accuracy** | Total accurate freight bills ÷ total freight bills | **> 99%** |\n| **Perfect delivery index** | On-time % × damage-free % × billing accuracy % | **> 95%** |\nSource: Brian J. Gibson.\n\nWhy it matters (pre-read): a delivery that is **on time, damage free and with an accurate bill** eliminates the need for administrative work, tempers the use of premium (expedited) service, and promotes customer satisfaction, inventory reduction and supply chain stability.\n\n### Perfect delivery index — worked example 📗\nOn-time 96%, damage-free 99.2% (damage 0.8%), billing accuracy 98.5% → PDI = 0.96 × 0.992 × 0.985 = **93.8%** < 95% target. Notice how a small shortfall in each metric compounds.\n\n### Other logistics KPIs worth knowing 📗\n| Category | KPIs |\n|---|---|\n| Service | OTIF (on time in full), perfect order rate, fill rate (order, line, unit), order cycle time, customer complaints |\n| Cost | Freight cost per unit / per kg / per km / as % of sales, cost per shipment, total landed cost, empty-mile %, accessorial charges |\n| Asset utilisation | Truck/container utilisation (cube and weight), load factor, dwell time, turnaround time, vessel/berth productivity |\n| Quality | Damage and claims ratio, claim settlement time, shortage rate, temperature compliance (cold chain) |\n| Speed | Transit time and variability, dock-to-stock time, customs clearance time |\n| Sustainability | CO₂ per tonne-km, % intermodal/rail, fuel efficiency |\n| Warehouse | Order accuracy, inventory accuracy, picking productivity, inventory turns |\nGood KPI practice: **SMART**, aligned to strategy, a few vital metrics, a clear data source and owner, targets with benchmarks, and a review cadence (monthly scorecard, quarterly business review).\n\n---\n\n## 4. The carrier performance scorecard ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\nA **weighted scorecard** converts several KPIs into one number so carriers can be compared, rewarded (more volume, awards) or eliminated:\n1. Choose **performance criteria** (on-time delivery, loss/damage, billing accuracy, equipment condition, customer service, loads rejected…).\n2. Assign a **weight factor** to each (total 100).\n3. Define a **performance evaluation scale** — bands of actual performance mapped to points (e.g. 5 = best … 0 = unacceptable).\n4. Category score = **weight × points**; **potential score** = weight × 5; total potential = 500.\n5. **Total score** = Σ category scores; compare carriers and over time.\n\n### Table 11.8 — Transportation performance scorecard (example) ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n| Performance criteria | Weight | Carrier performance | Performance scale | Points | Category score |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| On-time delivery | 35 | 96.7% | >98% = 5; 96.01–98% = 4; 94.01–96% = 3; 92.01–94% = 2; <92% = 0 | 4 | **140** |\n| Loss and damage rate | 30 | 0.6% | <0.5% = 5; 0.5–1% = 4; 1–1.5% = 3; 1.5–2% = 2; >2% = 0 | 4 | **120** |\n| Billing accuracy | 15 | 98.1% | >99% = 5; 97–99% = 3; 95–96% = 1; <95% = 0 | 3 | **45** |\n| Equipment condition | 5 | Acceptable | Safe & clean = 5; poor condition = 0 | 5 | **25** |\n| Customer service | 15 | Outstanding feedback on customer surveys | Superior = 5; good = 4; average = 3; fair = 2; unacceptable = 0 | 5 | **75** |\n| **Total score** | 100 | | | | **405 / 500** |\nHow to read it: the carrier scores **81%** of potential (405/500). It loses most points on **on-time delivery** (−35: 140/175) and **billing accuracy** (−30: 45/75); these are its improvement areas.\n\n---\n\n## Self-test\n**Q1. In FOB Destination, Freight Collect and Allowed, who owns the goods, who pays the carrier and who bears the cost?**\nThe seller owns the goods in transit and files claims; the buyer selects and pays the carrier; but the buyer deducts the freight from its payment, so the seller ultimately bears the freight cost. It is best when the buyer has more influence with carriers.\n\n**Q2. Which term gives the buyer full control and full cost?**\nFOB Origin, Freight Collect: buyer owns goods in transit, handles claims, selects and pays the carrier and bears the cost.\n\n**Q3. Write the formula for the perfect delivery index.**\nPDI = on-time % × damage-free % × billing accuracy %; typical target above 95%.\n\n**Q4. Rank the modes on transit time and cost (Table 11.6).**\nTransit time: air 1, truck 2, rail 3, water 4, pipeline 5. Cost: pipeline 1 (lowest), water 2, rail 3, truck 4, air 5 (highest).\n\n**Q5. How is a weighted carrier scorecard built?**\nChoose criteria, weight them to 100, define banded point scales (e.g. 0–5), multiply weight × points for each criterion and add up; compare the total to the 500 potential and across carriers.\n\n**Q6. What are the strengths and limitations of rail (Table 11.5)?**\nStrengths: high capacity, low cost. Limitations: accessibility, inconsistent service, damage rates. Role: large domestic shipments over long distances; low-value, high-volume raw materials like coal, lumber, grain, chemicals.\n"}, {"slug": "exam-questions-qna", "label": "Exam Q&A Bank", "tag": "Q&A", "group": "practice", "title": "Exam Q&A Bank — Long and Short Answers", "md": "# Exam Q&A Bank — Long and Short Answers\nProbable end-sem theory questions across the eight topics, with model answers you can expand. Turn on **Self-test** (`T`) to hide answers. Numericals are on the problem pages.\n\n---\n\n## Outsourcing & contracts\n\n**Q1. What is outsourcing? Discuss its advantages and risks in international logistics. (10 marks)**\nOutsourcing is contracting an external provider to perform an activity the firm could do in-house; in logistics, transport, warehousing, forwarding, customs and even supply-chain design are outsourced to 2PL–5PL providers. **Advantages**: lower cost (scale, labour arbitrage, variable cost), focus on core competence, access to expertise and technology (WMS, cold chain, global networks), flexibility for peaks, faster market entry, capital release, risk sharing. **Risks**: loss of control and visibility, hidden costs (audits, integration, QC, governance), service failures, IP and data leakage, dependence and switching costs, regulatory and ethical exposure (labour practices), currency and political risk, cultural/communication gaps. **Managing them**: TCO-based business case, weighted supplier selection, robust contract (SLA, LDs, audit, exit), risk matrix and mitigation, governance and KPI scorecards, dual sourcing for critical services.\n\n**Q2. Distinguish outsourcing, offshoring, nearshoring and insourcing.**\nOutsourcing: a third party performs the activity (anywhere). Offshoring: the activity moves to another country (own captive unit or third party). Nearshoring: moving to a nearby country for shorter lead times and lower risk. Insourcing/reshoring: bringing activities back in-house/home.\n\n**Q3. Explain the key clauses of a logistics outsourcing contract.**\nScope of work; price and indexation (fuel, currency); SLA with KPIs, service credits and incentives; liquidated damages; force majeure (definition, notice, mitigation, termination after a period); termination for cause (with cure period) and convenience; warranties and indemnities; limitation of liability; insurance; confidentiality and IP; audit rights; code of conduct/ESG; business continuity; change control; governance and escalation; dispute resolution and governing law; exit and transition-out; step-in rights.\n\n**Q4. What is an SLA? How do you design one for an outsourced warehouse?**\nA service level agreement defines measurable service levels, how they are measured and the consequences. Design: choose 5–8 KPIs (order accuracy, fulfilment time, inventory accuracy, damage rate, complaints, safety), define each formula and data source, set targets from baselines/benchmarks, specify measurement frequency and reporting, attach service credits for misses and bonuses for exceeding, list exclusions, set escalation and review meetings, and link repeated failure to termination rights.\n\n**Q5. Explain hidden costs of outsourcing and why they are ignored.**\nHidden costs are the extra costs caused by outsourcing beyond the contract price: search and audit, travel, transition and integration, quality inspection and rework, contract management and governance, coordination, buffer inventory and exit. They are ignored because they fall in other budgets, arise later, are hard to estimate, and procurement focuses on price. Include them in TCO.\n\n**Q6. Explain force majeure with reference to the COVID-19 lockdown.**\nForce majeure excuses non-performance caused by events beyond a party's control, unforeseeable and unavoidable — e.g. a government-ordered lockdown — if the clause covers it, the party gives prompt notice with evidence and mitigates. It suspends obligations (no damages), and prolonged FM may permit termination. Post-COVID contracts name pandemics and government orders explicitly, require notice timelines, partial performance, allocation, and hardship/termination provisions.\n\n**Q7. Single sourcing versus multi-sourcing: which builds resilience?**\nSingle sourcing gives lower cost, consistent quality and partnership but creates a single point of failure; multi-sourcing costs more and is harder to manage but spreads risk, adds flexible capacity and keeps competition. Resilience comes from multi-sourcing (geographically diverse), supported by safety stock and visibility; in stable times a primary + qualified backup split balances both.\n\n**Q8. Define BATNA and explain its use in a freight contract negotiation.**\nBATNA is the best alternative if no agreement is reached (e.g. spot market or another carrier for the buyer; other shippers for the carrier). It sets each side's walk-away point and bargaining power; parties explore trade-offs (volume vs price, speed vs price, contract length, indexation) within the zone of possible agreement.\n\n---\n\n## Incoterms\n\n**Q9. What are Incoterms? Why are they important in international trade? (10 marks)**\nIncoterms are the ICC's standard trade terms (first 1936, now 2020) that define the tasks, costs and risks of seller and buyer for delivery of goods: who arranges and pays each leg, who clears export and import, who insures, and where risk passes. Importance: clarity and certainty, fewer disputes and delays, lower costs, better risk management, simpler communication, alignment with L/Cs, insurance and customs valuation; globally recognised in all trade documents. Limits: they do not cover title, payment, price, remedies, FM, IP, tariffs or disputes.\n\n**Q10. Classify the Incoterms 2020 and explain each briefly. (10 marks)**\nAny mode: EXW (goods at seller's premises, buyer does everything); FCA (delivered to buyer's carrier, seller clears export); CPT (seller pays carriage to destination, risk at first carrier); CIP (CPT + seller insures ICC A 110%); DAP (seller delivers to named place ready for unloading; buyer clears import); DPU (seller delivers and unloads); DDP (seller delivers duty paid). Sea only: FAS (alongside ship); FOB (on board); CFR (on board, seller pays freight to destination port); CIF (CFR + seller insures ICC C 110%). Groups E, F, C, D in ascending seller obligation.\n\n**Q11. \"Risk passes before cost in C-terms.\" Explain with CIF.**\nUnder CIF the seller pays freight and insurance to the destination port, but risk passes to the buyer when goods are loaded on board at the port of shipment. Hence two critical points: risk at origin, costs at destination — which is why the seller must insure for the buyer's benefit.\n\n**Q12. Why should FCA be preferred to EXW and FOB for containerised exports?**\nEXW leaves export clearance and loading to a foreign buyer who usually cannot do them, and the seller gets no proof of export; FOB keeps risk with the seller until loading on board although containers are handed over at a yard/ICD days earlier and the seller no longer controls them. FCA passes risk on hand-over to the carrier, the seller clears export and loads at its premises, it works for all modes, and the 2020 on-board B/L option satisfies L/C requirements.\n\n**Q13. What changed in Incoterms 2020?**\nDAT renamed DPU; CIP insurance raised to ICC (A) while CIF stays at ICC (C); FCA on-board bill of lading option; use of own transport in FCA, DAP, DPU, DDP; security costs allocated; costs consolidated in A9/B9; article order changed; expanded guidance notes.\n\n**Q14. What factors determine the choice of Incoterm?**\nMode of transport; who has better logistics capability and freight rates; risk appetite and where risk should transfer; insurance needs and cargo value; customs expertise and legal ability to clear export/import; payment method (L/C needs C-terms or FCA on-board B/L); competitiveness of the offer; cargo nature (bulk vs container, oversized, perishables).\n\n---\n\n## Containerization & imbalance\n\n**Q15. Define containerization. Explain its objectives and advantages. (10 marks)**\n(See chapter.) ISO-standard boxes carried across modes without rehandling goods. Objectives: weather protection, a standard box, easier loading/unloading, temporary storage, reduced theft and pilferage, crane handling by hooks, reduced handling. Advantages: lower port handling cost, faster ship turnaround, less damage and pilferage, intermodal transport, economies of scale, lower insurance, standardised equipment and documentation.\n\n**Q16. Explain the different types of containers with examples.**\nGeneral cargo, thermal (reefer for meat/fish, insulated for fruit/veg with dry ice, ventilated for tea/coffee), dry (coal, grain, metals), flat rack (pipes, tanks, machinery), bulk (cement, ores, grain), garment (hanging clothes), tank (milk, chemicals), gas (liquid oxygen), open top (tall cargo), car carrier, special purpose (weapons), open side (long rail bars), double door, tunnel, half-height (dense cargo).\n\n**Q17. What is container imbalance? Discuss its causes, effects and remedies. (10 marks)**\nA spatial mismatch between container supply and demand: surplus empties in import-heavy regions (US, Europe), shortages in export-heavy regions (Asia, India). Causes: trade imbalance, high rates, space/weight issues, single forwarder for many destinations, container shortage, storage costs, demand surges, outdated planning, no carrier cooperation. Effects: late orders and cash-flow stress, higher freight and CIC, delays, stock-outs and penalties, environmental damage, unreliable trade. Remedies: digital tracking and forecasting, CAx monitoring, empty repositioning, virtual pools, SOC/one-way leasing, street turns, sharing, substitution, connectainers, foldable containers, optimisation models.\n\n**Q18. Explain empty container repositioning and its costs.**\nMoving empty boxes from surplus to deficit locations without cargo. Necessary because of trade imbalance, storage costs and time pressure. Costs: ship slot/freight, handling at both ends, drayage, transhipment, storage, repairs, planning — industry cost $15–20 bn/yr; empties are ~20.5% of port handlings.\n\n**Q19. What is the Container Availability Index?**\nContainer xChange's real-time measure of container availability at a port/region (ratio of full containers entering vs leaving). 0.5 = balance; >0.5 surplus; <0.5 shortage; persistent readings guide repositioning and leasing decisions.\n\n---\n\n## Maritime costing & air security cost\n\n**Q20. Explain the components of ocean freight and the factors affecting maritime freight rates. (10 marks)**\nComponents: base freight (per container FCL, per m³/t LCL), surcharges (BAF, CAF, PSS, EBS, GRI, security, green fees), handling (THC origin/destination), additional costs (CFS, documentation), service-based costs (inspection, customs, inland haulage, insurance, last mile). Factors: economic (supply–demand of ships/boxes, peak season, cycles), logistic (operating costs, routes, port infrastructure, handling capacity), regulatory/political (regulations, trade agreements, conflicts), environmental (emissions rules, weather, technology), plus fuel, port fees, currency, insurance, container type and special handling.\n\n**Q21. Describe the methods of calculating freight rates with formulas.**\nPer mile/km (rate × distance); weight-based (rate × weight); dimensional (L×W×H ÷ DIM factor; charge on higher of DIM and actual); flat rate; zone-based (zone pair rate); freight class for LTL (density = weight ÷ volume → NMFC class 50–500). Ocean: chargeable = max(actual, volumetric), 1 m³ = 1,000 kg; total = base + surcharges + extras.\n\n**Q22. Explain the basic principles of air freight price calculation.**\nMeasure to extreme points (rectilinear box; protrusions charged); weight or measure — 6,000 cm³ = 1 kg (1 m³ = 166.67 kg); chargeable weight is the higher, rounded up to the next 0.5 kg; multiply by the agreed tariff for the weight break and route (minimum charge; rate per kg decreases with weight); add fuel and war-risk surcharges; add origin and destination charges (pick-up, handling, documentation, customs, screening, delivery); consider limited carrier liability (22 SDR/kg) and insure.\n\n**Q23. Discuss air cargo security risks and measures, with key regulations.**\nRisks: explosives/incendiaries, undeclared dangerous goods, contraband, theft, hijack/sabotage. Measures: screening, facility security, known shipper/regulated agent regimes, staff training, access control, tamper-proof seals, blast-resistant ULDs, biometrics. Regulations: EU 831/06 (2007), UK known/unknown cargo, TSA 100% screening on passenger aircraft (2012) after the 2010 Yemen bomb, EU screening at origin (2012), ACC3 and independent validation (2014), US–EU mutual recognition (2012).\n\n---\n\n## Insurance & GA\n\n**Q24. Explain General Average with its essentials and an example. (10 marks)**\nDefinition, essentials (common peril, extraordinary, intentional, reasonable, common safety, success, direct consequence), York-Antwerp Rules, calculation (GA loss ÷ contributory values), settlement and security (GA bond, guarantee), example (jettison of wheat in a cyclone; flooding containers to fight fire), role of insurance (contributions covered under ICC A/B/C).\n\n**Q25. Distinguish General Average from Particular Average.**\nGA: intentional, for common safety, shared by all interests rateably, adjusted under YAR. PA: accidental partial loss borne by the owner of the damaged property, recovered from its own insurer.\n\n**Q26. Explain the principles of marine insurance.**\nUtmost good faith, insurable interest, indemnity, subrogation, contribution, proximate cause, warranties, mitigation of loss.\n\n**Q27. Compare ICC (A), (B) and (C).**\nA: all risks subject to exclusions (CIP minimum). B: named perils including C's plus earthquake/lightning, washing overboard, water entry, package lost in loading. C: fire/explosion, stranding/sinking, collision, overturning, discharge at port of distress, GA sacrifice, jettison (CIF minimum). All exclude wilful misconduct, inherent vice, insufficient packing, delay, war/strikes (unless added).\n\n**Q28. What is the role of the General Average adjuster?**\nIndependent expert who determines GA items, collects GA security from cargo before release, values the ship, cargo and freight, computes contributions and the GA rate, prepares the adjustment statement and settles balances.\n\n---\n\n## Air transportation\n\n**Q29. Explain the structure of the air cargo industry. (10 marks)**\nIATA and trade associations (standards for safety, security, training, ULDs; services like CASS); airlines (own/lease aircraft; combination carriers, all-cargo, integrators); lessors (dry, damp, wet/ACMI); IATA cargo agents (freight forwarders issuing HAWBs); airport authorities (AAI in India) and cargo terminal operators; plus ICAO/DGCA/BCAS regulators and customs.\n\n**Q30. Differentiate AWB and HAWB; explain consolidation.**\nThe AWB is the airline's contract of carriage with the shipper (or forwarder); the HAWB is issued by an IATA forwarder to each shipper whose cargo is consolidated; the MAWB covers the consolidated load between forwarder and airline. Consolidation combines many small shipments into one MAWB, getting lower weight-break rates; savings are shared between forwarder and shippers.\n\n**Q31. Explain the types of air freight services and aircraft.**\nServices: air mail, express (time-definite), scheduled, charter (heavy/bulky/unserved destinations), leased (dry, damp, wet/ACMI), forwarder services. Aircraft: passenger (belly), quick-change, combi, dedicated freighter.\n\n**Q32. Describe how air freight works (15 steps).**\nSupplier → Incoterms → forwarder → commercial documents → volumetric ratio → secure packing → route/airline → documentation → AWB after departure → transit → pre-arrival customs → arrival → terminal operator stores under bond → transport collection (tail-lift) → delivery.\n\n**Q33. Discuss the advantages and limitations of air freight with suitable products.**\nAdvantages: speed, reliability, security, track & trace accuracy, control (less storage, perishability), scheduling; suitable for high-value, urgent, perishable goods (pharma, electronics, flowers, fish). Limitations: high cost (4–5× road, 12–16× sea), limited capacity, weather, risk, restrictions on dangerous goods and heavy machinery.\n\n---\n\n## KPIs\n\n**Q34. Explain FOB terms and freight payment responsibilities (Table 11.4).**\nSix combinations of FOB Origin/Destination with Freight Collect, Prepaid, Prepaid & Charged Back, Collect & Allowed; FOB point decides ownership and claims; payment term decides who pays the carrier; charged back/allowed shift the ultimate cost; control follows carrier selection.\n\n**Q35. What are the common transportation KPIs and how is a carrier scorecard built? (10 marks)**\nOn-time delivery (> 95%), transit time average (low variation), damage rate (< 1%), shortage rate (< 1%), billing accuracy (> 99%), perfect delivery index (> 95%). Scorecard: criteria, weights, banded point scales, weight × points, total out of 500, compare carriers, review, reward or eliminate. Example Table 11.8 = 405.\n\n**Q36. Compare the modes of transport on strengths, limitations and performance.**\nTruck: accessible, fast, versatile; limited capacity, high cost. Rail: high capacity, low cost; accessibility, inconsistent service, damage. Air: speed, protection, flexibility; accessibility, high cost, low capacity. Water: capacity, low cost, international; slow, accessibility. Pipeline: in-transit storage, efficient, low cost; slow, limited network. Rankings per Table 11.6.\n\n---\n\n## Short notes (5 marks each)\n**Q37. Write short notes on: (a) CIC (b) SOC (c) street turns (d) BAF/CAF (e) NVOCC (f) ULD (g) CASS (h) ACC3 (i) York-Antwerp Rules (j) DPU.**\n(a) Container Imbalance Charge: surcharge to recover empty repositioning costs, paid by the party creating the imbalance. (b) Shipper-owned container: leased/owned by the shipper for one-way moves; avoids CIC, demurrage/detention and gives control. (c) Street turn: reusing an import box directly for a nearby export load. (d) BAF fuel and CAF currency adjustment surcharges. (e) NVOCC: carrier without ships that issues its own B/L, consolidates and resells space. (f) ULD: aircraft pallet/container shaped to the hold, IATA-coded. (g) CASS: IATA's settlement system — one payment a month. (h) ACC3: EU validation of non-EU carriers' cargo security at origin (2014). (i) YAR: international GA adjustment rules (latest 2016), incorporated in B/Ls. (j) DPU: Incoterm where the seller delivers and unloads at the named place; replaced DAT.\n"}, {"slug": "mcq-bank", "label": "MCQ Bank", "tag": "MCQ", "group": "practice", "title": "MCQ Bank — Quick Checks Across All Eight Topics", "md": "# MCQ Bank — Quick Checks Across All Eight Topics\n96 multiple-choice questions built from the course PPTs, the problem files and the KPI case tables. Turn on **Self-test** (`T`) to hide answers. The **Quiz** tab also generates random questions from the glossary.\n\n---\n\n## Outsourcing & contracts\n\n**Q1. Opening your own development centre in Vietnam is an example of: (a) Domestic outsourcing (b) Captive offshoring (c) Insourcing (d) Nearshore outsourcing**\n**Answer: (b) Captive offshoring** — the work moves abroad but stays in-house.\n\n**Q2. Hiring another Indian IT company to do your software work is: (a) Offshoring (b) Reshoring (c) Domestic outsourcing (d) Vertical integration**\n**Answer: (c) Domestic outsourcing** — third party, same country.\n\n**Q3. In ABC Electronics' vendor problem, the weighted score of Vendor B is: (a) 7.75 (b) 8.10 (c) 8.20 (d) 7.50**\n**Answer: (c) 8.20** — 270 + 200 + 140 + 120 + 90 = 820 ÷ 100.\n\n**Q4. A logistics provider that manages other 3PLs and the whole supply chain for a client is a: (a) 1PL (b) 2PL (c) 3PL (d) 4PL**\n**Answer: (d) 4PL** — the lead logistics provider/integrator.\n\n**Q5. Hidden costs of $720,000 on an $8 million contract are what % of total outsourcing cost? (a) 9.00% (b) 8.26% (c) 7.20% (d) 10.0%**\n**Answer: (b) 8.26%** — 720,000 ÷ 8,720,000.\n\n**Q6. Risk score in a risk matrix is: (a) Probability + Impact (b) Probability × Impact (c) Impact ÷ Probability (d) Probability²**\n**Answer: (b) Probability × Impact**\n\n**Q7. A pre-agreed sum payable for delay, without proving actual loss, is: (a) Force majeure (b) Indemnity (c) Liquidated damages (d) Step-in right**\n**Answer: (c) Liquidated damages**\n\n**Q8. BATNA stands for: (a) Best Agreement To Negotiate Again (b) Best Alternative To a Negotiated Agreement (c) Basic Agreement on Terms and Negotiated Amounts (d) Buyer's Alternative To New Agreements**\n**Answer: (b) Best Alternative To a Negotiated Agreement** — Fisher & Ury.\n\n**Q9. Which sourcing strategy best supports resilience after an earthquake? (a) Single sourcing (b) Multi-sourcing (c) Sole sourcing (d) Spot buying only**\n**Answer: (b) Multi-sourcing**\n\n**Q10. In Que 13, order accuracy (24,100 of 24,500) is: (a) 98.37% (b) 98.50% (c) 99.00% (d) 97.37%**\n**Answer: (a) 98.37%**\n\n**Q11. In Que 13, which KPI meets its target? (a) Order accuracy (b) Inventory accuracy (c) Damage rate (d) Complaint rate**\n**Answer: (d) Complaint rate** — 0.18% ≤ 0.2%.\n\n**Q12. The 6Ms of a fishbone diagram do NOT include: (a) Man (b) Machine (c) Money market (d) Measurement**\n**Answer: (c) Money market** — Man, Machine, Method, Material, Measurement, Mother nature.\n\n**Q13. In Que 12 the first-year outsourcing cost is: (a) $11.0 m (b) $11.4 m (c) $12.0 m (d) $14.3 m**\n**Answer: (c) $12.0 m** — 11 + 0.6 + 0.4.\n\n**Q14. Total landed cost per unit from India (unit $145, tariff 5%) is: (a) $145.00 (b) $150.00 (c) $152.25 (d) $160.00**\n**Answer: (c) $152.25**\n\n**Q15. A clause letting the buyer inspect the supplier's factory and records is: (a) Audit rights (b) Force majeure (c) Change control (d) Liquidated damages**\n**Answer: (a) Audit rights**\n\n## Incoterms\n\n**Q16. Incoterms are published by: (a) WTO (b) ICC (c) IMO (d) UNCTAD**\n**Answer: (b) ICC** — International Chamber of Commerce, Paris.\n\n**Q17. Incoterms were first published in: (a) 1919 (b) 1936 (c) 1953 (d) 1980**\n**Answer: (b) 1936**\n\n**Q18. How many Incoterms 2020 rules are there? (a) 9 (b) 11 (c) 13 (d) 7**\n**Answer: (b) 11** — 7 any-mode + 4 sea.\n\n**Q19. Which is a sea and inland waterway rule only? (a) FCA (b) CPT (c) FAS (d) DAP**\n**Answer: (c) FAS** — with FOB, CFR, CIF.\n\n**Q20. The only Incoterm requiring the seller to unload at destination is: (a) DAP (b) DDP (c) DPU (d) CPT**\n**Answer: (c) DPU**\n\n**Q21. DPU replaced which 2010 term? (a) DDU (b) DAT (c) DES (d) DAF**\n**Answer: (b) DAT** — Delivered at Terminal.\n\n**Q22. Under CIP (2020), the seller must insure for at least: (a) ICC (C), 100% (b) ICC (A), 110% (c) ICC (B), 110% (d) No insurance**\n**Answer: (b) ICC (A), 110%**\n\n**Q23. Under CIF, minimum insurance is: (a) ICC (A) (b) ICC (B) (c) ICC (C) (d) War risk only**\n**Answer: (c) ICC (C)**\n\n**Q24. Under which term does the buyer clear export? (a) FCA (b) EXW (c) FOB (d) CPT**\n**Answer: (b) EXW**\n\n**Q25. Under which term does the seller pay import duty? (a) DAP (b) DPU (c) CIF (d) DDP**\n**Answer: (d) DDP**\n\n**Q26. In CFR, risk passes: (a) At the destination port (b) On board at the port of shipment (c) At the seller's premises (d) After unloading**\n**Answer: (b) On board at the port of shipment**\n\n**Q27. The best Incoterm for containerised freight according to the PPT: (a) FOB (b) FCA (c) EXW (d) CFR**\n**Answer: (b) FCA** — risk passes on delivery to carrier.\n\n**Q28. Best Incoterm for high-value goods per the PPT: (a) CPT (b) CIP (c) EXW (d) FAS**\n**Answer: (b) CIP** — comprehensive insurance by seller.\n\n**Q29. Vaccines delivered to Berlin DC, buyer clears import: (a) DDP (b) DAP (c) CIF (d) FCA**\n**Answer: (b) DAP**\n\n**Q30. Buyer's forwarder collects machinery at Pune warehouse: (a) EXW (b) FCA (c) FOB (d) CPT**\n**Answer: (b) FCA**\n\n**Q31. Grapes to Jebel Ali, seller pays ocean freight, no insurance: (a) CIF (b) CFR (c) FOB (d) CPT**\n**Answer: (b) CFR**\n\n**Q32. Garments to Madrid Inland Terminal, multimodal, buyer insures: (a) CIP (b) CPT (c) CFR (d) DAP**\n**Answer: (b) CPT**\n\n**Q33. Incoterms do NOT determine: (a) Risk transfer (b) Cost allocation (c) Transfer of title (d) Export clearance responsibility**\n**Answer: (c) Transfer of title**\n\n**Q34. The most commonly used Incoterms for air freight are: (a) FOB, CFR, CIF (b) FCA, CPT, CIP (c) FAS, FOB, DDP (d) EXW, DAP, DPU**\n**Answer: (b) FCA, CPT, CIP** — risk at the airport cargo terminal.\n\n**Q35. Which term places maximum obligation on the seller? (a) EXW (b) DDP (c) CIF (d) DPU**\n**Answer: (b) DDP**\n\n**Q36. Correct way to write an Incoterm: (a) FOB (b) FOB China (c) FCA Warehouse 3, Shanghai, Incoterms® 2020 (d) Incoterms FCA**\n**Answer: (c) FCA Warehouse 3, Shanghai, Incoterms® 2020**\n\n## Containerization & imbalance\n\n**Q37. Internal capacity of a 20-ft container (PPT): (a) 33.2 m³ (b) 67.7 m³ (c) 28.2 m³ (d) 76 m³**\n**Answer: (a) 33.2 m³**\n\n**Q38. Max payload of a 40-ft container (PPT): (a) 28,180 kg (b) 28,750 kg (c) 30,480 kg (d) 26,500 kg**\n**Answer: (b) 28,750 kg**\n\n**Q39. Container for tea leaves and coffee beans (air circulation): (a) Insulated (b) Ventilated (c) Tank (d) Flat rack**\n**Answer: (b) Ventilated**\n\n**Q40. Container for odd-size cargo like large pipes or army tanks: (a) Flat rack (b) Garment (c) Bulk (d) Tunnel**\n**Answer: (a) Flat rack**\n\n**Q41. Container that can be opened at both ends for quick loading: (a) Open top (b) Tunnel (c) Half height (d) Reefer**\n**Answer: (b) Tunnel** (double door also opens wide).\n\n**Q42. A CAx value below 0.5 indicates: (a) Surplus of containers (b) Balance (c) Shortage of containers (d) No data**\n**Answer: (c) Shortage of containers** — more full boxes leave than enter.\n\n**Q43. The Container Availability Index is published by: (a) Maersk (b) Container xChange (c) IMO (d) Drewry**\n**Answer: (b) Container xChange**\n\n**Q44. The average container spends what share of its lifespan idle or empty? (a) 20.5% (b) 36% (c) 56% (d) 75%**\n**Answer: (c) 56%**\n\n**Q45. Annual industry cost of empty repositioning (PPT): (a) $1–2 bn (b) $5–8 bn (c) $15–20 bn (d) $50 bn**\n**Answer: (c) $15–20 bn**\n\n**Q46. Moving an empty box from an importer directly to a nearby exporter is: (a) Substitution (b) Street turn (c) Transhipment (d) Depot-direct**\n**Answer: (b) Street turn**\n\n**Q47. Connectainers are: (a) Foldable 40' boxes (b) 20' units that join to form a 40' (c) Reefer containers (d) Air ULDs**\n**Answer: (b) 20' units that join to form a 40'**\n\n**Q48. CIC should be paid by the consignee when it: (a) Ships more containers than it receives (b) Receives more containers than it ships (c) Uses SOC (d) Uses FCL**\n**Answer: (b) Receives more containers than it ships**\n\n**Q49. Free storage at port before charges (PPT) is about: (a) 1 day (b) 5 days (c) 14 days (d) 30 days**\n**Answer: (b) 5 days** — then up to $100/day.\n\n**Q50. The charge for keeping a carrier's container outside the terminal beyond free time is: (a) Demurrage (b) Detention (c) THC (d) CIC**\n**Answer: (b) Detention**\n\n**Q51. SOC stands for: (a) Standard Ocean Container (b) Shipper Owned Container (c) Sea Operating Carrier (d) Secure Ocean Cargo**\n**Answer: (b) Shipper Owned Container**\n\n**Q52. Foldable containers can save up to what % of storage space (Omega)? (a) 25% (b) 50% (c) 75% (d) 90%**\n**Answer: (c) 75%**\n\n## Maritime & logistics costing\n\n**Q53. LCL is best suited for shipments under: (a) 5 m³ (b) 15 m³ (c) 33 m³ (d) 67 m³**\n**Answer: (b) 15 m³**\n\n**Q54. Standard air DIM factor: (a) 5,000 cm³/kg (b) 6,000 cm³/kg (c) 1,000 cm³/kg (d) 3,000 cm³/kg**\n**Answer: (b) 6,000 cm³/kg** — 166.67 kg/m³.\n\n**Q55. A standard curtain-sider FTL holds about: (a) 20 pallets / 60 m³ (b) 33 pallets / 90 m³ (c) 40 pallets / 120 m³ (d) 26 pallets / 67 m³**\n**Answer: (b) 33 pallets / 90 m³**\n\n**Q56. BAF compensates for: (a) Currency changes (b) Fuel price changes (c) Peak season (d) Port congestion**\n**Answer: (b) Fuel price changes**\n\n**Q57. Surcharge for exchange-rate fluctuation: (a) BAF (b) THC (c) CAF (d) GRI**\n**Answer: (c) CAF**\n\n**Q58. An intermediary that issues its own bill of lading but owns no ships: (a) Freight forwarder (b) NVOCC (c) Shipping line (d) Customs broker**\n**Answer: (b) NVOCC**\n\n**Q59. DIM factor for international shipments in inches: (a) 139 (b) 166 (c) 194 (d) 250**\n**Answer: (b) 166** — 139 for domestic.\n\n**Q60. Ocean freight volume conversion factor: (a) 1 m³ = 167 kg (b) 1 m³ = 1,000 kg (c) 1 m³ = 200 kg (d) 1 m³ = 333 kg**\n**Answer: (b) 1 m³ = 1,000 kg** — W/M.\n\n**Q61. Shanghai–LA example, CAF at 5% on base ($2,100) + BAF ($180) is: (a) $105 (b) $114 (c) $120 (d) $96**\n**Answer: (b) $114**\n\n**Q62. Density of a 500 lb shipment, 4 × 4 × 4 ft: (a) 31.25 (b) 7.81 (c) 12.5 (d) 3.9 lb/ft³**\n**Answer: (b) 7.81 lb/ft³**\n\n**Q63. NMFC freight classes range from: (a) 1–100 (b) 50–500 (c) 10–1000 (d) 0–5**\n**Answer: (b) 50–500**\n\n**Q64. Which FCL rate type is a one-time price for a shipment at a moment? (a) Contract (b) Spot (c) All-in (d) Validity period**\n**Answer: (b) Spot**\n\n**Q65. Per UNCTAD 2024, ocean freight costs rose (2020–23) by: (a) 5–10% (b) 15–25% (c) 40–50% (d) 100%**\n**Answer: (b) 15–25%**\n\n**Q66. The 2M alliance (2015) was between: (a) CMA CGM & COSCO (b) Maersk & MSC (c) Hapag-Lloyd & ONE (d) Evergreen & HMM**\n**Answer: (b) Maersk & MSC**\n\n**Q67. The 2024 Red Sea crisis diverted ships via: (a) Panama Canal (b) Strait of Malacca (c) Cape of Good Hope (d) Northern Sea Route**\n**Answer: (c) Cape of Good Hope**\n\n## Air pricing & security\n\n**Q68. 1 m³ of air cargo equals what chargeable weight? (a) 100 kg (b) 166.67 kg (c) 200 kg (d) 1,000 kg**\n**Answer: (b) 166.67 kg**\n\n**Q69. Chargeable weight of 150 kg cargo measuring 120 × 80 × 50 cm: (a) 80 kg (b) 115 kg (c) 150 kg (d) 230 kg**\n**Answer: (c) 150 kg** — volume weight 80 kg is lower.\n\n**Q70. Air chargeable weight is rounded up to the next: (a) 1 kg (b) 0.5 kg (c) 5 kg (d) 0.1 kg**\n**Answer: (b) 0.5 kg**\n\n**Q71. Montreal Convention cargo liability limit (per the text): (a) 17 SDR/kg (b) 22 SDR/kg (c) 2 SDR/kg (d) 666.67 SDR/package**\n**Answer: (b) 22 SDR/kg** — ≈ US$31.57/kg.\n\n**Q72. Cylindrical cargo is charged on: (a) Its true volume (b) The box it fits in exactly (c) Its weight only (d) Half its volume**\n**Answer: (b) The box it fits in exactly**\n\n**Q73. US TSA requires screening of all cargo on passenger aircraft from: (a) 2001 (b) 2007 (c) 2012 (d) 2020**\n**Answer: (c) 2012**\n\n**Q74. EU regulation requiring client information and audits for air cargo consignors (2007): (a) 300/2008 (b) 831/06 (c) 185/2010 (d) 2015/1998**\n**Answer: (b) 831/06**\n\n**Q75. Screened and protected cargo is termed: (a) Known (b) Unknown (c) Bonded (d) Cleared**\n**Answer: (a) Known**\n\n**Q76. ACC3 relates to: (a) Ocean container standards (b) EU validation of third-country air carriers' cargo security (c) US customs pre-clearance (d) IATA billing**\n**Answer: (b) EU validation of third-country air carriers' cargo security** — since 2014.\n\n## Insurance & General Average\n\n**Q77. GA is shared in proportion to: (a) Freight paid (b) Contributory (saved) values (c) Weight of cargo (d) Number of packages**\n**Answer: (b) Contributory (saved) values**\n\n**Q78. International rules for adjusting GA: (a) Hague-Visby Rules (b) York-Antwerp Rules (c) Hamburg Rules (d) Rotterdam Rules**\n**Answer: (b) York-Antwerp Rules** — latest 2016.\n\n**Q79. An accidental partial loss borne only by its owner is: (a) General Average (b) Particular Average (c) Salvage (d) Constructive total loss**\n**Answer: (b) Particular Average**\n\n**Q80. In Que 1 (MV Pacific Star) the GA rate is: (a) 4% (b) 5% (c) 7% (d) 3.75%**\n**Answer: (b) 5%** — 700,000 ÷ 14,000,000.\n\n**Q81. Net settlement to the electronics owner in Que 1: (a) $300,000 (b) $125,000 (c) $175,000 (d) $425,000**\n**Answer: (c) $175,000** — 300,000 − 125,000.\n\n**Q82. In Que 3 (pirates) the steel owner's contribution is: (a) $37,500 (b) $75,000 (c) $112,500 (d) $60,000**\n**Answer: (a) $37,500** — 1,000,000 × 3.75%.\n\n**Q83. In Que 4 the crude oil owner's net position is: (a) Receives $350,000 (b) Pays $46,551.72 (c) Receives $46,551.72 (d) Pays $396,551.72**\n**Answer: (b) Pays $46,551.72** — 350,000 − 396,551.72.\n\n**Q84. In Que 6 (mangoes) the GA rate is: (a) 1.0909% (b) 1.3333% (c) 3.6% (d) 2.57%**\n**Answer: (b) 1.3333%** — 18 lakh ÷ 13.5 crore.\n\n**Q85. Insurance claim payable in Que 9 (TVs): (a) ₹40,32,000 (b) ₹38,30,400 (c) ₹28,22,400 (d) ₹10,08,000**\n**Answer: (c) ₹28,22,400**\n\n**Q86. Which ICC covers theft and pilferage? (a) ICC (A) (b) ICC (B) (c) ICC (C) (d) None**\n**Answer: (a) ICC (A)**\n\n**Q87. Security provided by a cargo owner before release in GA: (a) Letter of credit (b) GA bond and guarantee/deposit (c) Bill of exchange (d) Mate's receipt**\n**Answer: (b) GA bond and guarantee/deposit**\n\n**Q88. Principle by which an insurer, after paying, takes over the insured's rights against third parties: (a) Contribution (b) Subrogation (c) Indemnity (d) Proximate cause**\n**Answer: (b) Subrogation**\n\n## Air transportation\n\n**Q89. ULDs are described as: (a) Air waybills (b) Containers of the skies (c) Cargo terminals (d) Air corridors**\n**Answer: (b) Containers of the skies**\n\n**Q90. The document issued by a forwarder to individual shippers in a consolidation: (a) MAWB (b) HAWB (c) B/L (d) Manifest**\n**Answer: (b) HAWB**\n\n**Q91. CASS lets airlines and agents: (a) Screen cargo (b) Make one payment a month (c) Track ULDs (d) Book charters**\n**Answer: (b) Make one payment a month**\n\n**Q92. Air freight costs about how many times sea freight? (a) 2–3 (b) 4–5 (c) 12–16 (d) 30–40**\n**Answer: (c) 12–16** — and 4–5 times road.\n\n**Q93. A lease providing Aircraft, Crew, Maintenance and Insurance is: (a) Dry lease (b) ACMI/wet lease (c) Damp lease (d) Finance lease**\n**Answer: (b) ACMI/wet lease**\n\n**Q94. An aircraft quickly converted from all-cargo to all-passenger using palletised seats: (a) Combi (b) Quick-change (c) Freighter (d) Belly**\n**Answer: (b) Quick-change**\n\n## KPIs book case\n\n**Q95. Under FOB Destination, Freight Collect and Allowed, who ultimately bears freight cost? (a) Buyer (b) Seller (c) Carrier (d) Shared 50/50**\n**Answer: (b) Seller** — buyer pays carrier, deducts it from payment.\n\n**Q96. Bob's Custom BBQs: which carrier scores lowest? (a) Allied 460 (b) Certaint 400 (c) Bestway 360 (d) All equal**\n**Answer: (c) Bestway 360** — recommended for elimination.\n"}, {"slug": "glossary", "label": "Glossary A–Z", "tag": "A–Z", "group": "practice", "title": "Glossary — International Logistics Terms A–Z", "md": "# Glossary — International Logistics Terms A–Z\nExam-ready one- or two-line definitions. 📘 = term from your course PPTs, PDFs or problem files; 📗 = standard textbook term added for completeness. Use the flashcards and quiz views to self-test.\n\n## 0–9\n- **1PL to 5PL** 📗: Levels of logistics outsourcing: shipper's own logistics (1PL), asset-based carrier (2PL), contract logistics provider (3PL), integrator managing 3PLs (4PL), network aggregator (5PL).\n- **2M alliance** 📘: Vessel-sharing alliance between Maersk and MSC formed in 2015 that shaped freight rates through shared capacity.\n- **4 Ts of risk** 📗: Responses to a risk: treat (mitigate), transfer, tolerate, terminate (avoid).\n- **7 steps of shipping cost** 📘: Export transport, export clearance, origin handling, freight, import clearance, destination handling, import transport.\n\n## A\n- **ACC3** 📘: EU designation for air carriers flying cargo into the EU from third-country airports; since 2014 their origin screening must be validated and approved.\n- **ACMI lease** 📘: \"Wet\" lease in which the lessor provides Aircraft, Crew, Maintenance and Insurance.\n- **Actual total loss** 📗: Insured goods destroyed or irretrievably lost.\n- **Air waybill (AWB)** 📘: Contract of carriage by air issued by the airline; details the goods and limits the airline's liability; not a document of title.\n- **Airports Authority of India (AAI)** 📘: Manages air cargo logistics in India; its Department of Cargo develops cargo terminals.\n- **All-in rate** 📘: Freight rate that includes all transport costs, possibly door-to-door, in one figure.\n- **Audit rights** 📗: Contract clause letting the buyer inspect the supplier's premises, records and compliance.\n- **Average adjuster** 📗: Independent expert who determines GA items, collects security and prepares the GA adjustment.\n\n## B\n- **BAF (Bunker Adjustment Factor)** 📘: Ocean surcharge for fluctuating fuel costs, updated monthly or quarterly.\n- **BATNA** 📘: Best Alternative To a Negotiated Agreement — a party's fallback if negotiation fails.\n- **Bill of lading (B/L)** 📗: Ocean carrier's receipt, evidence of contract of carriage and document of title.\n- **Billing accuracy** 📘: Accurate freight bills ÷ total freight bills; typical target > 99%.\n- **Bulk container** 📘: Container with top hatches for free-flowing dry cargo such as cement, ores and grain.\n\n## C\n- **CAF (Currency Adjustment Factor)** 📘: Surcharge offsetting exchange-rate fluctuations, often a % of base freight plus BAF.\n- **CASS** 📘: IATA's Cargo Accounts Settlement System letting airlines and agents make or receive one payment a month.\n- **CAx (Container Availability Index)** 📘: Container xChange index of container availability; 0.5 = balance, above = surplus, below = shortage.\n- **CFR (Cost and Freight)** 📘: Sea Incoterm; seller pays freight to destination port; risk passes on board at port of shipment.\n- **Chargeable weight** 📘: The greater of actual and volumetric weight, used to price freight.\n- **Charter airfreight** 📘: Cargo shipped on a chartered aircraft because it is too heavy, too bulky or the destination isn't served.\n- **CIC (Container Imbalance Charge)** 📘: Surcharge compensating carriers for repositioning empty containers on imbalanced lanes.\n- **CIF (Cost, Insurance and Freight)** 📘: Sea Incoterm; seller pays freight and minimum insurance (ICC C, 110%) to destination port; risk passes on board.\n- **CIP (Carriage and Insurance Paid To)** 📘: Any-mode Incoterm; seller pays carriage and all-risks insurance (ICC A, 110%); risk passes at first carrier.\n- **Combi aircraft** 📘: Aircraft carrying cargo and passengers on the main deck at the same time.\n- **Connectainer** 📘: 20-ft container that can be joined with another to form a 40-ft unit.\n- **Constructive total loss** 📗: Loss where saving or repairing the goods would cost more than their value; notice of abandonment is given.\n- **Container** 📘: Sealed, rigid, reusable box for carrying goods without reloading.\n- **Container sharing** 📘: Carriers or shippers pooling containers to reduce empty moves.\n- **Container substitution** 📘: Meeting demand with another container type, e.g. one 40' for two 20' requests.\n- **Containerization** 📘: Transporting cargo in ISO-standardised containers that move across modes without handling the goods.\n- **Contract rate** 📘: Freight rate agreed for a set volume over a period between shipper and line.\n- **Contributory value** 📘: Value of each saved interest (ship, cargo, freight) on which GA contributions are calculated.\n- **CPT (Carriage Paid To)** 📘: Any-mode Incoterm; seller pays carriage to named destination; risk passes at first carrier; no insurance obligation.\n\n## D\n- **DAP (Delivered at Place)** 📘: Seller delivers to the named place ready for unloading; buyer clears import and pays duty.\n- **DDP (Delivered Duty Paid)** 📘: Seller delivers to the named place and clears import, paying duties — maximum seller obligation.\n- **Deferred air service** 📘: Slower, cheaper air service where cargo waits for consolidation.\n- **Demurrage** 📗: Charge for a container staying inside the port/terminal beyond free time.\n- **Detention** 📗: Charge for keeping a carrier's container outside the terminal beyond free time.\n- **DIM factor** 📘: Divisor converting volume to dimensional weight: 6,000 cm³/kg air; 139 (domestic) or 166 (international) in³/lb.\n- **Dimensional weight** 📘: Weight equivalent of a shipment's volume; L × W × H ÷ DIM factor.\n- **DPU (Delivered at Place Unloaded)** 📘: Seller delivers and unloads at the named place — the only rule with seller unloading; replaced DAT.\n- **Dry container** 📘: The most common general-purpose container for dry cargo like coal, grain and metals.\n\n## E\n- **EBS (Emergency Bunker Surcharge)** 📘: Fuel surcharge used in crises such as the Red Sea disruption.\n- **Empty container repositioning (ECR)** 📘: Moving empty containers from surplus to deficit locations without cargo.\n- **EXW (Ex Works)** 📘: Seller makes goods available at its premises; buyer loads, clears export and bears all costs and risks — minimum seller obligation.\n- **Express airfreight** 📘: Air cargo with a guaranteed predetermined delivery date (DHL, FedEx, UPS).\n\n## F\n- **FAS (Free Alongside Ship)** 📘: Sea Incoterm; seller places goods alongside the buyer's vessel at the port of shipment.\n- **FCA (Free Carrier)** 📘: Any-mode Incoterm; seller delivers to the buyer's carrier at a named place and clears export; preferred for containers.\n- **FCL (Full Container Load)** 📘: A whole container booked by one shipper; better above about 15 m³.\n- **FEU** 📗: Forty-foot equivalent unit = 2 TEU.\n- **Fishbone diagram** 📘: Ishikawa cause-and-effect diagram grouping root causes (6Ms) of a problem.\n- **Flat rack** 📘: Container with a strong base and collapsible ends for odd-size cargo.\n- **Flat rate** 📘: One fixed freight charge regardless of distance or weight.\n- **FOB (Free On Board)** 📘: Sea Incoterm; seller loads goods on the buyer's nominated vessel; risk passes on board.\n- **FOB Destination** 📘: US freight term where the seller owns goods in transit and files claims until delivery.\n- **FOB Origin** 📘: US freight term where the buyer owns goods in transit from the shipping point and files claims.\n- **Foldable container** 📘: Collapsible container (e.g. four folded inside a fifth) that saves up to 75% of space when empty.\n- **Force majeure** 📘: Clause excusing performance when an event beyond a party's control (war, disaster, lockdown) prevents it.\n- **Freight Collect and Allowed** 📘: Buyer pays the carrier then deducts the freight from its payment, so the seller bears the cost.\n- **Freight forwarder** 📘: Company that organises international shipments across modes, documents and customs on the shipper's behalf.\n- **Freight Prepaid and Charged Back** 📘: Seller pays the carrier then adds freight to the invoice, so the buyer bears the cost.\n- **Freighter** 📘: Aircraft dedicated to carrying cargo.\n- **FTL (Full Truckload)** 📘: Whole truck bought by one shipper — about 33 pallets or 90 m³.\n\n## G\n- **General Average (GA)** 📘: Intentional, reasonable extraordinary sacrifice or expenditure for the common safety of ship and cargo, shared rateably by all saved interests.\n- **GA bond** 📗: Undertaking by the cargo receiver to pay its share of GA, signed before cargo release.\n- **GA rate** 📘: Total GA loss ÷ total contributory value × 100.\n- **Garment container** 📘: Container with rails and hangers so clothes travel hanging, without creasing.\n- **GRI (General Rate Increase)** 📘: Temporary carrier-announced price rise on volatile trade lanes.\n\n## H\n- **H&M insurance** 📘: Hull and Machinery insurance covering the ship and its share of GA.\n- **HAWB (House Air Waybill)** 📘: Air waybill issued by a freight forwarder/IATA agent to each shipper in a consolidation.\n- **Hidden costs** 📘: Costs of outsourcing beyond the contract price — audits, travel, integration, inspection, contract management.\n- **Hubs and spokes** 📘: Air network that feeds cargo into strategically located hubs to offset high unit costs.\n\n## I\n- **IATA** 📘: International Air Transport Association — non-political commercial trade body of airlines (290 members) setting standards and services.\n- **IATA cargo agent** 📘: Freight forwarder licensed by IATA to handle air cargo and issue HAWBs.\n- **ICC (Institute Cargo Clauses)** 📗: Standard marine cargo covers: A (all risks), B and C (named perils).\n- **IE Code (IEC)** 📘: 10-digit Importer-Exporter Code (same as PAN) issued by DGFT, mandatory to import or export from India.\n- **Incoterms®** 📘: ICC's 11 standard trade terms (2020) defining seller and buyer tasks, costs and risk transfer.\n- **Insulated container** 📘: Thermal container using dry ice for fruit and vegetables.\n- **Insurable interest** 📗: Financial stake in the goods required to claim on insurance.\n- **Intermodal transportation** 📘: Moving goods in one load unit (container, trailer, swap body) by several modes without handling the goods.\n- **ISO** 📘: International Organization for Standardization, which standardised container sizes, ratings and terms.\n- **ISPS charge** 📘: Ship and port facility security surcharge.\n\n## K\n- **Known cargo / known shipper** 📘: Cargo screened and kept secure (or from a validated shipper) that may be loaded without re-screening.\n- **KPI** 📘: Key performance indicator — a measurable value showing how well a process meets its objectives.\n\n## L\n- **Landed cost** 📘: Purchase price plus all costs to get the goods to the buyer (freight, duty, insurance, handling, inventory cost).\n- **LCL (Less than Container Load)** 📘: Shared container for small shipments (under ~15 m³), priced per m³.\n- **Leased cargo aircraft** 📘: Aircraft obtained on dry, damp, wet or ACMI lease.\n- **Liquidated damages** 📘: Pre-agreed sum payable for a specified breach, such as delay, without proving actual loss.\n- **LTL (Less than Truckload)** 📘: Shared truck service for 1–2 pallets with hub handling.\n\n## M\n- **MAWB (Master Air Waybill)** 📘: Contract between forwarder and airline covering a consolidated shipment of many HAWBs.\n- **Montreal Convention** 📘: 1999 air carriage treaty limiting cargo liability (22 SDR/kg per the course text).\n- **Multi-sourcing** 📘: Buying an item from several suppliers to reduce disruption risk.\n\n## N\n- **Nearshoring** 📘: Moving production or services to a nearby country (US → Mexico, EU → Poland).\n- **NMFC freight class** 📘: US LTL classification (50–500) based on density, stowability, handling and liability.\n- **NVOCC** 📘: Non-Vessel Operating Common Carrier — issues its own B/L and resells space bought from shipping lines.\n- **NVO** 📘: Non-vessel operator — intermediary between shippers and lines that doesn't own ships.\n\n## O\n- **Offshoring** 📘: Moving an activity to another country, whether in-house or outsourced.\n- **On-time delivery** 📘: On-time deliveries ÷ total deliveries; typical target > 95%.\n- **Open top container** 📘: Container without a fixed roof for cargo taller than the box.\n- **Outsourcing** 📘: Contracting a third party to perform an activity previously or potentially done in-house.\n\n## P\n- **P&I club** 📗: Mutual insurer of shipowners' third-party liabilities.\n- **Particular Average (PA)** 📘: Accidental partial loss borne only by the owner of the damaged property.\n- **Payback period** 📘: One-time investment ÷ annual savings.\n- **Perfect delivery index** 📘: On-time % × damage-free % × billing accuracy %; target > 95%.\n- **PSS (Peak Season Surcharge)** 📘: Surcharge in high-demand seasons such as August–October.\n\n## Q\n- **Quick-change aircraft** 📘: Aircraft rapidly converted between all-cargo and all-passenger use with palletised seats.\n\n## R\n- **Reefer** 📘: Refrigerated container with its own cooling unit for meat, fish and other perishables.\n- **Risk matrix** 📘: Grid scoring risks by probability × impact to prioritise mitigation.\n\n## S\n- **Salvage** 📗: Reward to a voluntary salvor for saving maritime property; allowed in GA when for common safety.\n- **Scheduled airfreight** 📘: Routine air cargo services — reliable, efficient, relatively low cost.\n- **Shipper** 📘: Owner of goods who arranges their transport and is responsible for packaging and labelling.\n- **Single sourcing** 📘: Buying an item from one supplier — lowest cost but highest disruption risk.\n- **SLA (Service Level Agreement)** 📘: Contract section defining KPIs, targets, measurement, penalties and incentives.\n- **SOC (Shipper Owned Container)** 📘: Container owned or leased by the shipper, used to avoid CIC and carrier detention.\n- **Spot rate** 📘: One-time freight price for a specific shipment at current market conditions.\n- **Street turn** 📘: Reusing an import container directly for a nearby export load without returning it to port.\n- **Subrogation** 📗: Insurer's right, after paying a claim, to recover from the party responsible.\n\n## T\n- **Tank container** 📘: Stainless-steel container for liquids such as milk or chemicals.\n- **TEU** 📗: Twenty-foot equivalent unit, the standard unit of container capacity.\n- **THC (Terminal Handling Charge)** 📘: Charge for container handling at origin or destination terminals.\n- **Transit time average** 📘: Sum of transit times ÷ total deliveries; aim for low variation.\n- **Tunnel container** 📘: Container that opens at both ends for fast loading.\n\n## U\n- **ULD (Unit Load Device)** 📘: Pallet or container shaped to an aircraft hold — \"the shipping containers of the skies\".\n\n## V\n- **Ventilated container** 📘: Thermal container with side openings for air circulation, for tea and coffee.\n- **Volumetric weight** 📘: Volume converted to weight (air: m³ × 166.67 kg; ocean: 1 m³ = 1,000 kg).\n\n## W\n- **Weighted scoring model** 📘: Supplier or carrier selection by Σ weight × rating across criteria.\n- **W/M (weight or measure)** 📗: Ocean pricing on the greater of tonnes or cubic metres (revenue ton).\n\n## Y\n- **York-Antwerp Rules** 📗: International rules for adjusting General Average (latest 2016), incorporated in bills of lading.\n"}];
